4301:
** προλαμβάνω**,
_future_ **-λήψομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.16:}}_aorist_ προὔλαβον:—_passive_, see below{{Refs|1.5}}:— **take** or **receive before,** τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.9 codices; τὰ χωρία καὶ λιμένας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.9}}; ἀργύριον π. **receive** money **in advance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 50.14, 35; τὰ ἐφόδια 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.172; τρία τάλαντα παρά τινος [prev. author] 2.166; ἅπαντα ἡμῶν τὰ χωρία 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.16, etc.; also π. χάριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 914 (Lyric poetry)}}; μισθὸν τῆς ἀγγελίας **for** the message, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 37; γάλα μετὰ μέλιτος “IG” 42(1).126.15 (Epid., 2nd c.AD)}}; π. τὴν ἡλικίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.162}}; π. τὴν αὔξησιν **begin** their growth **before,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.1.4}}:—_passive_, **to be contained in advance,** ἐν τῷ ὄντι ἄρα ζωὴ προείληπται καὶ ὁ νοῦς {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 103.}}
**2** **take** or **seize beforehand,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.142; τὴν ἀρχήν 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 40.24; ὅσα τῆς πόλεως π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.26}}; τοῦτο π., ὅπως σώσομεν **provide** that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.2: with}}_participle_, προλαβὼν κατεγνωκότας ὑμᾶς **having first procured** your vote of condemnation, {{Refs|[prev. author] 24.77}}:—_passive_, σῶμα προειλημμένον ὑπὸ νόσου {{Refs|“Corpus Hermeticum” 12.3.}}
**2.b** **get** or **take as a start,** προειλήφασι πολὺν χρόνον **have had a** long **start,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 60.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; π. τῆς νυκτὸς ὁπόσον ἂν δυναίμην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 1.}}
**3** **take in preference,** τι πρό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1141.}}
**4** **take away** or **off before,** ἐκ γὰρ οἴκων προὔλαβον μόγις πόδα, μὴ θανεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1253.}}
**5** **assume in advance,** τὴν ὁλότητα προλαβὼν ἐγέννησεν ἀπ᾽ αὐτῆς τὴν παντότητα {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 253}}; προειλήφθω.. δισχιλίων σταδίων τὸ βάθος [εἶναι] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 34.6.7.}}
**II** **to be beforehand with, anticipate,**
**II.1** with _accusative_ _person_, **get the start of,** τὰς κύνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.19, see below 3; π. τῷ λόγῳ τινάς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 29}}; βραχὺν χρόνον π. ἡμᾶς, i.e. in dying, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.117e; π. τῇ ῥιζώσει τοὺς χειμῶνας 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.1.3, compare “CP” 3.24.3: with _genitive_ _person_, προλαβών μου ὥστε πρότερος λέγειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.6}}; ἵνα μὴ -λημφθῶμεν (i.e. by death) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 2.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _of things_, π. γόους, μαντεύματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 339 (Lyric poetry), “Ion” 407; τὸν καιρόν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.14.12, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 34, etc.; τὸν ὄρθρον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 15}}; of mental anticipation, π. ὡς οὕτως ἔχον πρὶν γινόμενον οὕτως ἰδεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 765a28; τὰ συμβησόμενα ταῖς ἐννοίαις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.112.7, compare 3.1.7; τὰ πολλὰ εἰκασίᾳ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 8; π. ὅτι.. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.102e, etc.}}
**II.3** with _genitive_ spatii, π. τῆς ὁδοῦ **get a start** on the way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.105; πολὺ τῆς ὁδοῦ π. 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 7.29.2 (but just above, π. ὡς πλείστην ὁδὸν τοὺς διώξοντας); π. ῥᾳδίως τῆς φυγῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.33}}; π. τῆς διώξεως **get a start** of the pursuers, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.94: _metaphorically_, μύθου προλαβοῦσα}} **speaking first,** Philicus in {{Refs|“Stud.Ital.” 9.44, compare 46.}}
**II.3.b** generally, π. τῶν κηρύκων **anticipate** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408b24}}; τοῦ χρόνου π. **precede** in point of time, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1050b5.}}
**II.4** with _dative_ modi, π. τῷ δρόμῳ **get a start** in running, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 7.7; τῇ διανοίᾳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 660; τῇ φυγῇ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 20, “Cic.” 47.}}
**II.5** with _infinitive_, προέλαβε μυρίσαι {{NT|NT.Mark.14.8.}}
**II.6** **detect,** ἐν παραπτώματι {{NT|NT.Gal.6.1 (_passive_).}}
**II.7** **anticipate,** τι τῶν μελλόντων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.620; τὸ μέλλον τοῖς λογισμοῖς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 54.2.}}
**II.8** _absolutely_, προὔλαβε πολλῷ **was** far **ahead,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.80, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.31, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.15.8}}; **gain an advantage,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.15.}}
**II.8.b** **anticipate the event, prejudge,** ἐπειδὰν ἅπαντ᾽ ἀκούσητε κρίνατε, μὴ πρότερον προλαμβάνετε {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.14; οἱ νόμοι προλαβόντες ἐπιμέλονται ὅπως..}}**by anticipation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.3}}; **come before the time,** opposed to ὑστερίζειν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.353}}; of corn-buyers, **buy earlier**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 976.49 (Samos, 2nd c.BC)}}:— _middle_, προλαμβάνου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 701:}} —_passive_, τὸ προειλημμένον that which is **prejudged,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ τῶν στάσεων” 1.}}
**II.8.c** **precede, go before,** ὁ προλαβὼν βίος his **previous** life,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|argument 2 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.3; τὰ προλαβόντα}}**what precedes,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Vandalico” 2.16}}; ἡ προλαβοῦσα τράπεζα the **preceding** meal, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 57.24}}; also τῶν προλαβόντων τἢν μνήμην the memory of **the past,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus Gaz. “Pan.” p.495 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**III** **repeat from the origin,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.16; μικρὸν π. [prev. author] 16.24.}}
**IV** Philos., **form a preconception** (compare πρόληψις), **prejudge,** οἷα προειλήφαμεν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.13, compare “Sign.” 22}}:—_middle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “D.” 1.13}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Oec.” p.57 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}

4302:
** προλέγω**,
**pick out, choose, prefer,** Ἀθηναίων προλελεγμένοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.689; ἐξοχώτατοι προλέγονται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.18; ἀριστῆες πασᾶν ἐκ πολίων προλελεγμένοι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.18.}}
**II** **foretell,** _Aeolic dialect_ _aorist_ _participle_ προλέξαις {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 22.7}}; **predict,** of an oracle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.53, 8.136; μέμνησθ᾽ ἁγὼ προλέγω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1071 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 973}}; π. πρόρρησιν, of a physician, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 15; τὰ μέλλοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.298.}}
**II.b** **say beforehand,** ταῦτα, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 337a, compare “Euthd.” 275e, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 7}}; ὡς προλέλεκται as **was said above,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 89}}; ὁ προλεχθείς the **aforesaid, PMasp.**{{Refs 6th c.AD+|32.63 (6th c.AD), +others}}
**II.c** προλεγόμεναι (i.e. θέσεις) **first principles,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Familiares” 9.18.3.}}
**II.d** μακρὰ προλεγομένγ a long syllable **placed, uttered first,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 39.}}
**II.2** **state publicly, proclaim,** προὔλεγον τὸ ψήφισμα καθελοῦσι μὴ ἂν γίγνεσθαι πόλεμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139; προλεγέτω ἁ ἀρχά “Foed.Delph.Pell.” 1 A 5, compare “OGI” 437.76 (Pergam., 1st c.BC)}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 336 (Lyric poetry), etc.: with _accusative_, ὁ νόμος πέφυκε προλέγειν ἃ μὴ δεῖ πράττειν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 4; πολέμους ἀλλήλοις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.3.5.}}
**II.3** π. τινὶ ἀπιέναι **order** him to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.3, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.71}}; **caution, warn,** π. τινὶ μὴ φεύγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 897.9 (_anapaest meter_); π. τινί, εἰ θεραπεύσοιτο, ὅτι διαφθαρήσοιτο 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.4; ὡς οὐ.. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.57.2.}}
**II.4** **denounce** punishment, π. δεσμόν τινι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.60.}}

4304:
** προμελετάω**,
**practise beforehand,** ἃ δεῖλέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 117; τὴν μέθοδον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 218d}}: with _infinitive_, π. ἐλαύνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.20: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1177, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.47.3.}}
**2** **acquire a habit of,** with _infinitive_, {{Refs|Diocl.fragment 27:}} —_passive_, τὸ μὴ -ηθέν that **to which one is** not **accustomed,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.26.}}
**II** in Medicine texts, **to be threatening,** of disease {{Refs|compare (μελετάω 11.6)}}, in _middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “fragment” 61(2).}}

4306:
** προνοέω**,
**perceive before, foresee,** δόλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.526, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.63; προνοῆσαι βραδεῖς τὰ.. ἀποβησόμενα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38; τὸ μέλλον ἔσεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 291a24}}; προνοῶν ὅτι ἀνάγκη ἔσοιτο **foreseeing** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.13}}; of divine **foreknowledge,** θεῖος νοῦς νοεῖ μὲν ὡς νοῦς, προνοεῖ δὲ ὡς θεός {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 134, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.8.2.}}
**b** **preconceive,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 26.}}
**II** **think of** or **plan beforehand, provide,** οὐ.. τι πάρα προνοῆσαι ἄμεινον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.364}}; opposed to μετανοέω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 280}}; π. τὸ παραγγελλόμενον **attend to** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.6}}: _absolutely_, **to be on one's guard, take precautions,** ὥρα προνοεῖν πρὶν πελάσαι στρατόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 289; περαιτέρω π. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.43; π. καὶ προβουλεύεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.10.3: followed by _relative_}} _adverb_, π. ὅπως.. **provide, take care** that.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 4.1}}; π. μὴ.., or ὡς μὴ.., **cavere ne..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Oec.” 9.11, “Cyr.” 1.6.24}}; π. ὅτι.. **pay** due **regard** to the fact that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_, **provide for, take thought for,** τῶν παίδων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.1, compare 8.7.15, etc.}}; θεὸς π. τῶν ὅλων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.14.11, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 120; μάλιστα δὲ προνόησον Ἀρσινόης “PHal.” 1.179 (3rd c.BC)}}; opposed to ὀλιγωρεῖν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.3.8}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 122.}}
**II.3** _passive_, **to be provided,** τὰ-ούμενα, of a poet's **equipment,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.3}}; of a patient, **to be treated,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.632}}; **receive consideration,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PGiss. 7.23 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** ={προνοητεύω}, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3408 (Magn.Sip.).}}
**B** _Attic dialect_ writers (also Inscrr. and Papyrus, see below) prefer _middle_ **προνοοῦμαι**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.9, etc. (so also 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.33, “Mem.” 4.3.12)}}: _future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 43, “IG” 22.1035.18, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.90, etc.}}: _aorist_ προὐνοησάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 399, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 421, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.43}}; in Prose mostly προὐνοήθην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 395c, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.29, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.46, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.64, “PSI” 3.166.34 (2nd c.BC)}} (used in _passive_ sense by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.404, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.632)}}: _perfect_ προνενόημαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.48.2, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.69}} (but _infinitive_ πεπρονοῆσθαι {{Refs|17.23), etc.}}—The sense and _construction_ are the same as in the _active_: **provide,** ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.61, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator [same place], 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.64, etc.}}; οὐδέν Pl.[same place]; οἰκίδιόν τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.23; τὰ συμφέροντα ὑπὲρ τῶν μελλόντων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.” [same place]: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.29, etc.; π. περὶ τούτων [prev. work]37; ὑπὲρ ὑμῶν καὶ τῆς πόλεως [prev. author] 26.15, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.4}}: with _infinitive_, **take care to** do, E.[same place], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator [same place]}}; π. μή with _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.135}}; π. ὅπως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.41; ὅτι.. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.16.1; ἵνα.. “Inscription Prien.” 27.12 (2nd c.BC).}}
**B.2** with _genitive_, **provide for,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.9, etc.; τοῦ μέλλοντος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.12; τῆς χώρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.33}}; **superintend,** τῆς οἰκοδομίας Haussoullier {{Refs|“Milet” p.252; τῆς ἀναστάσεως τοῦ ἀνδριάντος “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2930b10 (from Tralles).}}

4307:
** πρόνοια**,
_Ionic dialect_ **προν-οίη**, ἡ, (πρόνοος) **perceiving beforehand, foresight, foreknowledge,** τοὖπος τὸ θεοπρόπον τᾶς παλαιφάτου π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 823 (Lyric poetry); προνοίαισι τοῦ πεπρωμένου 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 684 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** ={πρόγνωσις}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.b, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18 (2).8.}}
**II** **foresight, forethought,** ἐπῄνεσ᾽.. πρόνοιαν ἣν ἔθου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 536; π. δ᾽ ἐστὶν οὐδενὸς σαφής [prev. author] “OT” 978}}; προνοίας οὕνεκα so far as **foresight, caution** is required, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 774, compare “El.” 1015; ἐκ προνοίης}}**with forethought, purposely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120, 159, etc.}}; opposed to κατὰ τύχην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.87, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.21, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241e}}; ἀπὸ προνοίας τινῶν by their **precautions,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.95; τὴν π. τὴν ές ἡμέας ἔχουσαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144}}; προνοίᾳ τῶν συγγενῶν, φίλων, τῆς πόλεως, **by care for..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.56}}; especially of crimes committed **with design** or **malice prepense,** ἐκ προνοίας τραῦμα, ἐκ π. φόνοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.212, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.6, etc.; ἐκ π. ἀποθνῄσκειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.22, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.28}}; τὰ ἐκ π., opposed to ἀκούσια, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1300b26}}; so οὐδεμία π. ἐστι τραύματος no **intention** of wounding, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.41}}; πρόνοιαν ἔχειν (or ἴσχειν) τινός to take **thought** for.., show **care** for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1061, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89, etc.; περί τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 283; ὑπέρ τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.9; ἡ τοῦ χόρτου π. “PFlor.” 131.7 (3rd c.AD), compare 148.2 (3rd c.AD): with}}_infinitive_, πολλὴν π. εἶχεν εὐσχήμως (perhaps εὐσχήμων) πεσεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 569}}; πολλὴν π. ἔχειν μέλλοντας.. to be**ware** of doing a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.91; π. ποιεῖσθαί τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.97, etc.: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.38.}}
**II.2** **providence,** τοῦ θείου ἡ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.108; τοῦ θεοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1180; θεία π. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 637 (troch.); πρόνοιαι θεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 44c}}: _absolutely_, **divine providence,** προνοίας ἔργῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.6, etc., compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.44, Cleanth.[prev. work]121, Chrysipp. [prev. work]2.168, +others (περὶ προνοίας as title of one of his works, [prev. work]3.203).}}
**II.3** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **five,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 31.}}
**II.4** **office of** προνοητής, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 472.10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**III** Πρόνοια Ἀθηνᾶ Athena as **goddess of Forethought,** under which name she was worshipped at Delphi, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.34, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.14, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 25, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.8.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.825b, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.149b, etc.; at Delos _accusative_ to 4th-5th c.AD: Macrobius Grammaticus “Sat.” 1.17.55, compare 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.97 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, “Or.” 37(2).26; also Ἰουλία θεὰ Σεβαστὴ Π. “IG” 3.461}}: this name of Athena, which is guaranteed by the context in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator, 4th-5th c.AD: Macrobius Grammaticus 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” cc., seems to have been a distortion of the name Προναία or Προνᾴα (see. πρόναος 1), but πρόνοια is _falsa lectio_ for{Προναία} (or Προνᾴα) in 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator (see. πρόναος 1), and 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” cc. should perhaps be corrected.}}]

4308:
** προορ-άω**,
_future_ πρόψομαι: _perfect_ προεόρᾱκα: (compare προεῖδον):— **see before one, look forward to,** τὰ ἔμπροσθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author = 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus] “HG” 4.3.23}}; **see what is just before the eyes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44}}: _absolutely_, **look before one** or **forward,** εἰς τὸ πρόσθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 524a14; ὀφθαλμοῖς π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.21.}}
**2** **foresee,** τὸ μέλλον γίνεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24, etc.; π. ὀλίγα περὶ τοῦ μέλλοντος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.15; ἑαυτοῖς τὸ ἐπιόν [prev. author] “Smp.” 4.5; πρὸ τῶν πραγμάτων π. οὐδέν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.41, compare 54.19 (_passive_): _absolutely_, π. διανοίᾳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252a32.}}
**3** with _genitive_, **take thought** or **care, make provision for..,** σεωυτοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.39; τοῦ σίτου [prev. author] 3.159; ἐκείνων προορῶν, ὅκως.. ἔχωσι [prev. author] 2.121}}. α᾽: _absolutely_, τὸ προορᾶν.. σευ your **thoughtfulness,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.79.}}
**4** **see previously,** {{NT|NT.Acts.21.29.}}
**II** _middle_, with _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _passive_, **look before one,** δυοῖν ὀφθαλμοῖν προεωρᾶτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.21 (assuming variant).}}
**II.2** **foresee,** ἐς οἷα φέρονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111; τὸν πόλεμον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.24.}}
**II.3** **provide for,** τὸ ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.17; ταῦτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 499a; πάνθ᾽ ἃ προσήκει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.8, etc.}}; **make provision,** περὶ τῶν μελλόντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 33.7; πρός τι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.102}}; π. μή c._infinitive_, **cavere ne..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.11.}}

4311:
** προπέμπω**,
_aorist_ προέπεμψα, _contraction_ προὔπεμψα, **send before, send forward** or **forth,** πρό μ᾽ ἔπεμψεν ἄναξ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.442; εὖτέ μιν εἰς Ἀΐδαο.. προὔπεμψεν 8.367, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.54, 117, 24.360; π. κήρυκας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.60, compare 4.33 (_passive_), 121, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.29, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1158, etc.; π. πρὸ τοῦ στρατεύματος ἄνδρας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.23}}:—_middle_,{{Refs|[prev. work] 5.3.53, “An.” 7.2.14}}:—_passive_, _impersonal_, προπέπεμπται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77.}}
**b** with a thing for the object, τινὶ φήμας π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1155}}; ξίφος **afford, furnish,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1205}} (Lyric poetry); π. ἄχη **cause,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1287 (Lyric poetry).}}
**2** of things, **send forth,** σποδὸς π. πίονας πλούτου πνοάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 820; ἰοὺς ἀφύκτους καὶ προπέμποντας φόνον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 105.}}
**II** **conduct, escort,** especially a departing traveller, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111, 3.50, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1667, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.16, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 5.2, etc.; τινὰ ἐς δόμους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 530; νύμφην π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.9, etc.}}; π. τινὰ χθονός **from** the land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1099; π. τινὰ μέλεσιν καὶ μολπαῖσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1525 (_anapaest meter_); π. τινὰ τοῖς ἵπποις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.8; τοῖς προπέμπουσι καμήλοις Πολύκαρπον “PFlor.” 206.2 (3rd c.AD)}}; especially **follow** a corpse **to the grave,** τινὰ ἐπὶ τύμβῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1064 (_anapaest meter_); Καρικῇ μούσῃ τοὺς τελευτήσαντας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 800e}}; τιμὰς π. θεοῖς **carry** offerings **in procession,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 622: jocosely, τὸν ἕνα ψωμὸν ἑνὶ ὄψῳ π.}} **let** one piece of bread **be attended** by one condiment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.14.6}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.148}}; of a funeral procession, -πεμφθέντες κοινῇ ὑπὸ τῆς πόλεως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 236d}}; πανδημεὶ προπεμπομένους ἐπὶ θάνατον, of the Minotaur's victims, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.27; ὑπὸ ποιητικῆς ἐπὶ φιλοσοφίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.37b.}}
**II.2** **pursue,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.13.}}

4312:
** προπετ-ής**, ές,
(προπίπτω) **falling** or **slipping down** in bed, εἰ π. γένοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 3; π. ἐπὶ πόδας [prev. author] “Coac.” 487; π. ἂν ἐγίνετο ἡ βάδισις}}**out of control,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 712a29, cf. Diocl.fragment 142.}}
**2** **inclined forward,** κεφαλὴ τοῦ βραχίονος π. ἐς τοὔμπροσθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 1}}; -έστεραι γένυες **more prominent,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 31; ὁ μὲν αὐχὴν.. μὴ π. πεφύκοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.8}}; **sloping,** of shoulders, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.623}}; **stooping,** μὴ ὀρθὸς ἀλλὰ μικρῷ -έστερος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 807b31.}}
**3** **thrown away,** κεῖται προπετές [τὸ κάταγμα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 701.}}
**4** **drooping, at the point of death,** ζῇ γὰρ π. {{Refs|[prev. work] 976 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ἡ π. Μοῖρα **untimely,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(1).1355 (Messenia, 2nd c.AD).}}
**5** **prominent,** of the eyes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.189, 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Synopsis” 8.10, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 2.22}}; γνάθοι, ὀφρῦς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.68, 134.}}
**II** _metaphorically_,
**II.1** **being upon the point of,** πολιὰς ἐπὶ χαίτας π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 909 (Lyric poetry); τύμβου π. παρθένος [prev. author] “Hec.” 150 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.2** **ready for, prone to** a thing, ἐπί or εἴς τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.15, 6.5.24; πρὸς τὰς ἡδονάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 792d}}: with _infinitive_, -έστατος μεταστῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.30.}}
**II.3** **headlong,** π. ἄγειν τὸν ἀκροατήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1409b31.}}
**II.4** **precipitate, rash, reckless,** π. σώματος ἡδοναί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.191}}; π. γέλως **uncontrolled** laughter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.15; εἴ τι -έστερον ἔπραττον 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Dem.fragment” 6, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 441; ἡ π. ἀκρασία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1150b26; π. βίος 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 382; π. γλῶσσα 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.57}}; of a lot, **drawn at random,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.63.}}
**II.4.b** of persons, οἱ θρασεῖς προπετεῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1116a7; τὰ θήλεα.. [τῶν ἀρρένων] -έστερα [prev. author] “HA” 608b1}}; μανικὸς καὶ π. ἐπὶ τῶν κινδύνων Theopomp.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Hist. 268; οἱ π. 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.13.5; οἱ γλώσσῃ προπετεῖς “APl.” 4.89 (Gall.)}}; τὸ π., ={προπέτεια}, opposed to τὸ σεμνόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 1.}}
**II.5** ἁρμονίαι π. **flowing** rhythms, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 40.}}
**II.6** in Medicine texts, **subject to diarrhoea,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.584d (_comparative_).}}
**III** _adverb_ -τῶς **headlong, out of control,** π. εἰς τὸ κάταντες φέρεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.8.}}
**III.2** _metaphorically_, **headlong, hastily,** π. φέρεσθαι εἰς τὴν τυραννίδα Id {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Hier.” 7.2; προπετέως ταχυγλωσσότεροι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.45; ἐπερέσθαι π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.8, cf. Mem. “Epit.” 306}}; ἀποκρίνεσθαι, ἀποφαίνεσθαι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 45a, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.272, etc.}}; π. ἔχειν to be **rash,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.4 (variant); μηδὲν.. πράξῃς π. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 574}}; **prematurely,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 5.144 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus); -έστερον χρῆσθαι ταῖς προνομαῖς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.102.11.}}

4313:
** προπορ-εύω**,
**cause to go before,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.22}}:—_passive_, with _aorist_ _middle_, **go before,** {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 23.10, etc.; π. τινός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 844b5, NT.Act.7.40; πρὸ προσώπου τινός LXX.Exo.32.34, NT.Luke.1.76; πρὸ τοῦ στρατοῦ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.15.1; π. ἐπὶ δὔ ἡμέραις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.52.8}}; οἱ προπορευόμενοι the **van,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.27.2, etc.}}; ἡ προπορευομένη, ={πρόπολος}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Seleucus Grammaticus et Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.267c}}; of a river, **flow onward,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.2.14 (variant).}}
**2** **come forward,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.80.8, etc.}}
**3** **to be promoted, advance,** πρὸς τὴν στρατηγίαν {{Refs|[prev. author] 28.6.9, compare 2.4.2.}}

4314:
** πρός**,
_preposition_, expressing direction,
**A** **on the side of, in the direction of,** hence with _genitive_, _dative_, and _accusative_, **from, at, to**: _Epic dialect_ also **προτί** and **ποτί**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} usually with _accusative_, more rarely with _dative_, and each only once with _genitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.831, 22.198}}:—dialectal forms: _Doric dialect_**ποτί** (which see) and **ποί**, but Cretan dialect **πορτί** {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 5.44, etc.}}, Argive **προτ**(ί) {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 84.3 (found at Tylisus, 5th c.BC), restored in “Mnemos.” 57.208(Argos, 6th c.BC), and in 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 30; 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 5th c.AD: Cypria}} **πός** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 306.11 (Tegea, 4th c.BC), “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.19 H}}., also sometimes in Asia Minor in compounds, see at {ποσάγω},{ποσφέρω}; _Aeolic dialect_ **πρός** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 69 (προσ-), 109, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 20 (assuming variant)}}; **πρές** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Joannes Alexandrinus Grammaticus “Comp.” 3.10}}; Pamphylian περτ (ί) {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 686.7, 686a 4.}} (With **προτί**, **πρός** cf. Sanskrit _práti_ 'towards, near to, against, back, etc.', Slavonic _protiv[ucaron],_ Lett. **pret** 'against', Latin _pretium_: **ποτί** (which see) and **πός** are not _cognate_) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus WITH GEN., πρός}} refers to that **from** which something comes:
**A.I** of Place, **from,** ἵκετο ἠὲ π. ἠοίων ἦ ἑσπερίων ἀνθρώπων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.29; τὸν π. Σάρδεων ἤλεκτρον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1037 (variant).}}
**A.I.2** **on the side of, towards,** νήσοισι πρὸς Ἤλιδος **towards** Elis, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.347}}; π. ἁλός, π. Θύμβρης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.428, 430; εἶναι π. θαλάσσης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.154; ἱδρῦσθαι π. τοῦ Ἑλλησπόντου [prev. author] 8.120; ἐστρατοπεδεύοντο π. Ὀλύνθου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.62, etc.}}; φυλακαὶ π. Αἰθιόπων, π. Ἀραβίων, π. Διβύης, **on the frontier towards** the Ethiopians, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.30: frequently with words denoting the points of the compass, δύω θύραι εἰσίν, αἱ μὲν π. βορέαο, αἱ δ᾽ αὖ π. νότου}} one **on the** north **side,** the other **on the** south **side,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.110; οἰκέουσι π. νότου ἀνέμου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.101; π. ἄρκτου τε καὶ βορέω ἀνέμου κατοικημένοι [prev. work] 102; π. μεσαμβρίης [prev. work] 107}}; π. τοῦ Τμώλου τετραμμένον τῆς πόλιος (in such phrases the _accusative_ is more common) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.84; π. Πλαταιῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.21; π. Νεμέας [prev. author] 5.59}}; ἀπὸ τῆσδε τῆς ὁδοῦ τὸ π. τοῦ λιμένος ἅπαν everything **on the** harbour-**ward side** of this road, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.892: combined with π. with _accusative_, π. ἠῶ τε καὶ τοῦ Τανάϊδος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.122; τὸν μέν π. βορέω ἑστεῶτα, τὸν δὲ π. νότον [prev. author] 2.121, compare 4.17.}}
**A.I.3** **before, in presence of,** μάρτυροι ἔστων π. τε θεῶν μακάρων π. τε θνητῶν ἀνθρώπων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.339; οὐδ᾽ ἐπιορκήσω π. δαίμονος 19.188}}; ποίτοῦ Ἀπόλλωνος.. ὑπίσχομαι probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1126.7 (Amphict. Delph., 4th c.BC); ὑποσχομένους πρὸς τοῦ Διός [prev. work]1237.16: hence}}
**A.I.3.b** **in the eyes of,** ἄδικον οὐδὲν οὔτε π. θεῶν οὔτε π. ἀνθρώπων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.71, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.6, etc.}}; ὅσιος π. θεῶν Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.97}}; κατειπάτω.. ἁγνῶς π. τοῦ θεοῦ if he wishes to be pure **in the sight of** the god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 986.9, compare 17 (Chios, 5th-4th c.BC)}}; ὁ γὰρ καιρὸς π. ἀνθρώπων βραχὺ μέτρον ἔχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.286.}}
**A.I.4** in supplication or adjuration, **before,** and so, **in the name of,** σε.. γουνάζομαι.. π. τ᾽ ἀλόχου καὶ πατρός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.67; π. θεῶν πατρῴων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 839 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; ἱκετεύω, ἀντιβολῶ π. παίδων, π. γυναικῶν, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.20, etc.: the verb is frequently omitted with π. θεῶν or τῶν θεῶν, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 551, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1037, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 760; π. τοῦ Διός [prev. author] “Av.” 130}}: less frequently with other words, π. τῆς ἑστίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 953.39; π. Χαρίτων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 14; μὴ π. γενείου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1208; μὴ π. ξενίας τᾶς σᾶς [prev. author] “OC” 515 (Lyric poetry)}}: sometimes in questions, π. θεῶν, τίς οὕτως εὐήθης ἐστί; **in** heaven's **name,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.15; π. τῆς Ἀθηνᾶς..; 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.45; ἆρ᾽ οὖν, ὦ π. Διός,..; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 459a, compare “Ap.” 26e}}: sometimes in Trag. with the _pronoun_ σε between _preposition_ and case, π. νύν σε πατρὸς π. τε μητρός.. ἱκνοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 468; μὴ π. σε γονάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 324.}}
**A.I.5** of origin or descent, **from, on the side of,** γένος ἐξ Ἁλικαρνησσοῦ τὰ π. πατρός **by** the father's **side,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.99; Ἀθηναῖον.. καὶ τὰ π. πατρὸς καὶ τὰ π. μητρός 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.42, “SIG” 1015.7 (Halic.)}}; πρόγονοι ἢ π. ἀνδρῶν ἢ γυναικῶν **in** the male or female **line,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173d; ὁ πατὴρ π. μὲν ἀνδρῶν ἦν τῶν Εὐπατριδῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.25; οἱ συγγενεῖς τοῦ πατρὸς καὶ π. ἀνδρῶν καὶ π. γυναικῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.23}}; οἱ π. αἵματος blood-relations, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1305; ἢ φίλων τις ἢ π. αἵματος φύσιν [prev. author] “El.” 1125.}}
**A.II** of effects **proceeding from** what cause soever:
**A.II.1** **from, at the hand of,** with Verbs of having, receiving, etc., ὡς ἂν.. τιμὴν καὶ κῦδος ἄρηαι π. πάντων Δαναῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.85, compare 1.160, etc.; τιμὴν π. Ζηνὸς ἔχοντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.302; δίδοι οἱ.. χάριν ποτ᾽ ἀστῶν καὶ ποτὶ ξείνων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.90; ἄνθεα τιμῆς πρὸς θνητῶν ἀνελέσθαι 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 4.7; φυλακῆς π. δήμου κυρῆσαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59; τυχεῖν τινος π. θεῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 550, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 527; λαχὼν π. δαιμόνων ὄλβον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.45; κακόν τι π. θεῶν ἢ π. ἀνθρώπων λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.139, etc.; μανθάνειν π. ἀστῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 13: with passive Verbs, προτὶ Ἀχιλλῆος δεδιδάχθαι}} to have been taught **by..,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.831, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 357; ἄριστα πεποίηται.. πρὸς Τρώων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.57}}; αἴσχε᾽ ἀκούω π. Τρώων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 525, compare 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 79; ταῦτα.. π. τούτου κλύειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 429; οὐ λέγεται π. οὐδαμῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.47; ἀτιμάζεσθαι, τετιμῆσθαι π. τινῶν, [prev. work] 61, 2.75}}; also λόγου οὐδενὸς γίνεσθαι π. τινῶν {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.120}}; παθεῖν τι π. τινός **at the hand of,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 73; π. ἀλλήλοιν θανεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1269, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1237}}; π. τῆς τύχης ὄλωλεν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 949; τὸ ποιεύμενον π. τῶν Λακεδαιμονίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.209; αἰτηθέντες π. τινὸς χρήματα [prev. author] 8.111; ἱμέρου βέλει π. σοῦ τέθαλπται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 650}}: with an _adjective_ or _substantive_, τιμήεσσα π. πόσιος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.162; ἐπίφθονος π. τῶν πλεόνων ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.139; ἔρημος π. φίλων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 919; ἀπαθὴς π. ἀστῶν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.297; πειθὼ π. τινός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 562; π. Τρώων.. κλέος εἶναι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.514}}; ἄρκεσις π. ἀνδρός, δόξα π. ἀνθρώπων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 73, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 624 (Lyric poetry); ἐλίπετο ἀθάνατον μνήμην π. Ἑλλησποντίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.144: with an}} _adverb_, οἶμαι γὰρ ἂν οὐκ ἀχαρίστως μοι ἔχειν οὔτε π. ὑμῶν οὔτε π. τῆς Ἑλλάδος I shall meet with no ingratitude **at** your **hands,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.18, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463d.}}
**A.II.2** of things, π. τίνος ποτ᾽ αἰτίας [τέθνηκεν]; **from** of **by** what cause? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1236}}; π. ἀμπλακημάτων **by** or **by reason of..,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 51.}}
**A.III** of dependence or close connexion: hence,
**A.III.1** **dependent on** one, **under** one's **protection,** π. Διός εἰσι ξεῖνοί τε πτωχοί τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.207, 14.57}}; δικασπόλοι, οἵ τε θέμιστας π. Διὸς εἰρύαται **by commission from** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.239}}; π. ἄλλης ἱστὸν ὑφαίνοις **at the bidding of** another, {{Refs|6.456.}}
**A.III.2** **on** one's **side, in** one's **favour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75, 124, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1434, “Tr.” 479, etc.; π. τῶν ἐχόντων.. τὸν νόμον τίθης 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 57.}}
**A.IV** of that which is **derivable from**: hence, **agreeable to, becoming, like,** τὰ τοιαῦτα ἔργα οὐ π. τοῦ ἅπαντος ἀνδρὸς νενόμικα γίνεσθαι, ἀλλὰπ. ψυχῆς τε ἀγαθῆς καὶ ῥώμης ἀνδρηΐης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.153, compare 5.12}}; ἦ κάρτα π. γυναικὸς αἴρεσθαι κέαρ 'tis very **like** a woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 592, compare 1636; οὐ π. ἰατροῦ σοφοῦ θρηνεῖν ἐπῳδάς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 581, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 369, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 950, etc.; π. σοῦ ἐστι [prev. author] “HF” 585, etc.; οὐκ ἦν π. τοῦ Κύρου τρόπου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.: of qualities, etc., π. δυσσεβείας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 704}}; π. δίκης οὐδὲν τρέμων **agreeably to** justice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1014, compare “El.” 1211; οὐ π. τῆς ὑμετέρας δόξης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59}}; ἐάν τι ἡμῖν π. λόγου ᾖ if it be at all **to** our purpose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 459c; εἰ τόδε π. τρόπου λέγω}}**correctly,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 470c}}; but π. τρόπου τι ὠνεῖσθαι buy **at a reasonable price,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 30.12; τὰ γενήματα π. ἐλάσσονος τιμᾶς πωλῶν “IG” 5(2).515.14 (from Lycosura)}}; π. ἀγαθοῦ, π. κακοῦ τινί ἐστι or γίγνεται, it is **to** one's advantage or otherwise, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 397a30, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 7.16.5, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.12}}; π. ἀτιμίας λαβεῖν τι to take a thing **as** an insult, regard it so, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 13; π. δέους λαβεῖν τι [prev. author] “Flam.” 7}}; λαβεῖν τι π. ὀργῆς (variant{ὀργήν}) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.1.3; μοι π. εὐκλείας γένοιτο [prev. work] 18.7.7}}; τῷ δήμῳ π. αἰσχύνης ἂν ἦν, π. ὀνείδους ἂν ἦν τῇ πόλει, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Declamationes” 43.27, 28.}}
**B** WITH DAT., it expresses proximity, **hard by, near, at,** ποτὶ γαίῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.190, 11.423; ποτὶ γούνασι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.408}}; ποτὶ δρυσίν **among** the oaks, {{Refs|14.398 (nisi to be read περί)}}; πρὸς ἄκμονι χαλκεύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.86; ποτὶ γραμμᾷ στᾶσαί τινα [prev. work] 9.118}}; ἄγκυραν ποτὶ ναΐ κρημνάντων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.24; δῆσαί τινα πρὸς φάραγγι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 15; νεὼς καμούσης ποντίῳ π. κύματι [prev. author] “Th.” 210; π. μέσῃ ἀγορᾷ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 371; π. Ἀργείων στρατῷ [prev. author] “Aj.” 95; π. πέδῳ κεῖται [prev. author] “OT” 180 (Lyric poetry)}}; θακεῖν π. ναοῖς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 20, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 855; π. ἡλίου ναίουσι πηγαῖς [prev. author] “Pr.” 808; π. τῇ γῇ ναυμαχεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.34}}; ἐς μάχην καθίστασθαι π. (variant ὑπ᾽) αὐτῇ τῇ πόλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.79; τεῖχος π. τῇ θαλάσσῃ [prev. author] 3.105; αἱ π. θαλάττῃ πόλεις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.1}}; τὸ π. Αἰγίνῃ στράτευμα **off** Aegina, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.105}}; Αίβυες οἱ π. Αἰγύπτῳ **bordering on..,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 104}}; τὸ π. ποσί that which is **close to** the feet, **before** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 130, etc.}}; θρηνεῖν ἐπῳδὰς π... πήματι **over** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 582}}; αἱ π. τῇ βάσει γωνίαι the angles **at** the base, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.5, +others; τὴν π. τῷ.. ιερῷ κρήνην “IG” 22.338.13, compare “SIG” 1040.15 (Piraeus, 4th c.BC), +others}}
**B.2** **before, in the presence of,** π. τοῖς θεσμοθέταις, π. τῷ διαιτητῇ λέγειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.98, 39.22; ὅσα π. τοῖς κριταῖς γέγονεν [prev. author] 21.18; π. διαιτητῇ φεύγειν [prev. author] 22.28.}}
**B.3** with Verbs denoting **motion towards** a place, **upon, against,** ποτὶ δὲ σκῆπτρον βάλε γαίῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.245, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.80; με βάλῃ.. ποτὶ πέτρῃ 5.415, compare 7.279, 9.284; νῆας ποτὶ σπιλάδεσσιν ἔαξαν 3.298, compare 5.401}}; λιαζόμενον ποτὶ γαίῃ sinking **on** the ground, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.420; ἴσχοντες πρὸς ταῖς πόλεσι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35.}}
**B.4** sometimes with a notion of **clinging** closely, προτὶ οἷ λάβε clasped **to** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.418; προτὶ οἷ εἷλε 21.507; πρὸς ἀλλήλῃσιν ἔχονται 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.329; προσπεπλασμένας.. π. ὄρεσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.111}}; π. δμῳαῖσι κλίνομαι fall **into the arms of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1189; π. τινί}}**close to,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 204.}}
**B.II** to express close engagement, **at the point of,** π. αὐτῷ γ᾽ εἰμὶ τῷ δεινῷ λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1169}}; **engaged in** or **about,** π. τῷ εἰρημένῳ λόγῳ ἦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, compare “Phdr.” 249c, 249d; ἂν π. τῷ σκοπεῖν.. γένησθε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.176; ἀεὶ π. ᾧ εἴη ἔργῳ, τοῦτο ἔπραττεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.22}}; διατρίβειν or σχολάζειν π. τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 11.3 (_anapaest meter_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308b36 (but π. ταῦτα ἐσχόλασα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.6); ὅλος εἶναι π. τῷ λήμματι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.127; π. τῇ ἀνάγκῃ ταύτῃ γίγνεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.74}}; τὴν διάνοιαν, τὴν γνώμην ἔχειν π. τινί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 500b, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.192}}; κατατάξαι αὐτὸν π. γράμμασιν, i.e. give him a post as clerk, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 342.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὁ π. τοῖς γράμμασι τεταγμένος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.27.7, compare 5.54.7, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.29, 3.22; ἐπιμελητὴς π. τῇ εἰκασίᾳ τοῦ σησάμου “PTeb.” 713.2, compare 709.1 (2nd c.BC).}}
**B.III** to express union or addition, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἄασάν μ᾽ ἕταροί τε κακοὶ π. τοῖσί τε ὕπνος}} and **besides** them sleep, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.68; π. τοῖς παροῦσιν ἄλλα}}**in addition to,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 323, compare “Pers.” 531, 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 8.3. 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 59.3; ἄλλους π. ἑαυτῷ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90}}; π. ταῖς ἡμετέραις [τριήρεσι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.90; δέκα μῆνας π. ἄλλοις πέντε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 45; τρίτος.. π. δέκ᾽ ἄλλαισιν γοναῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 774}}; κυβερνήτης π. τῇ σκυτοτομίᾳ **in addition to** his trade of leather-cutter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 397e: frequently with _neuter_ _adjectives_, π. τῷ νέῳ ἁπαλός}} **besides** his youth, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 195c, compare “Tht.” 185e; π. τῷ βλαβερῷ καὶ ἀηδέστατον [prev. author] “Phdr.” 240b}}; π. τούτοισι **besides** this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.51, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 237 (troch.), etc.}}; rarely in _singular_, π. τούτῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31, 41}}; π. τοῖς ἄλλοις **besides** all the rest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.61, etc.:—compare the Advb. usage, below 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**C** WITH ACCU{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, it expresses motion or direction towards an object:}}
**C.I** of Place, **towards, to,** with Verbs of Motion, ἰέναι π. Ὄλυμπον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.420}}; ἰέναι π. δώματα, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.288, etc.; ἰέναι π. ἠῶ τ᾽ ἠέλιόν τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.239; φέρειν προτὶ ἄστυ, ἄγειν προτὶ Ἴλιον, etc., 13.538, 657, etc.; ἄγεσθαιπρὸς οἶκον, ἐρύεσθαι ποτὶ Ἴλιον, 9.147, 18.174; ὠθεῖν, δίεσθαι προτὶ ἄστυ, 16.45, 15.681, etc.}}; ῥίπτασκε ποτὶ νέφεα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.374; βαλεῖν ποτὶ πέτρας 12.71; κυλινδόμενα προτὶ χέρσον 9.147}}; ἀπῆλθε πρὸς ἑαυτόν returned **to** his home, {{LXX|LXX.Num.24.25}}; κληθῆναι π. τὸ δεῖπνον (rarer than ἐπὶ δεῖπνον) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 3.}}
**C.I.2** with Verbs implying previous motion, **upon, against,** π. τεῖχος, π. κίονα ἐρείσας, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.112, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.66; ἅρματα.. ἔκλιναν π. ἐνώπια 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.435; ἔγχος ἔστησε π. κίονα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.127; ποτὶ τοῖχον ἀρηρότες 2.342; ποτὶ βωμὸν ἵζεσθαι 22.334; πρὸς γοῦνα καθέζετό τινος 18.395; π. ἄλλοτ᾽ ἄλλον πημονὴ προσιζάνει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 278; τὰ πολλὰ πατρὸς π. τάφον κτερίσματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 931}}; χῶρον π. αὐτὸν τόνδ᾽ uncertain in {{LXX+NT|[prev. author] “Ph.” 23; later, ἔστη π. τὸν στῦλον LXX.2Ki.23.3; ὁ ὄχλος π. τὴν θάλασσαν ἐπὶ τῆς γῆς ἦσαν NT.Mark.4.1}}; π. ὑμᾶς παραμενῶ **with** you, {{NT|NT.1Cor.16.6; ἐκήδευσαν τὸν.. πατέρα.. π. τοὺς λοιποὺς συγγενεῖς}}**beside,** {{Refs|Supp.epigram 6.106 (from Cotiaeum).}}
**C.I.2.b** of addition, ποὶ τὰν στάλαν ποιγραψάνσθω τάδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 56.46 (Argos, 5th c.BC)}}; ἵππον προσετίθει πρὸς τοὔνομα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 63, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.6, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 33c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1359b28}}; προσεδαπάνησε π. τὸ μερισθὲν αὐτῷ εἰς τὸ ἔλαιον ἐκ τῶν ἰδίων **over and above** the sum allotted to him, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1227.9}}; προσετέθη π. τὸν λαὸν αὐτοῦ was gathered **to** his people, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.49.33.}}
**C.I.3** with Verbs of seeing, looking, etc., **towards,** ἰδεῖν π. τινά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.244, +others}}; ὁρᾶν, ἀποβλέπειν π. τι or τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 725, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 291, etc.; ἀνταυγεῖ π. Ὄλυμπον 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 44}}; στάντε ποτὶ πνοιήν **so as to face** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.622 (similarly, πέτονται πρὸς τὸ πνεῦμα}} **against** the wind, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 597a32)}}; κλαίεσκε π. οὐρανόν cried **to** heaven, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.364: frequently of points of the compass, π. ζόφον κεῖσθαι}} lie **towards** the West, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.26; ναίειν π. ἠῶ τ᾽ ἠέλιόν τε 13.240; στάντα π. πρώτην ἕω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 477}}; so in Prose, π. ἠῶ τε καὶ ἡλίου ἀνατολάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.201, compare 4.40; π. βορέην τε καὶ νότον [prev. author] 2.149; also ἀκτὴ π. Τυρσηνίην τετραμμένη τῆς Σικελίης [prev. author] 6.22 (see. above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2)}}; π. ἥλιον **facing** the sun, and so, **in the** sun**light,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 772}}; so π. λύχνον **by** lamp**light,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 692, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 4; π. τὸ λύχνον 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 22 Diehl, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 375a27; πὸτ τὸ πῦρ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 751; πρὸς τὸ πῦρ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 870a21}}; π. φῶς **in open** day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 640}}; but, **by** torch-**light,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.237a.}}
**C.I.4** in hostile sense, **against,** π. Τρῶας μάχεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.471; ἐστρατόωνθ᾽.. π. τείχεα Θήβης 4.378}}; π. δαίμονα **against** his **will,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.98; βεβλήκει π. στῆθος 4.108; γούνατ᾽ ἐπήδα π. ῥόον ἀΐσσοντος 21.303; χρὴ π. θεὸν οὐκ ἐρίζειν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.88; π. τοὐμὸν σπέρμα χωρήσαντα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 304; ἐπιέναι π. τινάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; ὅσα ἔπραξαν οἱ Ἕλληνες π. τε ἀλλήλους καὶ τὸν βάρβαρον [prev. author] 1.118; ἀγωνίζεσθαι π. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 579c; ἀντιτάττεσθαι π. πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.18}}: also in argument, **in reply to,** ταῦτα π. τὸν Πιττακὸν εἴρηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 345c}}; and so in the titles of judicial speeches, πρός τινα **in reply to,** less strong than κατά τινος **against** or **in accusation,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20 at Title Page, etc.; μήτε π. ἐμὲ μήτε κατ᾽ ἐμοῦ δίκην εἶναι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.34.}}
**C.I.5** without any hostile sense, π. ἀλλήλους ἔπεα πτερόεντ᾽ ἀγόρευον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.155,compare 5.274, 11.403, 17.200}}; π.ξεῖνον φάσθαι ἔπος ἠδ᾽ ἐπακοῦσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.584}}; λέγειν, εἰπεῖν, φράζειν π. τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, 90, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 335, “Nu.” 359; ἀπαγγεῖλαι π. τινάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 267; μνησθῆναι π. τινά 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.19, etc.; ἀμείψασθαι π. τινά 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.60 codices; ἀποκρίνεσθαι π. τινάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 632, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.42}}; ὤμοσε δὲ π. ἔμ᾽ αὐτόν he swore **to** me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.331: π.}} sometimes governs the _reflexive_ _pronoun_, διαλογίζεσθαι π. ὑμᾶς αὐτούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.45}}; ἀναμνήσθητε, ἐνθυμήθητε π. ὑμᾶς αὐτούς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.52, 15.60; π. ἐμαυτὸν.. ἐλογιζόμην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21d}}; μινύρεσθαι, ἄδειν π. ἑαυτόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 880, 931; ἐπικωκύω.. αὐτὴ π. αὑτήν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 285.}}
**C.I.5.b** π. σφέας ἔχειν δοκέουσι, i.e. they think they are pregnant, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 30.}}
**C.I.6** of various kinds of intercourse or reciprocal action, π... Διομήδεα τεύχε᾽ ἄμειβεν changed arms **with** Diomedes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.235; ὅσα.. ξυμβόλαια.. ἦν τοῖς ἰδιώταις π. τοὺς ἰδιώτας ἢ ἰδιώτῃ π. τὸ κοινόν “IG” 12.116.19}}; σπονδάς, συνθήκας ποιεῖσθαι π. τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.15, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.6; ξυγχωρεῖν π. τινάς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.59; γίγνεται ὁμολογία π. τινάς [prev. author] 7.82, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.61; π. τινὰς ξυμμαχίαν ποιεῖς θαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.22; π. ἀλλήλους ἡσυχίαν εἶχον καὶ π. τοὺς ἄλλους.. εἰρήνην ἦγον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.51; π. ἀλλήλους ἔχθραι τε καὶ στέργηθρα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 491; also σαίνειν ποτὶ πάντας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.82, compare “O.” 4.6; παίζειν πρός τινας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 952, etc.; ἀφροδισιάζειν π. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.14; ἀγαθὸς γίγνεσθαι π. τινά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.86; εὐσεβὴς π. τινὰς πέλειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 340}}; διαλέγεσθαι π. τινά converse **with..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.1, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.38,40, 3.219; κοινοῦσθαι π. τινάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 930c; π. τοὺς οἰκέτας ἀνακοινοῦσθαι περὶ τῶν μεγίστων 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.2}}; διαλογίζεσθαι π. τινά balance accounts **with..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 52.3, compare “SIG” 241.127 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}; ἃ ἔχει διελόμενος π. τὸν ἀδελφόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(7).55.8 (Amorgos, 4th-3rd c.BC),compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.34.}}
**C.I.6.b** in phrases of the form ἡ π. τινὰ εὔνοια (ἔχθρα, etc.), π. sometimes means **towards,** as ἡ π. αὑτοὺς φιλία the affection of their wives **towards** or **for** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.39; ἡ π. ὑμᾶς ἔχθρα [prev. author] “HG” 3.5.10; ἡ ἀπέχθεια ἡ π. τοὺς πλουσίους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1305a23; τὴν π. τοὺς τετελευτηκότας εὔνοιαν ὑπάρχουσαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.314, compare “SIG” 352.13 (Ephesus, 4th-3rd c.BC), +others}}; φυσικαὶ τοκέων στοργαὶ π. τέκνα ποθεινά {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).305.13 (from Paros)}}: but sometimes **at the hands of,** ἡ π. τὸ θεῖον εὐμένεια the favour of the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.105}}; φθόνος τοῖς ζῶσι π. τὸ ἀντίπαλον jealousy is incurred by the living **at the hands of** their rivals, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.45}}; τὴν ἀπέχθειαν τὴν π. Θηβαίους.. τῇ πόλει γενέσθαι the hostility incurred by Athens **at the hands of** the Thebans, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.36, compare 6.3, 19.85}}; τῇ φιλίᾳ τῇ π. τὸν τετελευτηκότα the friendship **with** (not 'affection **for**') the deceased, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21c, 28a, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.101, 19.50, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 135, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.19, etc.; τίνος ὄντος ἐμοὶ π. ὑμᾶς ἐγκλήματο; 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.23, compare 16.10; τιμώμενος.. διὰ τὴν π. ὑμᾶς πίστιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.12, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.67, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.25}}; τῷ φόβῳ τῷ π. ὑμᾶς the fear **inspired by** you, {{Refs|[prev. author] 25.93}}; τῇ π. Ῥωμαίους εὐνοία his popularity **with** the Romans, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.7.5.}}
**C.I.7** of legal or other business transacted **before** a magistrate, witness, etc., τάδε ὁ σύλλογος ἐβουλεύσατο.. π. μνήμονας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 45.8 (Halic., 5th c.BC),compare “IG” 7.15.1 (Megara, 2nd c.BC); γράφεσθαι αὐτὸν κλοπῆς.. π. τοὺς ἐπιμελητάς [prev. work]12.65.46; ἀτέλειαν εἶναι αὐτῷ καὶ δίκας π. τὸν πολέμαρχον [prev. work]153.7; λόγον διδόντων τῶν.. χρημάτων.. π. τοὺς λογιστάς [prev. work]91.27}} **before** a jury, ἔστι δὲ τούτοις μὲν π. ὑμᾶς ἁγών, ὑμῖν δὲ π. ἅπασαν τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.14; ἀντιδικῆσαι τῷ παιδὶ.. π. ὑμᾶς 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.19 codices (uncertain)}}; **before** a witness to whom an appeal for corroboration is made, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.25; ὀμόσαντες πὸ (τ) τὸν θεόν “Schwyzer” 418.11 (from Elis)}}; φέρρεν αὐτὸν πὸ (τ) τὸν Δία **in the eyes of** Zeus, {{Refs|[prev. work]415.7([prev. passage])}}; λαχεῖν πρὸς τὸν ἄρχοντα, γράφεσθαι π. τοὺς θεσμοθέτας, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.15, Legal [prev. work] 21.47, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 56.6; τοῖς ἐμπόροις εἶναι τὰς δίκας π. τοὺς θεσμοθέτας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.1}}; θέντων τὰ.. ποτήρια.. π. Πολύχαρμον having pawned the cups **with** P., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IPE” 12.32 A 15 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}; also διαβάλλειν τινὰ π. τοὺς πολλούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.33.}}
**C.II** of Time, **towards** or **near** a certain time, **at** or **about,** ποτὶ ἕσπερα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.191; ποτὶἕσπερον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 552; πρὸς ἑσπέραν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328a; ἐπεὶ π. ἑσπέραν ἦν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.22; π. ἡμέραν [prev. author] “An.” 4.5.21; π. ὄρθρον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1089}}; ποτ᾽ ὄρθρον (nisi to be read πότορθρον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.126, Erinna Lyrica in “PSI” 9.1090.48 + 8 (p.xii); πρὸς ἕω 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 312}}; π.ἀῶ ἐγρέσθαι, π. ἡμέραν ἐξεγρέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.55, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 223c}}; π. γῆρας, π. τὸ γῆρας, **in** old age, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 592, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 653a}}; π. εὐάνθεμον φυάν **in** the bloom of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.67}}; μέχρις ὅτου π. γυναῖκας ὦσι, i.e.of marriageable age, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1368.41: later, π.τὸ παρόν}} **for** the moment, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ep. Sat.” 28, etc.}}; see below 111.5.
**C.III** of Relation between two objects,
**C.III.1** **in reference to, in respect of, touching,** τὰ π. τὸν πόλεμον military matters, equipments, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17, etc.}}; τὰ π. τὸν βασιλέα our **relations to** the King, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.2}}; τὰ π. βασιλέα πράγματα the negotiations **with** the King, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128}}; τὰ π. τοὺς θεούς our relations, i.e. duties, **to** the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1441; μέτεστι π. τὰ ἴδια διάφορα πᾶσι τὸ ἴσον.. ἐλευθέρως δὲ τὰ π. τὸ κοινὸν πολιτεύομεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37; οὐδὲν διοίσει π. τὸ γενέσθαι..}}**in respect of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 24a25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 111b}}; ἕτερος λόγος, οὐ π. ἐμέ that is another matter, and does not **concern** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.44, compare 21,60, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12}}; τῶν φορέτρων ὄντων π. ἐμέ freightage shall be my **concern,** i.e. **borne by** me, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 91.18 (2nd c.AD)}}; π. τοῦτον ἦν ἡ τῶν διαφόρων πρᾶξις {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.4.28}}; ἐὰν.. βοᾷ καὶ σχετλιάζῃ μηδὲν π. τὸ πρᾶγμα, **nihil ad rem,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.61}}; οὐδὲν π. τὸν Διόνυσον Prov. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 39.2.3, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; οὐδὲν αὐτῷ π. τὴν πόλιν ἐστίν he owes no reckoning **to** the State, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.44; λόγος ἐστὶν ἐμοὶ π. Ἀθηναίους 5th c.BC: Philonides Comicus 1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; π. Ἰάσονά ἐστιν αὐτῷ περὶ τῆς τιμῆς “PHamb.” 27.8 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 150.18 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἔσται αὐτῷ π. τὸν Θεόν (i.e. ὁ λόγος) he shall have to reckon **with** God, {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 6.188, compare 194, +others (from Eumenia); without αὐτῷ, [prev. work]236 (from Phrygia)}}; ἔσται π. τὴν Τριάδαν {{Refs|“MAMA” 1.168, compare “Supp.epigram” 6.302 (Laodicea Combusta); ἕξει π. τὸν Θεόν [prev. work]300, +others ([prev. passage]); ἕξει π. τὴν ἐωνίαν κρίσιν [prev. work]4.733 (from Eukhaita), compare 6.841 (from Cyprus)}}; π. πολλοὺς ἔχων ἀγωνιστάς {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see at {ὅσα μῦς ἐν πίσσῃ},{{NT+5th c.BC+|cf. NT.2Cor.5.12: with _adverbs_, ἀσφαλῶς ἔχειν π. τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.14, etc.}}; [τὸ or τὰ] πρός τι, the **relative** term or terms, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 1b25, 6a36, +others}}; τὸ π. τι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **two,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 8}}; π. ἡμᾶς **relatively to** us, opposed to ἁπλῶς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 72a1}}; ὀρθὸς πρός or ποτί with _accusative_, perpendicular **to,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.3, “Spir.” 20; ἁ Δζ ποτὶ τὰν ΑΔ ἀμβλεῖαν ποιεῖ γωνίαν [prev. work] 16.}}
**C.III.2** **in reference to, in consequence of,** πρὸς τοῦτο τὸ κήρυγμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52, compare 4.161}}; π. τὴν φήμην **in view of..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.153, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.39; χαλεπαίνειν π. τι [prev. author] 2.59; ἀθύμως ἔχειν π. τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.4}}, etc.: with _neuter_ _pronoun_, π. τ; **wherefore? to what end?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 766, 1027, etc.}}; π. οὐδέν **for** nothing, in vain, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 1018}}; π. οὐδὲν ἀναγκαῖον unnecessarily, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.23; π. ταῦτα}}**therefore, this being so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.9, 40, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 915, 992, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 426, etc.; compare οὗτος with v111. 1b.}}
