. τὰς τιμὰς τῶν κριθῶν τὰ ἄλφιτα πωλήσουσι **on the basis of** the price of barley, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 51.3}}; ἐξέστω αὐτοῦ ἀπογραφὴ τῆς οὐσίας π. τοῦτο τὸ ἀργύριον Ἀθηναίων τῷ βουλομένῳ property **equal in value to** this silver, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1013.14, compare “PHib.” 1.32.9 (3rd c.BC), “IG” 5(1).1390.78 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; τῶν ἐγγύων τῶν ἐγγυωμένων π. [αὐτὰ] τὰ κτήματα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 364.42 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC)}}; θέντων τὰ ποτήρια π. χρυσοῦς ἑκατόν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IPE” 12.32 A 16 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}; τοὺς ἀπαγομένους εἰς φυλακὴν π. τὰ χρέα imprisoned **for** debt, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.11.10, compare 1.72.5, 5.27.4, 5, 7, 5.108.1, “PTeb.” 707.9 (2nd c.BC)}}; τοὺς π. καταδίκας ἐκπεπτωκότας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 25.3.1, compare “SIG” 742.31 (Ephesus, 1st c.BC)}}; ἐγδίδομεν τὸ ἔργον.. π. χαλκόν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 7.3073.6 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC),compare “PSI” 5.356.7 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 825 (a). 16 (2nd c.BC), “Sammelb.” 5106.3 (2nd c.BC)}}; οἷον π. ἀργύριον τὴν δόξαν τὰς ψυχὰς ἀποδιδόμενοι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.42b}}; π. ἅλας ἠγορασμένος, i.e. 'dirt cheap', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 828 (also π. ἅλα δειπνεῖν καὶ κύαμον}} i.e. dine frugally, take pot-luck, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.684f); so ἡδονὰς π. ἡδονὰς.. καταλλάττεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 69a}}; of measurements of time **by** the flow from the clepsydra, π. ἕνδεκα ἀμφορέας ἐν διαμεμετρημένῃ τῇ ἡμέρᾳ κρίνομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.126, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 67.2, 3, 69.2; λεγέσθω τᾶς δίκας ὁ μὲν πρᾶτος λόγος ἑκατέροις ποτὶ χόας δεκαοκτώ “SIG” 953.17 (Calymna, 2nd c.BC); λεξάντων πρὸς τὴν τήρησιν τοῦ ὕδατος [prev. work]683.60 (Olympia, 2nd c.BC)}}; π. κλεψύδραν Eub.p.182 K., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Epinicus Comicus 2; π. κλεψύδρας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1451a8; π. ὀλίγον ὕδωρ ἀναγκαζόμενος λέγειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.30}}; hence later, π. ὀλίγον **for** a short time, ἐπανεῖναι π. ὀλίγον τὴν πολιορκίαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.9.1, compare 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Topicorum libros octo commentaria - pars” 560.2, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.19, “POxy” 67.14 (4th c.AD), 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 116, “Geoponica” 4.15.8}}; π.ὀλίγον καιρόν, χρόνον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 9.24.26, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus}}**Prooem.;** π. ὀλίγον ἐστὶ τὸ ζῆν poetical in {{Refs|“Mus.Script.” p.452 von Jan; μήτηρ δ᾽ ἦν π. μικρόν “Sammelb.” 7288.4 (from Ptolemaic)}}; π. βραχύ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.47b (but π. βραχὺ παρηβηκυίας}} (**by**) a little past their best, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Geoponica” 4.15.3); π. βραχὺν καιρόν 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21. κα; π. τὸ ἀκαρές 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Gaurum - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 3.3; π. μίαν ἢ δευτέραν ἡμέραν 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.101, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.56; π. δύο ἡμέρας ἐκοίμησα ἐκεῖ “BGU” 775.8 (2nd c.AD)}}; π.μόνην τὴν ἐνεστῶσαν ἡμέραν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 7399 (2nd c.AD),compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 12.4; προστιμάσθω π. χρόνον μὴ εἰσελθεῖν ὅσον ἂν δόξῃ “IG” 22.1368.89.}}
**C.III.5** **in** or **by reference to, according to, in view of,** π. τὸ παρεὸν βουλεύεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.20, compare 113, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, 47, “IG” 22.1.20, etc.; π. τὴν παροῦσαν ἀρρωστίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.47; ἵνα π. τὸν ὑπάρχοντα καιρὸν ἕκαστα θεωρῆτε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.17, compare 314, etc.; εἴ τι δεῖ τεκμαίρεσθαι π. τὸν ἄλλον τρόπον [prev. author] 27.22}}; τοῖς π. ὑμᾶς ζῶσι those who live **with** your **interests in view,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 19.226; ἐλευθέρου τὸ μὴ π. ἄλλον ζῆν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1367a32; π. τοῦτον πάντ᾽ ἐσκόπουν, π. τοῦτον ἐποιοῦντο τὴν εἰρήνην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.63}}; τὸ παιδεύεσθαι π. τὰς πολιτείας **suitably to** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1310a14}}; ὁρῶ.. ἅπαντας π. τὴν παροῦσαν δύναμιν τῶν δικαίων ἀξιουμένους **according to** their power, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.28; π. τὰς τύχας γὰρ τὰς φρένας κεκτήμεθα}}**according to..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 701}}; πὸς τὰς συνθέσις **in accordance with** the agreements, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“IG” 5(2).343.41,60 (Orchom. 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus)}}; τὸν δικαστὰν ὀμνύντα κρῖναι πορτὶ τὰ μωλιόμενα **having regard to** the pleadings, {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 5.44}}, compare 9.30; αἱ ἀρχαὶ.. πρὸς τὰ κατεσκευασμένα σύμβολα σηκώματα ποιησάμεναι after making weights and measures **in accordance with,** or **by reference to,** the established standards, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1013.7}}; π. τὰ στάθμια τὰ ἐν τῷ ἀργυροκοπίῳ **as measured by** the weights in the mint, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 30,compare “PAmh.” 43.10 (2nd c.BC)}}; [Εόλων] ἐποίησε σταθμὰ π. τὸ νόμισμα made (trade-) weights **on the basis of** (i.e. **proportional to**) the coinage, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 10.2; ὀρθὸν π. τὸν διαβήτην “IG” 22.1668.9, compare 95,7.3073.108 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC)}}; π. τὸ δικαιότατον **in accordance with** the most just principle, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 104.6.}}
**C.III.6** **with the accompaniment of** musical instruments, π. κάλαμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).84}}; π. αὐλόν or τὸν αὐλόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 346, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 6.3, etc.; π. λύραν.. ᾄδειν “SIG” 662.13 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; π. ῥυθμὸν ἐμβαίνειν to step **in time,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.34.}}
**C.III.7** **πρός** c._accusative_ frequently _periphrastic_ for _adverb_, π. βίαν, = βιαίως, **under** compulsion, νῦν χρὴ.. τινα π. βίαν πώνην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 20 (assuming variant); π. βίαν ἐπίνομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 73; τὸ π. βίαν πίνειν ἴσον πέφυκε τῷ διψῆν κακόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 735}}; ἥκω.. π. βίαν **under** compulsion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 16.10 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; **by** force, forcibly, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 210, 355, etc.}}; οὐ π. βίαν τινός not **forced** by any one, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 5}} (but also, **in spite of** any one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 657); π. τὸ βίαιον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 130 (Lyric poetry); π. τὸ καρτερόν [prev. author] “Pr.” 214}}; π. ἀλκήν, π. ἀνάγκαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 498, “Pers.” 569 (Lyric poetry); οὐ διαχωρέεει [ἡ γαστὴρ] εἰ μὴ π. ἀνάγκην 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 8, 19; π. ἰσχύος κράτος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 594; π. ἡδονὴν εἶναί τινι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 494}}; π. ἡδονὴν λέγειν, δημηγορεῖν, **so as to** please, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 921, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.38, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 773; οἱ πάντα π. ἡδονὴν ἐπαινοῦντες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1126b13; ἅπαντα π. ἡδ. ζητεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.15, compare 18.4}}; λούσασθαι τὸ σῶμα π. ἡδ. **as much or little as** one like s, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.133; πίνειν π. ἡδ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 176e}}; π. τὸ τερπνόν **calculated to** delight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53}}; π. χάριν **so as to** gratify, μήτε π. ἔχθραν ποιεῖσθαι λόγον μήτε π. χ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.1, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1152; π. χάριν δημηγορεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.3, etc.:}} with _genitive_ _of things_, π. χάριν τινός **for the sake of,** π. χ. βορᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 30, compare “Ph.” 1156 (Lyric poetry); π. ἰσχύος χ.}}**by means of,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 538}}; π. ὀργήν **with** anger, angrily, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 369, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.16 (variant); π. ὀργὴν ἐλθεῖν τινι [prev. author] 39.23, etc.}}; π. τὸ λιπαρές importunately, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1119; π. εὐσέβειαν [prev. author] “El.” 464}}; π. καιρόν seasonably, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 38, etc.; π. φύσιν [prev. author] “Tr.” 308}}; π. εὐτέλειαν cheaply, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 226.2}}; π. μέρος **in** due proportion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.32; π. ὀλίγον μέρος “Geoponica” 2.15.1}}; τέτραπτο π. ἰθύ οἱ straight **towards** him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.403}}; π. ὀρθὰς (i.e. γωνίας (.. τῇ AEB **at** right angles to, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 373a14, compare 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1.11, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 1.3; π. ὀρθὴν τέμνουσα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 363b2}}; π. ἀχθηδόνα, π. ἀπέχθειαν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 9, “Hist.Conscr.” 38}}; γυνὴ π. ἀλήθειαν οὖσα **in** truth a woman, a **very** woman, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.687a, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 48, “Alex.” 61: with _superlative_, π. τὰ μέγιστα}} **in** the highest degree, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.20.}}
**C.III.8** of Numbers. **up to, about,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.7.5, etc.: compare πρόσπου.}}
**D** AB{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}} AS _adverb_., **besides, over and above;** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always π. δέ or ποτὶ δέ, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.307, 10.108, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, etc.; π. δὲ καί [prev. work] 164, 207; π. δὲ ἔτι [prev. author] 3.74; καὶ π. [prev. author] 7.154, 184, probably in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 301, etc.; καὶ π. γε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 110, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328a, 466e; καὶ.. γε π. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 73; καὶ δὴ π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.67}}; frequently at the end of a second clause, τάδε λέγω, δράσω τε π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 622; ἀλογία.., καὶ ἀμαθία γε π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 90e, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 610; ἐνενήκοντα καὶ μικρόν τι π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.28, compare 22.60.}}
**E** IN COMPOSITION (joined with other words),
**E.I** motion **towards,** as προσάγω, προσέρχομαι, etc.
**E.II** addition, **besides,** as προσκτάομαι, προσδίδωμι, προστίθημι, etc.
**E.III** a being **on, at, by,** or **beside**: hence, **a remaining beside,** and _metaphorically_ **connexion and engagement with** anything, as πρόσειμι, προσγίγνομαι, etc.
**F** REMARKS,
**F.1** in poetry πρός sometimes stands after its case and before an attribute, ποίμνας βουστάσεις τε π. πατρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 653, compare “Th.” 185, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 178 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 94}}; ἄστυ πότι (or ποτὶ) σφέτερον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.419, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.5.}}
**F.2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus it is frequently separated from its Verb by tmesis.}}
**F.3** sometimes (in violation of the rule given by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 127.8, “Pron.” 42.5)}} followed by an _enclitic_ _pronoun_, πρός με {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 292, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1055, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.14 (variant), 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 978, “Pk.” 77, “Comica Adespota” 15.25 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 22.68 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.}}

4316:
** προσᾰγορ-εύω**,
_Attic dialect_ _aorist_ being προσεῖπον, _future_ and _perfect_ προσερῶ, προσείρηκα (but προσαγορεῦσαι occurs in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.2.1}}, προσαγορεύσομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 147e)}}_aorist_ _passive_ προσερρήθην (but προσηγορεύθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 834, 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 21.4, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 101.6)}}; coupled with προσείποις, προσρητέον in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, 182d}} {προσαγριαίνω}:—**address, greet,** ἀλλήλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134, 2.80}}; δυστυχοῦντες οὐ προσαγορευόμεθα in misfortune **we are** not **spoken to,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.16; π. τοὺς νέους δι᾽ εὐχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 823d; πόρρωθεν π. 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 5.2; ἐν ταῖς ἐπιστολαῖς τοὺς φίλους π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 315b.}}
**2** with _double_ _accusative_, **address** or **greet as** so and so, Δίκαν δέ νιν προσαγορεύομεν βροτοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 950 (Lyric poetry); τὸν αὐτὸν πατέρα π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.14; βασιλέα π. τινά 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 8}}:—_passive_, ὑφ᾽ ὧν προσηγορεύθης ἡ Διὸς δάμαρ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 834}}; -αγορευθεὶς αὐτοκράτωρ, Latin _imperator consalutatus,_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 8, etc.}}
**3** simply, **call by name, call** so and so, τὸν Ἀγαμέμνονα π. ποιμένα λαῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.2.1; τί τὴν πόλιν προσαγορεύει; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 428d, compare “Grg.” 474e, “Sph.” 216c, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 26; π. τινὰ ὀνόματι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.40, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 291e, “Tht.” 147e, etc.; ὀνομαστὶ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.47; τοῦτο τοὔνομα π. σφᾶς αὐτούς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.8.1}}:— _passive_, **to be called,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 129J., etc.; π. ἑταίρα 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus [same place]; λίθος 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus [same place]; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “R.” 597e, “Phlb.” 54a}}; τῷ τοῦ ὅλου ὀνόματι, ἑνὶ ὀν. π., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 205c, “Sph.” 219b, etc.}}
**4** with _infinitive_, πάσας ἡδονὰς ἀγαθὸν εἶναι προσαγορεύεις {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 13b, compare “Prt.” 325a}}; π. τινὰ χαίρειν **bid** one hail or farewell, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 323, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 771a; also μετὰ τοῦ χαίρειν π. τινά 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 17.}}
**II** **appeal to,** in argument, τὰ τῶν θηρίων ἤθη uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.493c.}}

4317:
** προσάγω** [ᾰ],
_aorist 2_ προσήγᾰγον: for _aorist 1_ προσῆξα see below {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.3 near the end:}}_future_ _middle_ (in _passive_ sense), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.115}}: once **ποσάγω** (which see):— **bring to** or **upon,** τίς δαίμων τόδε πῆμα προσήγαγ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.446, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 993 (Lyric poetry); π. δῶρά τινι “hymnus ad Apollinem” 272; ἄστει κόσμον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).69; θυσίας τινί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.24; βοσκήματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 762; τῳ θεῶν ὕμνους ἢ χορείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 799b; ἱερεῖα τοῖς βωμοῖς 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.27; ποταγόντω.. τὰ ἱερεῖα.. ποτὶ τὸν βωμόν “SIG” 1010 (from Chalcedon)}}; π. πάντα ἱκανά **furnish, supply,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.5}}; ἁρμαμάξας{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.3.1; λίθους “PCair.Zen.” 34.13 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **put to, add,** ἅμα ἠγόρευε καὶ ἔργον προσῆγε (variant{προῆγεν}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.92}}; of exercises and food, ἐξ ὀλίγου π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 89; compare προσαγωγή 11.5.}}
**3** **bring to, move towards, apply,** τὴν ἄνω γνάθον π. τῇ κάτω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.68}}; μὴ π. τὴν χεῖρά μοι **lay** it not **on** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 893}}; π. κεγχρώμασιν ὀφθαλμόν **apply** it **closely,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1386; π. τὴν ῥῖνά τινι 4th c.BC: Diodorus Comicus 2.39; πρὸς τὸ στόμα τὰς χεῖρας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 587a27}}: especially of medical applications, ἤπια [ἰήματα] μετὰ τὰ ἰσχυρά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130; προσαχθέντος φαρμάκου 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 46.1.125: _metaphorically_, [παιδιὰς] π. φαρμακείας χάριν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1337b41; παρρησίαν καὶ δηγμὸν ἀνθρώπῳ δυστυχοῦντι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.69a.}}
**4** of meats, etc., **set before,** βρώματά τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.4, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.126a, etc.}}
**5** _metaphorically_, π. ὅρκους σφι **put** oaths **to** them, **make** them **take** oaths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.74.}}
**6** in military sense, **bring up** for the attack, **move on towards,** π. πύλαις λόχον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1104; τῇ Ποτειδαίᾳ τὸν στρατόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.64; τὸ στράτευμα ἀντίπρῳρον π. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.23; [στρατιὰν] π. πρὸς πολεμίους [prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.6.43}}; see{{Refs 5th c.BC+|below 11: so also π. μηχανὰς πόλει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.27, etc.}}; μηχανῆς μελλούσης προσάξεσθαι (in _passive_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.115}}; π. βίαν τοῖς τείχεσι, τῇ πόλει, etc., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.32, 12.46, etc.}}
**7** _metaphorically_, π. βίαν τοῖς πολεμίοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 15.68, compare “PTeb.” 61 (b). 33 (2nd c.BC, _passive_)}}, etc.; τὰς ἀνάγκας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99; συκοφαντίαν π. τοῖς πράγμασι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.98}}; δεινὰν π. τόλμαν **apply** or **put forth** daring, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 859 (Lyric poetry)}}; γράψας.. τίνα οἰκονομίαν προσαγήγοχας what steps you **have taken,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 240.10 (3rd c.BC)}}; πολλῶν φόβων προσαγομένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.23; π. ἡδονάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 798e.}}
**8** **bring to** or **before,** τῷ Κύρῳ τοὺς αἰχμαλώτους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.12, compare “HG” 3.4.8, etc.}}; **bring in, bring with one,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.16}}; **introduce,** πρὸς τὸν δῆμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.61; πρὸς τὴν βουλήν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.111, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.29}}; π. τοὺς πρέσβεις (i. e. before the assembly) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.28, compare 213; πρεσβείαν ἐλθοῦσαν π. πρὸς βουλὴν καὶ δῆμον “IG” 12.39.12}}; **introduce** at court, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.8}}; **bring** a person **into a law-court** as defendant or as witness, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHal.” 8.5 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**8.b** **introduce** in writing, λόγῳ π. ὅτι.. **introduce** the statement.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 304a13; π. [ἡλικίαν] πρὸς μάθησιν [prev. author] “Pol.” 1336a24; [παιδάριον] π. πρὸς τὰ μαθήματα “PSI” 4.340.24 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ λοιπὰ μυθικῶς προσῆκται **have been introduced,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1074b4.}}
**9** **bring hither, lead on,** τίς [σε] προσήγαγεν χρεί; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 236; ἐλπίς μ᾽ ἀεὶ προσῆγε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 27}}:—_passive_, οἴκτῳ καὶ ἐπιεικείᾳ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.48; βίᾳ [prev. work] 95}}; ἄκοντες π. ὑπ᾽ Ἀθηναίων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 63, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.7.}}
**10** _passive_, **to be brought over, attached** to the cause of, with _dative_, εἴ πως σφίσιν προσαχθείη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.77: _absolutely_, προσήγεσθε ὑπ᾽ Ἀθηναίων [prev. author] 3.63; compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.}}
**11** **increase a rent** or **other charge,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 72.187 (_passive_), 200 (2nd c.BC); προσηγμένων τοῖς ἀπαιτησίμοις [prev. work]217; ᾧ προσάγω ὑπὲρ ἐπιθέματος ἄλλας δραχμὰς ἑξήκοντα “PRyl.” 99.7 (3rd c.AD).}}
**12** ={προσαγγέλλω}, **announce, report,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 60.69 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**13** **debit** a person with an amount, **charge** it to him, συνέβη ναῦλον ἡμῖν προσάγεσθαι τοῦ πλοίου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 368.28, compare 326.16 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** seemingly _intransitive_ (i.e. ἑαυτόν, στρατόν, etc.), **draw near, approach,** {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.22; πρός τινας LXX.1Ki.18.21}}; especially in a hostile sense, **advance against, attack,** π. πρὸς τὸ κέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.9, etc.; κώμῃ τινί 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.4; δι᾽ ἀπάτης τοῖς βασιλεῦσι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.800a; ἐγγυτέρω ταῖς ἐλπίσιν [prev. author] “Galb.” 9}}; τοῖς τετταράκοντα [ἔτεσι] {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pomp.” 46}}; πόταγε (_Doric dialect_ for πρόσαγε) **come on!** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.62, 15.78}}; μαλακῶς π. [γυναικί] **make advances** to a woman in an effeminate manner, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.240e}}; of Time, τῆς προσαγούσης τρύγης the **approaching** vintage, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 5810.16 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.2** (i.e. ναῦν) **bring to, come to land,** τόποις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.54.5, etc.; Ῥόδῳ 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 2.1.4 codices}}
**II.3** δυσχερῶς προσῆγον πρὸς τὰς εἰσφοράς uncertain reading in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.30.5 (πως εἶχον πρὸς Hultsch): ὅσων προσῆξαν}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97 (ὅσωνπερ ἦρξαν Dobree).}}
**B** _middle_, **bring** or **draw to oneself, attach to oneself, bring over to one's side,** σοφίῃ αὐτούς, οὐκ ἀγνωμοσύνῃ προσηγάγετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.172; ἀνάγκῃ προσάγεσθαί τινα [prev. author] 6.25, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99; τἀρετῇ π. πόσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 226; ἀπάτῃ π. τὸ πλῆθος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.43; χρήμασι καὶ δωρεαῖς τὸν δῆμον προσάγεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 695d; τῷ ποιεῖν εὖ π. τὰς πόλεις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.80; θεραπείαις [prev. author] 3.22; so [ἵππον] ἠρεμαίως π. τῷ χαλινῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 9.5; συμμάχους καὶ βοηθοὺς π. [prev. author] “Mem.” 3.4.9; τὴν τῶν Ἀθηναίων ξυμμαχίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.82}}; πάντων π. ὄμματα **draw** all eyes **upon oneself,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.9.}}
**B.2** _absolutely_, **draw to oneself, embrace,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 141, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.39, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 439b; ἥ γ᾽ ἐμὴν γενειάδα προσήγετ᾽ ἀεὶ στόματι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1100.}}
**B.3** with _infinitive_, ἡ Σφὶγξ τὸ πρὸς ποσὶ σκοπεῖν.. ἡμᾶς.. προσήγετο **put** us **upon** considering, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 131}}; προσάξομαι δάμαρτ᾽ ἐᾶν σε.. **will induce** her to suffer thee.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 659.}}
**B.II** **take to oneself, take up,** ὀστᾶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Supp.” 949; τὰ ναυάγια 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.106.}}
**B.II.2** **get for oneself, procure, import,** ὧν δεῖται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 1.7}}; τὰ προσαχθέντα **imports,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.18.}}
**B.II.3** αἷς [ταῖς προβοσκίσι] π. εἰς τὸ στόμα τὴν τροφήν with which **they bring** it to their mouths, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 523b31, compare 526a28, “PA” 685b10.}}
**B.II.4** μηδὲ προσάγου τῷ πράγματι χειμῶνας ἑτέρους **do** not **add** further troubles, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 187}}; π. τὸν χρόνον καὶ τὸν πόνον **employ** it **for one's own advantage,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 29.17.4.}}
**B.II.5** μάρτυρα π. **cite** as witness, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1049b.}}

4318:
** προσᾰγωγ-ή**, ἡ,
**bringing to,** πρὸς τὴν τῆς τροφῆς π. for the purpose of **bringing** the food **to the mouth,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 687b26; οἰκοδόμῳ εἰς π. πλίνθου “PCair.Zen.” 176.14 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **bringing up,** μηχανημάτων, ὀργάνων, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.48.2 (_plural_), 14.10.9 (_plural_)}}; ποιεῖσθαι τὴν π., much like our phrase 'to make **approaches',** {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.41.1.}}
**3** **a bringing over, acquisition,** ξυμμάχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82}}; ἐκ π. φίλος a friend under **compulsion,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.174 (ἐκ προαγωγῆς 1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus).}}
**4** **administering** or **taking** of medicine, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.44 W.(_plural_), 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.148.}}
**II** **solemn approach,** as at festivals or in supplication, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.58 (_plural_).}}
**II.2** **approach, access, introduction to** a person, especially to a king's presence, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.45, cf. NT.Rom.5.2, NT.Eph.2.18, etc.}}
**II.3** π. νεῶν **a place** for ships **to put in,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.1.6, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.46, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 13.}}
**II.4** **attack,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 10.23 (_plural_).}}
**II.5** **addition,** of food, opposed to ἀφαίρεσις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 89; ἐκ προσαγωγῆς}}**by gradual additions, gradually,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Acut.” 11, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.10.5, etc.}}; opposed to ἀθρόος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1308b16; ἐκ π. καὶ κατὰ μικρόν [prev. work] 1306b14, compare 1315a13}}; opposed to ἐξαίφνης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 368a7}}; τόποι ὑψηλοὶ ἐκ π. **rising gradually** to a height, {{Refs|[prev. work] 350b22.}}
**III** **accession, addition,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 190.}}
**III.2** **increase of rent,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 72.449 (2nd c.BC).}}
**IV** **surface** of a stone **intended for application** to another, {{Refs|“IG” 22.244.102 (_plural_).}}

4319:
** προσαιτ-έω**,
**ask besides,** οὐδέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 177.6}}; αἷμα π. **demand more** blood, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 401 (_anapaest meter_)}}; π. μισθόν **demand higher** pay, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.21.}}
**2** **demand** or **require for** a purpose, ὅσους.. τὰ ἔργα προσαιτοίη {{Refs|[prev. author] “Vect.” 4.39.}}
**II** **ask as well as others** (i.e. for a share), and so, with _accusative_ _person_, **ask an alms of,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.83, etc.}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, **beg for** a thing, βίοτον, στρατιάν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 791, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1141}}: with _double_ _accusative_, **beg** somewhat **of** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 512, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.31}}: with _genitive_, **beg some of..,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 4}}: _absolutely_, **to be importunate,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 64 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 429, 452}}; **to be a beggar,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203b, “PCair.Zen.” 493.6 (3rd c.BC), probably in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.294a.}}

4320:
** προσανα-βαίνω**,
_future_ **-βήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.2}}:—**go up,** or **mount besides,** [prev. cited]; of water-birds, π. πρὸς τὰς πέτρας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 617a26; π. πρὸς τὸ ὄρθιον 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.45}}; **rise higher,** as a swollen river, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.72.4; πόλις προσαναβαίνουσα}}**lying on a mountain side,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.20: _metaphorically_, π. τῷ Ῥωμύλῳ}} **go back as far as** R., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 1.}}
**II** with _accusative_ loci, **climb, ascend,** τὸ σιμόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 79.}}

4323:
** προσανα-τίθημι**,
**offer** or **dedicate besides**, δηνάρια πεντακισχίλια {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2782.44 (from Aphrodisias)}}; τῷ θεῷ -τεθεικὼς ἅπαντα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}}**ad Them.**267b; τὴν παρθενίαν θεῷ {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see at {Πουλχερία}:—_middle_, **take an additional burden on oneself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.8}}; but π. τινί τι **contribute of oneself** to another, {{NT|NT.Gal.2.6.}}
**2** **ascribe**, τινί τι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 3.11.}}
**II** προσανατίθεσθαί τινι **take counsel with** one, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.344, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.31 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, NT.Gal.1.16, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 1; τοῖς μάντεσι περί τινος 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.116}}; **refer** a matter **for consideration,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 99.5 (2nd c.BC).}}

4326:
** προσδέω**,
**A** **bind on** or **to**, πρὸς ὕπερον τὸ ξύλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 13; αὐλίσκον πρὸς κύστιν [prev. author] “Nat.Puer.” 17; χρυσαῖς σειραῖς τὸ τοῦ Ἀπόλλωνος ξόανον τῇ βάσει 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.41}}:—_middle_, σιδήριον ὀξὺ -δησάμενος πρὸς τὸν δάκτυλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.28}}:—_passive_, ἥμισυ ἀσκοῦ οἱ προσδέδεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.119; κύνας προσδεδέσθαι νυκτερεύοντας 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 22.14: _metaphorically_, ἡδονῇ π. 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 5.2.7.}}
**A.2** with _accusative_ only, **attach**, τοὺς κάλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36; ἐπ᾽ ἄκρῳ μυρσίνην [prev. author] 4.195.}}

4327:
** προσδέχομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **προσδέκομαι**: used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _Epic dialect_ _present_ _participle_ ποτιδέγμενος (see. below {{Refs|111)}}: _aorist 1_ προσεδέχθην{{Refs 4th c.BC+|in _passive_ sense, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 956b25, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.33.9, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.70}}:— **receive favourably, accept**, τὸ ἐκ Δελφῶν [χρηστήριον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.48, compare “SIG” 557.11 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 3rd c.BC), etc.}}; π. συμμαχίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.2}}; τὴν φιλίαν, τὰς διαλύσεις, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.16.8, 1.17.1; also π. ἑκάστους ἐπὶ.. ὁμολογίαις [prev. author] 3.18.7}}; **receive hospitably**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1428, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1706; ζῶνθ᾽ Ἡρακλῆ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 233.}}
**II** **admit**, ἐς τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.12}}; **admit into one's presence**, of a king, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.37, “HG” 1.5.9}}; of a demos **receiving** foreign emissaries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 561.7 (Chalcis, found at 5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** **admit to citizenship**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 708a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.59}}; so ποία δὲ χέρνιψ φρατέρων προσδέξετα; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 656; τοὺς οἰκέτας π. εἰς τὸ πολίτευμα “IG” 9(2).517.32 (Epist. Philippi, Larissa, 3rd c.BC)}}; π. τινὰ εἰς τοὺς ἐφήβους {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 7333.40 (Alexandria, 2nd c.AD)}}; ὁ προσδεχθησόμενος εἰς τὴν στιβάδα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1368.52; ὅταν τις.. προσδεχθῇ εἴς τι τῶν κατὰ τὸ σῶμα ἀθλημάτων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus [prev. cited]}}
**II.3** of the female, ἡ ἵππος π. τὸν ὄνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HA” 577b15, compare 575b17, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121}}.έ.
**II.4** **admit** an argument, π. τὸ ψεῦδος, λόγον ἀληθῆ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 485c, 561b, compare “SIG” 685.130 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC)}}; π. πρόφασιν **accept** an excuse, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.82 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.5** **admit, be capable of**, μήτε γένεσιν μήτε ὄλεθρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15b; φθοράν [prev. author] “Ti.” 52a.}}
**II.6** **undertake**, προσδέχεσθαι μάλα χρὴ τὰ τοιαῦτα ἰήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 69}}; **take** a liability **upon oneself, guarantee**, τὸ ἀνάλωμα {{Refs|“IG” 5(1).501, 555b, +others (from Sparta)}}; **credit** a sum to a person or an account, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.58 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 4.372.9 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 306.11, 355.69, +others (3rd c.BC), “Ostr.” 1089 (2nd c.BC), “Ostr.Bodl.” i 256 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**III** **await, expect**, the only sense in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, in _Epic dialect_}}_participle_ ποτιδέγμενος **waiting for** or **expecting**, δῶρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.186}}; σὴν ὁρμήν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 403; σὸν μῦθον 7.161; ἡμέας 9.545; λαῶν ὀτρυντύν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.234; ἀγγελίην [prev. work] 336}}; so later, προσδεκομένους τοιοῦτον οὐδέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.146, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 15, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 131 (Lyric poetry), etc.; παρὰ ἃ προσεδέχετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.19}}; τῷ Νικίᾳ προσδεχομένῳ ἦν τὰ παρὰ τῶν Ἐγεσταίων was according to his **expectation**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.46}}; π. τινός τι **expect** anything from anybody, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 10}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_ _future_, οὐδὲν πάντως προσεδέκοντο.. τὸν στόλον ὁρμήσεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34, compare 6.100, 7.156, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.9; πολεμίους παρέσεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.22}}: with _participle_ _future_, τοῦτον π. ἐπαναστησόμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.89}}; πανταχόθεν π. τοὺς πολεμίους **await** them, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.69.6, etc.}}
**III.2** **wait**, ἥατ᾽ ἐνὶ μεγάροις ποτιδέγμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.137, compare 9.628, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.205, etc.; π. ὁππότ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἔλθοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.415}}; π. εἰ with _optative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.91.}}

4328:
** προσδοκ-άω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **προσδοκ-έω** (which is sometimes found as _falsa lectio_, e.g. in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.7.3):}}_future_ -ήσω: _aorist_ -εδόκησα: _perfect_ προσδεδόκηκα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Memnon Historicus 45.3}}:—**expect**, whether in hope or fear; mostly with _infinitive_ _future_, **expect** that one **will do** or that a thing **will be**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 930, 988, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.42, 7.156, 235, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 699b, etc.: with}}_infinitive_ _aorist_ and ἄν, that one **would do** or that a thing **would be**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 556, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 438e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.3, etc.: without ἄν, Μενέλεων.. προσδόκα μολεῖν (={τὸ μολεῖν αὐτόν})}}] **expect** his arrival, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 675.}}
**2** with _infinitive_ _present_, **think, suppose** that one **is doing** or that a thing **is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1091, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 803e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.16}}: with _infinitive_ _perfect_, **think** that a thing **has been.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 275a.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **expect, look for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1026, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 784, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 56, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.19, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.14, etc.}}; π. τινά **expect, wait for** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 363, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.20, etc.; σωτῆρας σφῶν π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 170b.}}
**4** _absolutely_, ἰλλὸς γεγένημαι προσδοκῶν **from expectation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 846}}; μηδεὶς.. προσδοκησάτω ἄλλως (i.e. τοῦτ᾽ ἔσεσθαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17c; πρᾶγμ᾽ ἔστ᾽ ἐπίπονον τὸ προσδοκᾶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Kith.fragment” 7.}}
**5** _passive_, τὸ προσδοκώμενον, opposed to τὰ ἄελπτα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 550; τὸ π. ὑπὸ τῶν πολλῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 966e, etc.; ἐλπίδα τῶν δωρειῶν προσδοκᾶσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.5; θᾶσσον ἢ προσεδοκήθη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.204d.}}
**6** _passive_, also, ὁ Νικίου οἶκος προσεδοκᾶτο εἶναι.. ἑκατὸν ταλάντων **was supposed** to be worth.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.47; προσεδοκᾶτο ἔχειν [prev. work]48.}}
**7** **hesitate**, περί τινος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 7.8.4.}}

4329:
** προσδοκ-ία**, ἡ,
**looking for, expectation**, whether in hope or fear, but more commonly fear,
**1** with _genitive_, μέλλοντος κακοῦ, δεινῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 198b, “Ti.” 70c; π. τοῦ μέλλοντος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 669a21; τὸν φόβον ὁρίζονται π. κακοῦ [prev. author] “EN” 1115a9}}: in good sense, π. ἀγαθῶν ἐμβάλλειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.19 (_plural_)}}; τῆς ἀσφαλείας ἔχειν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.281; π. μεγάλην ἔχειν ὡς εὖ ἐροῦντος ἐμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 194a}}; τὰς τῶν ἔργων προσδοκίας ἀπαιτεῖν τινα, i.e. **the fulfilment of the expectations raised**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.178.}}
**2** _absolutely_, τῶν ὑποκειμένων π. καὶ τῶν ἐλπίδων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.24}}; αἱ ἔσχαται π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.78.}}
**3** followed by a conjunction, προσδοκία οὐδεμία (i.e. ἦν) μὴ ἐπιπλεύσειαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.93; π. οὔσης μή τι νεωτερίσωσιν [prev. author] 5.14; προσδοκίαν παρέχειν ὡς.. [prev. author] 7.12; π. ἐμποιεῖν ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.6.}}
**4** with Preps., πρὸς προσδοκίαν according to **expectation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.63}}; κατὰ τὴν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 264b}}; opposed to παρὰ προσδοκίαν, which is used of a kind of joke frequently in Comedy texts, e.g. ἔχειν ὑπὸ ποσσὶ—χίμεθλα (where πέδιλα was **expected**), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 152, 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ μεθόδου δεινότητος” 34, Tiberius Rhetor “de Figuris - Rhet.” 16: generally, τὸ παρὰ π. ἐξαπίναιον 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.19.}}

4330:
** προσεάω**,
**suffer to go further**, μὴ -εῶντος ἡμᾶς τοῦ ἀνέμου {{NT|NT.Act.27.7.}}
**2** **permit as well,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|PLond. 5.1790.7 (5th-6th c.AD).}}

4331:
** προσεγγίζω**,
**bring near**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 53.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **approach,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 7.422 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus), 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 12.1; τοῖς τόποις 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.16; τοῖς τῆς ἀκμῆς ἰδιώμασι 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 5.30.9}}; τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 585: _absolutely_, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.7.4, 2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 96}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 439}}; πρός c._accusative_, {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.157.}}

4332:
** προσεδρ-εύω**,
**sit near, wait** or **watch beside**, πυρᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 403}}; π. τινί **to be always at** his **side, keep watch on** him, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.26}}; τοῖς πράγμασι, τοῖς καιροῖς, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.18, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.13.9}}; [τοῖς ἐφήβοις] προσκαρτερῶν ἐπιμελῶς καὶ -εύων, of a κοσμητής, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1028.84}}; π. τῷ θεῷ **wait upon** God, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 3.4.1}}; **attend to**, τοῖς τῆς Ἀσίας πράγμασιν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“AJA” 18.327 (Sardis, 1st c.BC),compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2715.18 (from Stratonicea)}}; τῇ θεραπείᾳ τοῦ θεοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.7; τοῖς ὑπομνήμασι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.26d. 5, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.61 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 568b15, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.4.2}}; **watch** the rise of the Nile, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Sammelb.” 6597 (3rd c.AD), +others}}; **persist in**, ταῖς φιλοπονίαις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1338b25; τῷ πόθῳ 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 234}}; **apply oneself**, λίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1337b16}}; πρὸς ἴδιον to one's own affairs,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1263a29; εἰς τὰ μαθήματα “PSI” 1.94.8 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **besiege**, ταῖς Συρακούσαις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.7.11.}}
**3** **wait**, προσέδρευσα ἐφ᾽ ἡμέρας δύο ἐκδεχόμενός σε {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 892.5 (2nd c.AD)}}; especially **attend** at a law-court, παρεῖναι καὶ π. τῷ βήματι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.81.9 (3rd c.AD)}}; **attend regularly, serve**, as clerk of the court, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 82.3 (3rd-4th c.AD), “POxy.” 59.10 (3rd c.AD).}}
**4** **to be in seruice, serve**, πρὸς τῷ διδασκαλείῳ (as a menial), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.258}}; of an apprentice, π. τῷ διδασκάλῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 725.10 (2nd c.AD)}}; of a servant, παραμένειν.. καὶ π. {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PStrassb.” 40.31 (6th c.AD).}}

4333:
** προσεργάζομαι**,
**work besides**, μηδὲν τοῖς δεδραμένοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1013; τὸ χρυσίον τῷ ἀγάλματι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 31}}; ἀγαθὰ π. τινί **do** good **service** to one **besides**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.61 (nisi to be read προ-); ὠμότατον π. τινά 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 17.}}
**2** **make, earn in addition**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.28, “PCair.Zen.” 5c9. 13 (3rd c.BC).}}

4334:
** προσέρχομαι**,
_imperfect_ **-ηρχόμην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.121 (unless from προσάρχομαι)}}: _future_ **-ελεύσομαι**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.14.6 (but the _Attic dialect_ _imperfect_ and _future_ are commonly προσῄειν, πρόσειμι, which see):}}_aorist_ -ήλυθον, -ῆλθον: _perfect_ -ελήλυθα:—**come** or **go to**, with _dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 285, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1104, etc.}}; π. Σωκράτει **visit** him as teacher, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.47}}; τινὶ ὥσπερ ἀθλητῇ Th. [prev. cited] (see. προσάρχομαι); αἷς ἂν προσέλθω [γυναιξί] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.38: with _dative_ loci, δόμοις, ἀκταῖς, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 474, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1539: with _accusative_ loci, πεσσούς, δῶμα, βωμούς, [prev. author] “Med.” 68, 1205, Alcaeus171: rarely with _accusative_ _person_, ἐπειδὴ τοὺς πρυτάνεις προσήλθομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristomenes Comicus 4: with Preps. governing _accusative_, π. πρὸς τὸ ἄγγος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. ; πρὸς Απολλώνιον “PCair.Zen.” 375.4 (3rd c.BC)}}: with _adverbs_, π. δεῦρο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1171, etc.; πέλας π. μου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 589, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1076, etc.}}; ἐγγύθεν, ὄπισθεν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 289d, “R.” 327b; ὅπῃ π. χρή [prev. work] 493b}}: _absolutely_, **approach, draw nigh**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, etc.; opposed to ἀπέρχομαι, [prev. work] 199}}; of pain, pleasure, etc., **to be nigh at hand**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 788, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 859.}}
**2** in hostile sense, **attack**, π. πρὸς τοὺς ἱππέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.16.}}
**3** **come in, surrender, capitulate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.59.}}
**4** **come forward to speak**, π. τῷ δήμῳ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.13; πρὸς τὸν δῆμον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.220; πρὸς ὑμᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.69, 24.176; πρὸς τοὺς ἱερομνήμονας “SIG” 419.6 (Delph., 3rd c.BC), compare 613.24 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC), +others}}; π. πολιτείᾳ **enter** political life, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 12}}; π. πρὸς τὰ κοινά **come forward** in public, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.257}}; π. πρὸς τὸ πολιτεύεσθαι, πρὸς τὴν πολιτείαν, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.111 (variant{εἰς}), 2.15; πρὸς τὴν πόλιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.30; π. πρὸς ἓν πρᾶγμα ἴδιον [prev. author] 32.32}}]; ὑμῖν (i.e. Ἀθηναίοις) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 25.42; ἐπὶ τοὺς συμμάχους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.3.}}
**5** **appear** before a tribunal, προσελθὼν εἶπεν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 587.2 (2nd c.AD),compare “PAmh.” 2.66.43 (2nd c.AD)}}; π. τῷ δικαστηρίῳ κατ᾽ αὐτοῦ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PSI” 1.41.18 (4th c.AD)}}; **approach** an official, π. διὰ βιβλιδίων τῷ λαμπροτάτῳ ἡγεμόνι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 614.12 (3rd c.AD)}}; π. τοῖς θεοῖς in supplication, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 56.9.}}
**6** π. τῇ φιλοσοφίᾳ, τοῖς νόμοις, **apply oneself to.. **, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.18, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.95; ἐπεὶ προσῆλθον ἀγορασμῷ ἢ καὶ ὑποθήκῃ κλήρου κατοικικοῦ “BGU” 650.6 (1st c.AD); ἐξ οὗ χρόνου προσῆλθεν ἕκαστος τῇ μισθώσει [prev. work] 1047 iv 6 (2nd c.AD)}}; π. τῇ τούτου κληρονομίᾳ **enter upon** his inheritance, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 76.22 (2nd c.AD), compare 907.5 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; **have recourse to**, τοῖς ἀνασκευαστικωτέροις {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.50.}}
**7** of things, **to be added**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 321b27, “GA” 723a13.}}
**II** **come in**, of revenue, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.144, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.20, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.12.}}
**III** **have sexual intercourse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.3.14.}}

4335:
** προσευχή**, ἡ,
**prayer**, οἶκος προσευχῆς, of the Temple, {{LXX+3rd c.AD+|LXX.Isa.56.7; κατὰ τὰς κοινὰς ἡμῶν εὐχὰς καὶ προσευχάς “BGU” 1080.5 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** **place of prayer, sanctuary, chapel**, {{Refs|“IPE” 12.176 (from Olbia), 2.52 (from Panticapaeum)}}; especially among the Jews. **synagogue,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|PEnteux. 30.5 (3rd c.BC), “OGI” 726 (Egypt, 3rd c.BC), 96.6 ([prev. passage], 3rd-2nd c.BC), +others, “PTeb.” 86.18 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.523, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 54, 1st c.AD: Apion Grammaticus cited in [same work] “Ap.” 2.2, NT.Act.16.13, 1st c.AD: Juvenalis, D. Junius Poeta Latinus 3.296.}}

4336:
** προσεύχομαι**,
_future_ **-ξομαι**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 317}}:—**offer prayers** or **vows**, θεοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus [same place], compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 116, +others, etc.; τῷ ἡλίῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220d; θεῷ π. σωτηρίαν ἡμῖν διδόναι [prev. author] “Criti.” 106a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.1.}}
**2** with _accusative_, π. τὸν θεόν **address** him **in prayer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 958, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 887.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **offer prayers, worship**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.48, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 937, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1337, etc.}}; π. γλώσσῃ, πνεύματι, νοΐ, {{NT|NT.1Cor.14.14, 15.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **pray for** a thing, νίκην πολέμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.22}}: c._infinitive_, ἕλκειν τὸ βέδυ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Philyllius Comicus 20}}; ζῆσαι προσεύχου **pray** for life, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 1040.11 (from Adada): followed by τοῦ}} with _infinitive_, {{NT|NT.Jam.5.17}}; π. ἵνα.., περί τινων ὅπως, {{NT|NT.Matt.24.20, NT.Act.8.15.}}

4337:
** προσέχω**
{{Refs 5th c.AD+|(5th c.AD: Cypria}} **ποέχω** (which see)) and **προσίσχω**: _aorist_ προσέσχον:— **hold to, offer**, προσέσχε μαζὸν [δράκοντι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 531}}; **hold against**, [τὴν ἀσπίδα] προσῖσχε πρὸς τὸ δάπεδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.200}}; **apply**, χλιάσματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.129.}}
**2** π. ναῦν **bring** a ship **to port**, προσσχόντες τὰς νέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.99; Μαλέᾳ προσίσχων πρῷραν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 362}}; τίς σε προσέσχε.. χρεί; **brought** thee **to land** here? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 236; [ναῦν] πρὸς τὴν γῆν προσσχεῖν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.4: more frequently without ναῦν}}, **put in, touch at** a place, προσσχεῖν ἐς Τύρον, ἐς τὴν Σάμον, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, 3.48, +others; πρὸς τὴν Σίφνον προσῖσχον [prev. work] 58}}: with _dative_ loci, π. τῇ γῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.156; τῆς νήσου τοῖς ἐσχάτοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.30; Λιβύῃ κατὰ τὴν Μαυρουσίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 7: with _accusative_ loci, τίνι στόλῳ προσέσχες τήνδε γῆ; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 244, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.9.2}}: _absolutely_, **land**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.182, etc.}}: with words added, πλέων δι᾽ Ἑλλησπόντου π. ἐς Κύζικον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.76, compare 6.119; ναυσὶ προσσχεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.11; τῇ νηῒ π. εἰς Ῥόδον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.9}}; ὡς γῇ προσέξων τὸ σῶμα, of a shipwrecked sailor, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1103e.}}
**3** **turn to** or **towards** a thing, π. ὄμμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 931: mostly, π. τὸν νοῦν}} **turn one's mind, attention to** a thing, **be intent on** it, τοῖς ἀναπαίστοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 503; ἐμοί [prev. work] 1014, compare 1064, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.2, etc.}}; π. τὸν νοῦν τινι **give heed** to him, **pay court** to him, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 5.5.40}}; ἑαυτῷ π. τὸν νοῦν to be thinking with himself, in a fit of abstraction, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174d; also πρὸς τὴν ἑαυτοῦ κατηγορίαν π. τὸν νοῦν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.1; πρὸς τούτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1010}}; π. τὸν νοῦν μὴ.. **take heed** lest.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 432b, etc.: _absolutely_, πρόσεχε τὸν νοῦν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 284, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 154, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 113, etc.; δεῦρο τὸν νοῦν προσέχετε [prev. author] “Nu.” 575, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217b}}; προσεχέτω τὸν νοῦν **let him take heed**, as a warning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1122}}; also τὴν γνώμην π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ec.” 600, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.95, 2.11, 5.26, 7.15; π. τὴν διάνοιαν ὡς πράξει μεγίστῃ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 14}}; but περὶ τούτου τῇ διανοίᾳ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 7.2225.44 (2nd c.BC)}}; π. τῇ διανοίᾳ εἰς τὸ ῥῆμα Κυρίου {{LXX|LXX.Exo.9.21.}}
**4** without τὸν νοῦν, μὴ πρόσισχε.. βουκόλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 286}}; σαυτῷ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 294 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.7.9}}; π. ἑαυτοῖς ἀπό τινος **to be on one's guard** against, {{NT|NT.Luke.12.1}}; πρόσεχ᾽ οἷς φράζω **attend** to what I shall tell you, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.21 (_anapaest meter_), compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.3, etc.; π. τῶν ἐμπείρων.. ταῖς ἀναποδείκτοις φάσεσι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1143b11; τῷ πολλῷ χρόνῳ [prev. author] “Pol.” 1264a2; π. τοῖς νόμοις [prev. author] “fragment” 539}}; τοῖς χιλιάρχοις **take orders from** them, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.37.7; also π. ἐπί τινι LXX.Gen.4.5: _absolutely_, πρόσεχε, κἀγώ σοι φράσω 3rd c.BC: Athenio Comicus 1.8}}; προσέχων ἀκουσάτω **attentively**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.8; πρόσσχες “An. Ox.” 1.121: also with _accusative_, προσέχων τε ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 25.19 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; οὐ προσέχει τὰ πράγματα 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 73.4; π. νόμον θεοῦ LXX.Isa.1.11, cf. LXX.Exo.34.11: also π. ἀπὸ τῶν ἁγίων, τῶν γραμματέων,LXX.Lev.22.2, NT.Luke.20.46; π. τοῦ μὴ φαγεῖν αἷμα LXX.Deu.12.23}}; π. ἵνα μὴ μαστιγωθῇς{{LXX|LXX.2Ch.25.16.}}
**4.b** **devote oneself to** a thing, with _dative_, γυμνασίοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.33; τοῖς ἔργοις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 553; τοῖς ναυτικοῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.15; τῷ πολέμῳ [prev. author] 7.4; πλούτῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.122d; τούτῳ τῷ ἀγῶνι 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 10}}; τοῖς κοινοῖς, γεωργίᾳ καὶ εἰρήνῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 19, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.11.3, etc.:— _absolutely_, ἐντεταμένως, προθύμως π., 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18, 8.128.}}
**5** **continue**, ἡ νοῦσος, ἡ ὀδύνη π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 11, 7.}}
**6** _middle_, **attach oneself to** a thing, **cling, cleave to** it, ὅ τι πρόσσχοιτο τοῦ πηλοῦ τῷ κοντῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.136; ὥσπερ λεπὰς προσεχόμενος τῷ κίονι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 105, compare “Pl.” 1096; τῷ τοίχῳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 555a1: _absolutely_, οἱ πολύποδες οὕτω π. ὥστε μὴ ἀποσπᾶσθαι [prev. work] 534b27.}}
**6.b** _metaphorically_, **devote oneself to the service of** any one, especially a god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.51 (uncertain).}}
**7** _passive_, **to be held fast** by a thing, ὑπό τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 756}}; **to be attached** to it, πρὸς τῷ στήθει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14}}; πρὸς τῷ δένδρῳ προσέχεσθαι, of gum, **stick to**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.4.4}}: _metaphorically_, **to be implicated in**, τῷ ἄγει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.127.}}
**II** **have besides** or **in addition**, δεῖ καὶ τοῦτο προσέχειν τὸ μάθημα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 521d, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 31.7, etc.}}

4338:
** προσηλόω**,
**nail, rivet, fix to**, [Ἰξίονα] τῷ τροχῷ {{Refs|E.cited in Plu. 2.19e}}; σταυρῷ τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.14.9, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 2; ἐν δέλτῳ γεγραμμένα π. “IG” 12(2).35b19 (from Mytilene): _metaphorically_, ψυχὴν πρὸς τὸ σῶμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 83d, compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 2.6}}: with _accusative_ _person_, **crucify**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.206a}}:—_passive_, **to be fastened by nails,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1640.7, 14.759}}; of persons, ={προσπασσαλεύω}, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.105; τοῖς ζυγοῖς τῶν πλοίων προσηλωμένοι τοὺς τραχήλους LXX.3Mac.4.9: _metaphorically_, 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 106}}; of the soul, π. φθαρτικαῖς ὕλαις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.237; προσηλωθέντα, εἰ χρὴ φάναι, τῷ θεῷ 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 1.57.}}
**II** **nail up**, τὰ παρασκήνια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.17:}} —_passive_, τὸ ἐργαστήριον σανιδίοις προσηλοῦσθαι to be boarded up, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“SIG” 799.26 (Cyzicus, 1st c.AD).}}

4339:
** προσήλυτ-ος**, ον,
**one that has arrived at** a place, **stranger, sojourner**, τῷ προσελθόντι προσηλύτῳ{{LXX|LXX.Exo.12.49, +others}}
**II** **one who has come over to Judaism, convert, proselyte**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.219, NT.Matt.23.15, NT.Act.2.10.}}

4340:
** πρόσκαιρος**, ον,
**occasional, extraordinary**, ἑορτή {{Refs|“IG” 22.1368.44}}; αἱ π. ἐπιβολαί the **additional** taxes, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.979.19 (4th c.AD)}}; τὰ δημόσια τέλη κανονικά τε καὶ π. τούτων {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 168.36 (6th c.AD).}}
**2** **opportune**, ἐκδρομαί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 15; θόρυβοι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 31}}; ῥῆμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 274.}}
**3** **at the time**, τὰ π. ἄδηλα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.78.}}
**4** πρόσκαιρον, τό, **agreement having temporary validity,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Sammelb. 6000v.35 (6th c.AD).}}
**II** **lasting for a time, temporary**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.3.11; ἀνοχή 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 46.1}}; π. ἡ τέρψις, opposed to ἀθάνατος, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 7.4, 6}}; opposed to αἰώνιος, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.2Cor.4.18, compare “OGI” 669.14 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; **transient,** {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Matt.13.21, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.8.8}}. _adverb_ **-ρως**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.31, Pseudo-Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.58, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 4.14.7.}}

4341:
** προσκᾰλέω**,
**call on, summon**, τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.98 (variant), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 89, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 82a, etc.}}; **address, accost**, ὀνόματι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 71.34; ἑαυτόν 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 218.27 (_middle_).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **call forth, excite**, ἔκκρισιν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.26; ἱδρῶτα [prev. work] 31.}}
**II** _middle_ with _perfect_ _passive_ (see. below), **call to oneself, invite, summon**, τινα variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.2, compare “PCair.Zen.” 647.25 (3rd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.354d, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 19.1; τὰς κύνας 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.85}}; especially **call to one's aid**, τινα Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.166}}; τινὰ ἐς τὴν πολιτείαν uncertain reading in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 21: with double _accusative_, τὸ ἔργον ὃ προσκέκλημαι αὐτούς}} to which **I have called** them, {{NT|NT.Act.13.2.}}
**II.2** as law-term, of an accuser, **cite** or **summon into court**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1334}}; π. τινὰ ὕβρεως **lay** an action for assault, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1417}}; in full, π. δίκην ἀσεβείας πρὸς τὸν βασιλέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.11, compare 21.19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.150; π. τινὰ πρὸς τὸν πολέμαρχον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 23.2; π. σε.. πρὸς τοὺς ἀγορανόμους βλάβης τῶν φορτίων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1406; π. τινὰ εἰς δίκην δημοσίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.5; π. τινὰ πρὸς τὸν ἄρχοντα εἰς διαδικασίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.7, compare 15; τραύματος εἰς Ἄρειον πάγον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 46, compare “Pisc.” 39}}:—_passive_, **to be summoned**, λιποταξίου, ξενίας, **on a charge of.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.17,18; φόνου δίκην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 16.8}}; ὑπομεῖναι προσκληθεὶς δίκην εἰς Ἄρειον πάγον submitted **to be summoned.. **before the Areopagus, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1315b21}}; προσκληθείς **summoned**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.13, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1277}}; παρὰ τοῦ.. ἔχοντος τὸν κλῆρον προσκαλεῖσθαι **that citation should be made** of the party in possession, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.7}}; compare πρόσκλησις.
**II.3** **cite as witness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936e codices; εἰς μαρτυρίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.20 codices; μάρτυρα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.205b.}}

4342:
** προσκαρτερ-έω**,
_Doric dialect_ **ποτι-** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).63.4 (Epid., 2nd c.BC)}}:— **persist obstinately in**, τῇ πολιορκίᾳ {{NT+5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.55.4, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.87; τῇ προφορᾷ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.158 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; τῇ προσευχῇ NT.Act.1.14: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.5.14, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 101.9, LXX.Num.13.21(20), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.1.3, 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.10}}; καίπερ ἀχθόμενοι τῇ καθέδρᾳ π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 5.2.6.}}
**2** **adhere firmly to** a man, **be faithful to** him, τινι {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.5.3, NT.Act.8.13, 10.7}}; of servants, **remain in** one's **service**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.120}}; of a κοσμητής, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1028.84.}}
**2.b** **remain in attendance** at a law-court, τῷ βήματι, τῷ κριτηρίῳ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PHamb.” 4.7 (1st c.AD), “POxy.” 261.12 (1st c.AD).}}
**2.c** **devote oneself to** an office or occupation, τῇ στρατηγίᾳ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 82.4 (3rd c.AD)}}; τῇ ἑαυτῶν γεωργίᾳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.65.3 (2nd c.AD).}}
**3** _passive_, ὁ προσκαρτερούμενος χρόνος time **diligently employed**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.29.}}
**4** **wait for** a person, Φιλέᾳ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“POxy.” 1764.4 (3rd c.AD): _absolutely_, ἕως ἂν Ἐτέαρχος παραγένηται “PSI” 5.598.7 (3rd c.BC).}}

4344:
** προσκεφᾰλ-αιον**, τό,
**cushion for the head, pillow**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 16, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 542, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.18, etc.}}: generally, **any cushion**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 269, 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 54, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.11, “PCair.Zen.” 92.22 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Eze.13.18, NT.Mark.4.38, etc.}}: _Doric dialect_ **ποτικεφάλαιον** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.23 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; also **ποικεφ-**, {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 323 C 30 (Delph.).}}; ** προσκεφᾰλ-ίς**, ίδος, ἡ,, {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**II** name for **a treasure-chamber** of the Persian kings, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Chares Historicus 2 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}

4346:
** πρόσ-κλῐσις**, εως, ἡ,
**leaning against**, ζῴου πρὸς δένδρον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.27.}}
**2** **genuflexion**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.12 (_plural_).}}
**II** **inclination, predilection**, τῶν γερόντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.10.10}}; τινι to one, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.51.8; αἵρεσίς ἐστι π. δογμάτων “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.37}}; π. δόγμασιν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. passage], 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius “Prooem.” 20, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.16; μετὰ -κλίσεως Carnead. and Clitomach. cited in [same work][prev. work] 230}}; κατὰ πρόσκλισιν with **partiality**, {{NT|NT.1Tim.5.21.}}

4347:
** προσκολλ-άω**,
**glue on** or **to**, τι πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 33}}:—_passive_, generally, **to be stuck to, stick** or **cleave to**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 82e, “Lg.” 728b}}; ὑπὸ τοῦ αἵματος προσκολληθῆναι τὴν ῥομφαίαν αὐτοῦ τῇ δεξιᾷ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 7.12.4}}; of a snail, τοῖς θαμνίσκοις π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.9}}; of a husband, π. τῇ γυναικί {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.19.5, cf. LXX.Gen.2.24, NT.Mark.10.7, NT.Eph.5.31; τοῖς ἐπαοιδοῖς LXX.Lev.19.31; ψυχαὶ π. θεῷ 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus “Fragmenta” 51 H.}}
**II** _intransitive_ of style, **to be compact**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Demosthene” 43.}}

4348:
** πρόσκομμα**, ατος, τό,
(προσκόπτω) **stumble**, λίθου πρόσκομμα {{LXX|LXX.Isa.8.14}}; ὁ λίθος τοῦ π. {{NT|NT.Rom.9.33}}: hence, **offence, obstacle, hindrance**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.23.33, NT.Rom.14.13, etc.}}
**II** **result of stumbling, bruise, hurt**, προσκομμάτων ἀπόλυσις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1048c, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.97f.}}

4349:
** προσ-κοπή**, ἡ,
= πρόσκομμα,
**B** **offence taken**, φθόνος καὶ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.7.8; π. καὶ μύσος [prev. author] 30.29.7}}; πρός τινα ἀλλοτριότης καὶ π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 31.10.4, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Po.Herc.” 994.38, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.17}}; νοσήματα κατὰ προσκοπὴν γινόμενα, i.e. antipathies, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.102; προσκοπῆς ἄξιος 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.195: but μηδεμίαν π. διδόναι}} give no **cause of offence**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.6.3.}}

4350:
** προσκόπτω**,
**strike** one thing **against** another, πρὸς λίθον τὸν πόδα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.90(91).12; π. τὸν δάκτυλόν που 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 275 (lyr).}}
**b** _intransitive_, **stumble** or **strike against**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.6, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 81, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 882b18, “GC” 326a27, etc.}}; π. τῷ ὀφθαλμῷ (i.e. ῥίζῃ κυάμου) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.8.8}}; of liquid, **to be checked by striking against**, with _dative_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 9}}; πνεῦμα προσκόπτον **broken, interrupted** breathing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.68.}}
**2** π. τῷ ἄξονι **encounter friction at.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 852a32.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, ={προσκρούω} {{Refs|111}}**offend**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 36; τοῖς πολλοῖς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.49.5}}; οὐ μόνον δυσαρεστήσειν ἀλλὰ καὶ προσκόπτειν ἔμελλε προφανῶς, of an open **breach**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.5.6.}}
**II.2** **take offence at**, ἵνα μή μοι προσκόψῃς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 463.11 (3rd c.BC)}}; τῇ βαρύτητί τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.31.7, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Carneiscus Philosophus “Herc.” 1027.14; θεοὺς -κόψαντάς τισι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.59}}; of things, π. τῷ ζῆν **to be disgusted with** life, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.61:}} —_passive_, δῆμος π. αὐτῷ διά τι **being offended with** him, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.27, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 30, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 9.3.}}

4351:
** προσκῠλ-ίω**,
**roll to, roll up**, τὸν ὅλμον.. προσκύλιε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 202}} codices: _aorist_ _participle_, προσκυλίσας λίθον {{NT|NT.Matt.27.60, cf. NT.Mark.15.46.}}
**II** _metaphorically_ in _passive_, **wallow in**, τοῖς ἀφροδισίοις {{Refs|Anonymus “in EN” 436.7}}. [; but προσκυλῐσασα in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 5.200.]}}

4352:
** προσκῠν-έω**,
_future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 32 (tmesis), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 469a}}: _aorist_ προσεκύνησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.18; poetry προσέκῠσα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 640, _imperative_ πρόσκυσον [prev. work] 156, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 776}}, _infinitive_ **-κύσαι** {{Refs|[prev. work] 657}}, _participle_ **-κύσας** {{Refs|[prev. work] 533, 1408 (troch.)}}: _perfect_ **-κεκύνηκα**{{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Exo.32.8, “OGI” 196.2 (Egypt, 1st c.BC)}}:—_passive_, _present_ _infinitive_ προσκυνεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1021}}: _aorist_ προσεκυνήθην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 4.11.9}}:— **make obeisance** to the gods or their images, **fall down and worship**, with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121, etc.; γῆν τε π. ἅμα καὶ τὸν θεῶν Ὄλυμπον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1654, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 499, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 156: proverbial, οἱ -κυνοῦντες τὴν Ἀδράστειαν σοφοί}}, of deprecating the wrath of Nemesis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 936, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451a; τὸν φθόνον δὲ πρόσκυσον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 776; στεῖχε προσκύσας χθόνα, to avert divine wrath, [prev. work] 1408 (troch.)}}; of sacred places, **do reverence to**, πατρῷα ἕδη θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 1374; τὰς θήκας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 469b; τὴν θόλον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.314: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 640.}}
**2** especially of the Oriental fashion of **prostrating oneself before** kings and superiors, _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119, 8.118: with _accusative_, π. τὸν Δαρεῖον}} **make obeisance** to him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.86}}; προσκυνεῖν διδάσκονται τὸν βασιλέα [οἱ ἐλέφαντες] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 630b20}}; προσπίπτων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134, compare 7.136; πάντες σε προσκυνοῦμεν οἵδ᾽ ἱκτήριοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 327}}; προσκυνῶ σ᾽, ἄναξ, προσπίτνων {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1507 (troch.), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.18, 8.3.14, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 27, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus [same place], etc.; κύψας ὁ λαὸς προσεκύνησεν LXX.Exo.12.27}}; οὐδένα ἄνθρωπον δεσπότην ἀλλὰ τοὺς θεοὺς π. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13; π. τοὺς ὑβρίζοντας ὥσπερ ἐν τοῖς βαρβάροις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.106: ironically, π. τινὰ ὡς ἱερὸν καὶ θαυμαστόν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398a: later with _dative_, LXX.Gen.24.26, +others, NT.Matt.2.2, 11, NT.John.4.23, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 67.13; τῳ θεῷ 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 9.13.2}}. (Orig. perhaps **throw a kiss to** the god,{{Refs 4th c.AD+|compare 4th c.AD(?): Apuleius Scriptor Botanicus “Met.” 4.28: the gesture is probably represented in Sumerian and Babylonian art monuments.)}}
**II** later, **kiss**, σοῦ -ήσω τὴν χεῖρα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 423.15 (2nd c.AD)}}; τὸ πρόσωπον, τὴν ὄψιν, τοὺς πόδας, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1244.4 (4th c.AD), “PGiss.” 22.5 (2nd c.AD), “PGen.” 91.6 (6th-7th c.AD).}}
**II.2** **greet**, σπουδάζουσα -ῆσαί σε (by letter) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 615.8 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἔλθω πρὸς ὑμᾶς ἵνα ὑμᾶς -ήσω διὰ πολλοῦ χρόνου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLips.” 110.19 (3rd-4th c.AD),compare “PGiss.” 17.11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.3** **welcome respectfully, respect**, προσεκύνησά σου τὰ γράμματα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 237 vi 37 (2nd c.AD),compare “PTeb.” 286.22 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; τὴν θείαν ἀντιγραφὴν ὑπὸ πάντων προσκυνουμένην {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“OGI” 262.27 (Baetocaece, 3rd c.AD).}}

4355:
** προσλαμβάνω**,
_future_ **-λήψομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.13}}: _aorist_ προσέλᾰβον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 3.14.4}}: _perfect_ **-είληφα**{{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.6.32}}, _Ionic dialect_ **-λελάβηκα**{{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Eusebius Myndius Philosophus 51}}:—**take** or **receive besides** or **in addition, get over and above**, ἄρτον προσέλαβε (i.e. τῷ ὄψῳ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Mem.”}}[same place]; πρὸς τοῖς παροῦσιν ἄλλα [κακά] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 323; τὸ ἀναίσχυντον τῇ συμφορᾷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1145; π. αἰσχύνην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111; ἐμπειρίαν [prev. author] 6.18; ὧν μάλιστα δεόμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.23; δόξαν γελοίαν ἡμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.8; ἄλλην εὔκλειαν πρὸς ἐκείνοις [prev. author] “An.” 7.6.32}}; μισθόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.3.13; λόγον τῇ ἀληθεῖ δόξῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 207c; δωρειάς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.147; παιδείαν [prev. author] 61.42}}; παιδεύματα [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus] “fragment” 1120.4; ἃ μὴ μεμάθηκας προσλάμβανε ταῖς ἐπιστήμαις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.18; in tmesi, τοῦτο πρὸς ζητεῖς λαβεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 132; καιρούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 272a}}: _absolutely_, **make gains**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.7}}; **make progress**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 54.16:}} —_passive_, τὸ προσειλημμένον **what has been gained**, opposed to τὸ ἀπολειπόμενον, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.77c.}}
**2** **take in, add** an area to a building site, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 193.6 (3rd c.BC):—in Mathematics texts, τὸ ποτιλαμβανόμενον or ποτιλᾱφθὲν χωρίον 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “Spir.Praef.”}}; προσλαβών, **plus**, opposed to λιπών, minus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 3.12.}}
**2.b** προσλαβών, **multiplied by.. **, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.8.2:}} —_passive_, κοινοῦ -ληφθέντος λόγου if the ratio **be multiplied** into both, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 164.22.}}
**2.c** in Music, ὁ προσλαμβανόμενος [τόνος] the **added note** at the bottom of the scale, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.111, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1028f, etc.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _person_, **take to oneself as one's helper** or **partner**, κῆδος καινὸν καὶ ξυνασπιστὰς φίλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 378, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 219, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 885, “Hipp.” 1011; ἱππέας καὶ πελταστάς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.16; πόλεις τὰς μὲν βίᾳ τὰς δ᾽ ἑκούσας [prev. author] “HG” 4.1.1; τινὰς τῶν πολιτῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.14; τὸν δῆμον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1312b17}}; π. ἀδελφοὺς τοῖς παισί, by a second marriage, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.9: with a second _accusative_, π. τινὰ σύμμαχον [prev. author] “An.” 7.6.27, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.16}}:—_middle_, πόλεις προσλαβέσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.37.5; μισθοφόρους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 27}}; π. τινὰ συνεργόν, κοινωνόν, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PFay.” 12.10 (2nd c.BC), “PAmh.” 100.4 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; of **admitting** into the army, π. τὸν.. μου ἀδελφὸν.. εἰς τὴν Δεξειλάου σημέαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 14.21 (2nd c.BC)}}; προσλαβέσθαι γνώμην τινός **get** his vote **besides**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.70.2}}:—_passive_, -ληφθέντες εἰς τὴν κατοικίαν **admitted, enrolled,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 61 (a).2, compare31, +others (2nd c.BC).}}
**3.b** _middle_, **appropriate** neighbouring land, π. τῇ ἑαυτοῦ οἰκίᾳ ψιλοὺς τόπους {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 5954.5 (1st c.AD),compare “BGU” 1060.17 (1st c.BC).}}
**4** in Logic, **add by apposition**, ὅρους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 78a14, compare [prev. author] “APr.” 58b27 (_passive_)}}; **assume as minor premiss,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Stoic. 2.85, 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus “fragment” 1p.2H., 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.855S.}}; compare πρόσληψις.
**5** **borrow**, τι κερμάτιον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἥρως” 32; ἡ σελήνη φέγγος ἴδιον οὐκ ἔχει, ἀλλ᾽ ἀπὸ τοῦ ἡλίου προσλαμβάνει 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus “Ars astronomica” 11.15.}}
**II** **take hold of**, με π. κουφίσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1025 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. τὸν ἀγωγέα βραχύτερον shorten the rein, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 52}}:—_middle_, **take hold of**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1215f, “Lys.” 202; μικρᾶς ῥοπῆς ἔξωθεν δεῖται προσλαβέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556e.}}
**II.2** **fasten**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 78, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 670a14}}; καταδεῖν καὶ π. variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.2.2:}} —_passive_, δεσμοῖς π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 654b27, compare “HA” 497a22}}; **to be enveloped**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “ἀνατομή” 32.}}
**II.3** **lend a hand, help**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.11 and 12; π. τινί}}**help, assist,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.374.54, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 9 (_middle_)}}; τῆς ἀποκρίσεως ὑμῖν.. π. **help** you to find an answer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 897d}}; οἱ ποταμοὶ π. τῇ θαλάττῃ **co-operate with.. **, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.17, compare 11.4.2, 13.1.1:}} —_middle_, προσελάβετο τοῦ πάθεος he **was partly the author** of what befell, conjecture for -εβάλετο in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.90:}} — _passive_, π. ὑπό τινος **to be aided by.. **, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 58.16.}}
**III** προσείληφασιν **have learnt, believe**, ὅτι.. _falsa lectio_ for{προσυπ-} in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.141.}}

4356:
** πρόσ-ληψις**, εως, ἡ,
**taking in addition**, λόγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 210a, compare 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.11}}; ἀορίστου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.6, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “τέχναι ῥητορικαί” 1pp.473,534 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; τοῦ ἄρθρου, τοῦ ῑ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 170.3, “Pron.” 87.13.}}
**2** **acquisition**, δυνάμεως, τοῦ εὐδαίμονος, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.1.2, 18.9.6; μείζονος τιμῆς Anonymus “Trop.” 23.}}
**2.b** **rise in rank by acquisition of catoecic land**, προσλήψεως στέφανος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 61 (b). 254 (2nd c.BC).}}
**3** **enrolment**, εἰς τὸ ταγματικόν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTheb.Bank” 8.4 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** **additional assumption**, διὰ προσλήψεως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 58b9 (uncertain); κατὰ πρόσληψιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Analyticorum Priorum librum I commentarium - pars” 378.14, 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Analytica Priora commentaria - pars” 416.24; ἐνθύμημα γίνεται.. κατὰ πρόσληψιν Aps. “Rh.” p.288 H.: specifically, π., ἡ}}, **minor premiss**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Crinis “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.269, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.387c, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 250.21, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.149, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.82; ἐν προσλήψει 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 250.18.}}

4357:
** προσμένω**,
_Doric dialect_ **ποτι-** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 615.7 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}:— **bide, wait**, χρόνον πολλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.199, compare 5.19; σῖγ᾽ ἔχουσα πρόσμενε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1236, compare 1399}}; ἡσυχάζων π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 620}}; π. χρόνον ὀλίγον ἔστ᾽ ἂν.., π. ἕως.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.7.}}
**2** with _dative_, **remain attached to, cleave to**, πάθεα π. τοκεῦσιν {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 497 (Lyric poetry); τῷ Κυρίῳ NT.Act.11.23}}; π. ταῖς δεήσεσιν **continue in.. **, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.1Tim.5.5; ταῖς ἑαυτῶν ἀγωγαῖς 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.436.}}
**II** _transitive_, **wait for, await**, with _accusative_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1144, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 837, “El.” 164 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; **face** in battle, **stand one's ground against**, δορίκτυπον ἀλαλάν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.60}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_ _future_, Ὀρέστην τῶνδε προσμένουσ᾽ ἀεὶ παυστῆρ᾽ ἐφήξειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 303.}}

4359:
** προσοφείλω**,
**owe besides** or **still**, ἔτι πολλά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48; διακόσια τάλαντα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 15; π. σοι ἄλλας χάριτας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.16, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.31, 23.89, “IG” 22.1623.54; τὸ λοιπὸν ὃ προσοφείλεις μοι “PHib.” 1.63.14 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 150.17 (3rd c.BC), “OGI” 90.13 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}, etc.: _absolutely_, προσοφείλοντας ἡμᾶς ἐνέγραψεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.38 (divisim)}}:— _passive_, **to be still owing**, ὁ προσοφειλόμενος μισθός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.45, compare “SIG” 410.18 (Erythrae, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἡ ἔχθρη ἡ προσοφειλομένη ἐς Ἀθηναίους ἐκ τῶν Αἰγινητέων the hatred **which was still due** from the Aeginetans to the Athenians, i.e. their ancient feud, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.82 (variant for{προοφ-}).}}]
**II** **to be behindhand**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.8.6.}}

4360:
** προσοχθ-ίζω**,
_future_ -ιῶ (compare {προσόχθισμα}), **to be wroth with**, τινι {{LXX|LXX.Psa.94(95).10}}; οὐ μὴ προσοχθίσῃ ὑμῖν (variant{ὑμᾶς}) ἡ γῆ{{LXX+4th c.AD+|[prev. work = 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica”] LXX.Lev.20.22}}; προσώχθικα τῇ ζωῇ μου I **am weary** of..,{{LXX|LXX.Gen.27.46.}}
**2** _passive_, **to be treated with contumely**,{{LXX|LXX.2Ki.1.21.}}

4363:
** προσπίπτω**,
_Doric dialect_ **ποτιπίπτω** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1}}: _future_ **-πεσοῦμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 350: for ποτιπεπτηυῖαι}}, see at {προσπτήσσω}:—**fall upon, strike against**, ἔς τι variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 855}}; τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.6, etc.; πρός τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 800a2, +others}}; **fall against**, as a mound against a wall, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75}}; but πρὸς τὸ οὖς προσπίπτων is uncertain reading in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.10 (προσκύπτων conjecture Valckenaer).}}
**2** **fall upon, attack, assault**, πόλεσιν, ὁπλίταις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.3, etc.: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.30, 103, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.38.}}
**3** simply, **run to**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.4.}}
**4** **fall upon, embrace**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place], “IA” 1191: hence, π. τινί}} **join the party of** another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.42}}; also, **fasten on**, in argument, τῇ διαφορᾷ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 36.}}
**5** **fall in with, meet with, encounter**, μὴ λάθῃ με προσπεσών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 46, compare 156 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 270a: with _dative_ _of things_, [prev. author] “Tht.” 154b}}; **fall in with**, δυστυχεστάτῳ κλήρῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 291 (Lyric poetry); αἰσχρᾷ ἐπιθυμίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 30; μεγίσταις ἡδοναῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 637a}}; δήγματι to be bitten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 6.51: with _accusative_, μείζω βροτείας π. ὁμιλίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 19}}: with a _preposition_, πρὸς τὰς τῶν φυλάκων ψυχάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 906b}}; εἰς βράχεα, πρὸς τὰ κοινά, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.39.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.788c.}}
**II** of things,
**II.1** of events, accidents, etc., **come suddenly upon, befall** one, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 225, “IT” 1229 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 396d; τὰ προσπίπτονθ᾽ ἡμῖν δείματα [prev. author] “Lg.” 791c, etc.: _absolutely_, ἄτην προσπεσοῦσαν ἐνεῖκαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32}}; αἱ συμφοραὶ προσπίπτουσαι misfortunes **by befalling**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.46, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 5.4; αἱ π. χρεῖαι “PCair.Zen.” 31.7 (3rd c.BC)}}; αἱ π. τύχαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84; τὰ προσπεσόντα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 505; γενναίως φέρειν τὰ προσπίπτοντα 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.44 at Title Page; οἱ τὰ π. κρίναντες χρηματισταί “PPetr.” 3p.53 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἡ π. ἐπιθυμία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 561c}}; πρὸς τὰ προσπίπτοντα according to **circumstances**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286a11; οἱ προσπίπτοντες κίνδυνοι 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 117}}; τὰ π. εἰς τὸν ἀνθρώπινον βίον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Epit.” 43; ὅ τι ἂν προσπέσῃ ἰχθύδιον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 590a27, compare “PCair.Zen.” 186.15 (3rd c.BC)}}; προσπεσούσης μοι τῆς.. ἐπιστολῆς **when** the letter **came to hand,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PStrassb. 111.2 (3rd c.BC),compare “PPetr.” 3p.71 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 240.9 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** of expenses, **to be incurred**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.28, “PCair.Zen.” 60.3 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.3** of money, **to be paid in** to an account, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 701.9 (3rd c.BC), “PPetr.” 3p.290 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.4** of rights and duties, etc., **pass to, devolve** or **fall upon**, ὅταν λειτουργία προσπέσῃ ἀπολύειν αὐτούς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.78.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὥστε μηδεμίαν ὑποψίαν ἐκείνῳ γε προσπεσεῖν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.340.12 (3rd c.BC)}}; κληρονομίας -πεσούσης μοι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 340.9 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.5** **come to one's ears, be told as news**, εἴ τισιν ἀπιστότερος προσπέπτωκεν ὁ λόγος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.59, compare “PSI” 6.614.13 (3rd c.BC), “UPZ” 9.9 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.101.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 16, etc.; εἰς τὴν Ῥώμην 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.6.1: _impersonal_, προσέπεσε}} **news came** that.., with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 24.14.10, compare 31.14.8}}; προσπέπτωκεν Παῶν ἀναπλεῖν Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 10 (2nd c.BC)}}; προσπεσόντων τῷ βασιλεῖ περὶ τῶν γεγονότων {{LXX|LXX.2Mac.5.11.}}
**II.5.b** π. δι᾽ ἑαυτοῦ or αὐτόθεν **to be** self-**evident**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.168, “M.” 1.300; τὰ ποτιπίπτοντα ποτὶ τὰν αἴσθησιν 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 5, 41.}}
**II.6** **sit** or **fit closely**, of a bandage, προσπεπτωκός, opposed to χαλαρόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 5.}}
**II.7** Geometry texts, **meet**, πόλος πρὸς ὃν αἱ γραμμαὶ προσπίπτουσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 376a19, compare 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἑλίκων” 6}}; of lines, **to be drawn to meet**, πρὸς κύκλον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 3.37}}; π. ἐπὶ.. **pass through** a point, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἑλίκων” 14.}}
**II.8** of the pulse, ={ὑποπίπτω}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “Puls.” 6.2}}; of the womb, ἔνθα καὶ ἔνθα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 44, compare “Mul.” 2.125, +others}}
**III** **fall down at** another's **feet, prostrate oneself**, προσπίπτων προσκυνέει τὸν ἕτερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134; προσπεσὼν ἔχου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1181; ἱκέτης προσπίπτω 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.6.2: with _dative_, π. βωμοῖσι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 904, compare “OC” 1157; τινὸς γόνασι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1332, “Andr.” 860 (Lyric poetry), etc.; προσπεσὼν αὐτῷ.. ἱκέτευε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 349a; θεῶν πρὸς βρέτας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 31; πρὸς γόνυ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 79: also with _accusative_, π. βρέτη δαιμόνων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 94 (Lyric poetry)}}; compare προσπίτνω.

4364:
** προσποι-έω**,
**make over to, add** or **attach to**, π. τισὶ τὴν Κέρκυραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.55, compare 2.2, 3.70, etc.}}; Λέσβον π. τῇ πόλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.28, etc.; χάριν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.14, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.1.21.}}
**2** μνημείῳ κακὸν π. **do** damage **to,** {{Refs|MAMA 4.27.}}
**3** ={προσποιέομαι} 11.3, ὡς εἴη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 1.5.}}
**II** mostly in _middle_, with _aorist_ _passive_ in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus (see. below 5), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.46, 19.6}}:—**procure for oneself**, ξύλινον πόδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37}}; **include in one's purview**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.1.19, 6.3.8, 6.3.19}}: most frequently of persons, **attach to oneself, win** or **gain over**, ἑταιρηΐην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.71, compare 6.66, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.77, etc.; τὸν δῆμον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 215}}; [θεούς] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 6.3: c.double _accusative_, φίλους π. τινάς}} **as** friends, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.6; εὔνουν π. τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1387; ὑπηκόους τὰς πόλεις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8; π. χωρίον ἐς τὴν ξυμμαχίαν [prev. author] 2.30.}}
**II.2** **take to oneself what does not belong to one, pretend to, lay claim to**, with _accusative_, τὴν τῶν γεφυρῶν οὐ διάλυσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.137; φήμην 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.166; μείζω τῶν ὑπαρχόντων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1127b9: with _genitive_ _partitive_, π. τῶν χρημάτων}} **claim some of.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 871, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.3, 7.}}
**II.3** generally, **pretend, affect**, ὀργήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. ; τὸ δεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.24}}; π. ἔχθραν **use** it **as a pretence, allege**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.108; π. Ἀριστοτέλην 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pisc.” 50}}: c._accusative_ _participle_, προσποιούμενος τὸν ἡδόμενον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.531.}}
**II.4** with _infinitive_, **pretend** to do or to be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.2, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.2, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.13}}; ὅσοι πολιτικοὶ π. εἶναι **profess** to be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 519c, cf. Alcaeus1.108e, etc.; π. μὲν εἰδέναι, εἰδότες δὲ οὐδέν [prev. author] “Ap.” 23d}}, compare 26e; ὅρα μὴ τούτων μὲν ἐχθρὸς ᾖς, ἐμοὶ δὲ προσποιῇ (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.125}}; μὴ ἀποκτείνας π. (i.e. ἀποκτεῖναι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.75}}: _aorist_ _passive_, -ποιηθεὶς στρατεύειν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.6}}: with _infinitive_ _future_, **make as if one** would, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.20, etc.}}
**II.5** with a _negative_, **pretend the contrary**, δεῖ δέ, εἰ καὶ ἠδίκησαν, μὴ προσποιεῖσθαι one must **make as if it were** not **so**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.47, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 1.5; τούτων οὐ προσποιουμένων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.10; οὐδὲν πέπονθας δεινόν, ἂν μὴ προσποιῇ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Epit.fragment” 8, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 23}}: _aorist_ _passive_, σαφῶς εἰδὼς.., οὐ προσποιηθεὶς δέ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.25.7, compare 31.14.1.}}

4365:
** προσπορεύομαι**,
_Doric dialect_ **ποτιπορ-** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 338.15 (Rhodes, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}: — **go to, approach**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 625a13, compare “PSI” 4.403.16 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; **apply** for a loan, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMich.Zen.” 46.5 (3rd c.BC); for hire of a boat, [prev. work] 60.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; π. πρὸς τὰς προσοδικὰς κρίσεις **take up** revenue cases, of advocates, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PAmh.” 33.17 (2nd c.BC)}}; π. πρὸς ταῦτα καὶ πρὸς τὰ λοιπὰ μαθήματα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 60.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; π. πρὸς τὰ γενήματα **make a start** or **get on with** the crops, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 132.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; π. πρὸς τὴν ἀγορανομίαν **go in quest of** the office of aedile, **be candidate for** it, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.4.1}}; of a writer, π. πρὸς πᾶν παρὰ τὴν κοινὴν ἔννοιαν λεγόμενον **hunt for** paradoxes, {{Refs|[prev. author] 10.27.8: with _genitive_, π. τῶν μὴ καθηκόντων αὐτῷ}} **encroach on** what does not belong to him, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 69.7 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** of a certain day, **draw near, approach**, προσπορευομένης τῆς νουμηνίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1353b1.}}
**II** **attach oneself to**, ἀνδρὶ ἁμαρτωλῷ {{LXX|LXX.Sir.12.14}}; of proselytes,{{LXX|LXX.Num.1.51, +others, LXX.Lev.9.1 (8.35)}}; also ὅταν -πορεύωνται πρὸς τὸ θυσιαστήριον λειτουργεῖν{{LXX|LXX.Exo.30.20.}}
**III** of revenue, **come in**, {{Refs|“SIG” 344.112 (Antig.Epist.).}}

4366:
** προσρήγνῡμι**,
**dash** or **beat against**, παιδία πέτραις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 9.4.6.}}
**II** _intransitive_ in _active_, προσέρρηξεν ὁ ποταμὸς τῇ οἰκίᾳ {{NT|NT.Luke.6.48.}}
**III** **burst**, τὰς φλέβας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.2.}}

4367:
** προστάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **προστάττω ;** _Doric dialect_ **ποτιτάσσω** {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).155.91 (from Rhodes), Anonymus in “PSI” 9.1091.1}} also **ποιτάσσω** {{Refs|“IG” 42(1).122.39, +others (Epid.)}}: _perfect_ προστέταχα {{LXX|LXX.Dan.2.8}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|1}} _aorist_ προσετάχθην (see. below), also {{Refs|2}} _aorist_ προσετάγην{{LXX|LXX.Sir.3.22:}}
**I** with _accusative_ _person_,
**I.1** **place** or **post at** a place, χωρεῖτε οἷ προστάσσομεν (i.e. ὑμᾶς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1678}}: —_passive_, προσταχθέντα.. πύλαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 527, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 670; ᾗ ἄν τις προσταχθῇ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87, compare 7.70.}}
**I.2** **attach to,** πρὸς τοῖσι ἔθνεσι τοὺς πλησιοχώρους π. **attaching** to certain tribes their next neighbours, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.89}}; π. τινάς τινι **assign** them **to** his command, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.8, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.20}}:—_passive_, Ἰνδοὶ προσετετάχατο.. Φαρναζάθρῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.65; κατὰ τέλη στρατηγῷ τινι προστεταγμένοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.42.}}
**I.3** reversely, ἐπὶ μὲν τῇ [μοίρῃ] ἑωυτὸν π. **appointed** himself to the one part as their head, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94}}; π. ἄρχοντά τισι **appoint** as commander over them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.93: with _dative_ omitted, [prev. author] 3.16, 8.23:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 8.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **command, prescribe, enjoin,** περὶ βοηθείας ἢ ἄλλο τι προστάττοντες τῆσι πόλεσι {{Refs|“IG” 12.57.43}}; ἑκάστῳ ἔργον π., αὑτῷ πόνον π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1176, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.25; πολὺ ἔργον π. ὡς τηλικῷδε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 136d, etc.; πολλὰς ἐπιμελείας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1299b8; π. μνᾶς ἕξ}}**prescribe** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|6 minae, [prev. author] “EN” 1106b2; τισὶ περί τινος π. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.71:}} —_passive_, τοῖσι δὲ ἵππος προσετέτακτο to others **orders had been given** to supply cavalry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.21, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 208}}; τὰ προσταχθέντα **orders given**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.13; τὸ προστεταγμένον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.104; τὸ προσταχθέν [prev. author] 1.114, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1010; τὰ προσταχθησόμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.6: _absolutely_, προσταχθὲν αὐτῷ}} **the order having been given** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.2, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 50.12; πλείω τῶν ὑπὸ τῆς πόλεως προσταττομένων δαπανᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.13.}}
**II.2** with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **command, order** one to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.105, 9.99, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1018, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.6.3, “PEnteux.” 6.4 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; the _dative_ must be supplied in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 494, etc.}}:—_passive_, _impersonal_, ἐκέλευε τοῖσι προσετέτακτο ταῦτα πρήσσειν διαταμεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.39; ὁ βασιλεὺς.. ἢ ἄλλος τις οἷς προστέτακται περὶ τούτων “IG” 12.94.19.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 890}}; both usages in successive clauses, ὅσα οἱ νόμοι π. τοὺς προσήκοντας ποιεῖν, ἡμῖν π. καὶ ἀναγκάζουσι ποιεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.59}}:—_passive_, **to be ordered** to do, τέσσερες.. κῶμαι.. τοῖσι κυσὶ προσετετάχατο σιτία παρέχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.192, etc.}}; ὥσπερ προσετάχθησαν (i.e. ἐξεργάσασθαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.75.}}
**II.4** _absolutely_, **command, order**, opposed to ὑπηρετέω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 129a12}}:—_passive_, **receive orders**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 14; οἱ προστεταγμένοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.136.}}
**III** Astrology texts, _correlative_ of ἀκούω see, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 35, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus Astr. 1.9, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Serapio Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).226.}}

4369:
** προστίθημι**,
_Doric dialect_ **ποτι-**, also _aorist_ _infinitive_ ποιθέμεν {{Refs|“IG” 42(1).121.17 (Epid.)}}; ποτθέμειν probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170.8}}: late _present_ **προστιθῶ** Ps.- {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 18,27; _imperative_ προστίθει 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 83:}}_future_ προσθήσω: _aorist_ προσέθηκα, _plural_ -έθεμεν, _subjunctive_ προσθῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86}}, _Ionic dialect_ προσθέω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108}}:— _middle_, _future_ προσθήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Exo.14.13}}: _aorist 1_ προσεθηκάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.65: more frequently}}_aorist 2_ προσεθέμην, _subjunctive_ προσθῶμαι (not πρόσθωμαι), _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _optative_ προσθεῖτο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.12, but πρόσθοιτο [prev. author] 11.6}}; _Doric dialect_ _participle_ ποτθέμενος, Πρακτικά 1931.89 {{Refs|(from Dodona)}}: _perfect_ **-τέθειμαι**{{LXX|LXX.Deu.23.15}}:—_passive_, _aorist 1_ προσετέθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}: _future_ **-τεθήσομαι**{{LXX|LXX.Num.27.13, +others (-τεθήσεσθαι}} is _falsa lectio_ {{LXX|LXX.Exo.5.7): but the}}_perfect_ _passive_ is chiefly supplied by πρόσκειμαι:—**put to**, χερσὶν ἀπώσασθαι λίθον ὃν προσέθηκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.305}}; π. τὰς θύρας, τὴν θύραν, **put to, close** the door, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.13; τὰς πύλας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67}}; κλίμακας [τοῖς πύργοις] {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.23}}; κόμῃ προσθεῖσα βόστρυχον **holding** it **close** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 229; χέρα ἐλάτῃ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1110; γόνασιν ὠλένας [prev. author] “Andr.” 895, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 942; τοῖς καλλίστοις τοῦ ζῴου τὰ κάλλιστα φάρμακα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420c}}; π. μύωπας **apply** the spur, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.18.4; π. χεῖρ᾽ ἐπὶ πρόσωπα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1699}}; **apply** a pessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 32, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.62, +others}}; [κύαθον] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 890b24}}:—_passive_, of pessaries, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.76, +others, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.35, +others}}
**2** **hand over, deliver to**, θεῶν γέρα.. ἐφημέροισι προστίθει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 83, compare “hymnus ad Mercurium” 129}}; τινὶ γυναῖκα π. **give** her to him as wife, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.126}}; but π. γυναικὶ τάλαντον, as a dower, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 13; π. τινὰ ἄλλῳ πατρί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1545; Ἅιδῃ ἐμὸν δέμας [prev. author] “Hec.” 368, compare “IA” 540; π. τινὰ πυρί [prev. author] “Supp.” 948; σφαγέντα παῖδα π. πόλει [prev. author] “Ph.” 964; τισὶ π. πόλιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.86; τὴν διοίκησιν τῶν κοινῶν ἑαυτῷ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.14; also νᾶσον εὐκλέϊ π. λόγῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.68.}}
**3** **give besides** or **also**, φερνάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 628; προῖκα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.195; χρήματα [prev. author] 18.239, etc.; πίστιν ὑμῖν [prev. author] 54.42; τὰ ἴδια τοῖς ἀλλοτρίοις 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 557}}: _absolutely_, **spend money**, οὐ μόνον ἄνευ μισθοῦ, ἀλλὰ καὶ προστιθεὶς ἂν ἡδέως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3d, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1130a25, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 9.}}
**II** **impose upon**, πρῆγμα τὸ ἄν τοι προσθέω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, compare 3.62}}: with _infinitive_, π. τινὶ πρήσσειν {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.30}}; π. μέτρον **impose** measure or bounds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 796 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. τινὶ ἀτιμίην **impose, inflict** disgrace upon him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11}}; π. [φθόρον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 482; ἐπ᾽ ἐμαυτῷ ἀράς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 820; ὄκνον [prev. author] “Ant.” 243; αύτὸς αὑτῷ τὴν βλάβην [prev. author] “fragment” 350}}; λύπην, πόνους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 946, “Heracl.” 505; ἀναλώματα “IG” 14.830.12 (Puteoli, 2nd c.AD, _passive_)}}; π. τινὶ ἔκπληξιν ἀφασίαν τε strike him dumb with fear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 549; ἐνθύμιον τοῖς ζῶσι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.1.2; τισὶ ζημίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39}}; π. φιλανθρωπίαν εἰς τὰ τῆς πόλεως πράγματα **employ** it on.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.140.}}
**II.2** **attribute** or **impute to**, τῷ θεῷ τὴν αἰτίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1525, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39 (_passive_)}}; π. θράσος μοι **impute** boldness to me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 475; θεοῖσι π. ἀμαθίαν [prev. author] “Hipp.” 951; ἀπληστίαν λέχους γυναιξί [prev. author] “Andr.” 219; τὸ ἐμπλήκτως ὀξὺ ἀνδρὸς μοίρᾳ προσετέθη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82.}}
**III** **add**, τάδε τούτοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.20, +others; πρὸς [τῇ γνώμῃ] ἔργα [prev. author] 4.139}}; ἄλλον πρὸς ὦν ἔθηκαν χρυσόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 196; χάριτι χάριν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 327; νοσοῦντι νόσον [prev. author] Alcaeus1048; π. τῷ νόμῳ τὸν λόγον τόνδε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.35, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.136 (_passive_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 468b; προσθεῖναι τῷ δικαίῳ ἢ ὡς ἐλέγομεν (for πλέον ἤ..) [prev. work] 335a}}; ἄγγελλε δ᾽ ὅρκον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 47 (Reiske for ὅρκῳ codices,compare ὅρκου προστεθέντος “fragment” 472; ὀμόσας.. προσθείς τε χεῖρα δεξιάν “Ph.” 942); τὴν στήλην ὕστερον προσέθηκε “IG” 12.374.174; τοῖς εὖ ἔχουσιν ἔργοις οὔτ᾽ ἀφελεῖν ἔστιν οὔτε προσθεῖναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1106b11; ἐάν τι ἀφέλωμεν ἢ προσθῶμεν ἢ μεταθῶμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 432a; π. γράμματα [prev. work] 418a, compare 431c}}; also π. ἐπὶ τοῖσδε χάριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1253; ἵππον πρὸς τοὔνομα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 63; πρὸς τὸν μισθὸν ἑκάστῳ ὀβολόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 33c}}: _absolutely_, **make additions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45; πρὸς τὰ ὑπάρχοντα -τιθέντες πλουσιώτεροι γίνονται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1359b28}}; **make additions** to a story, **improve** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Po.” 1460a18}}; also of actors,{{Refs|[prev. work] 1461b30}}: especially of adding articles to statements or documents, προσθεῖναι οὐδὲν εἶχον τοῖς εἰρημένοις οὐδ᾽ ἀφελεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.264, compare “POxy.” 1062.4 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; π. καὶ ἀφελεῖν τι περὶ τῆς ξυμμαχίας Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.23, compare 29; π. τὶ πρὸς τοῖς ξυγκειμένοις Foed.[prev. work] 47}}; πρὸς τὰς συνθήκας Foed. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.43.27; π. ὅτι.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.231}}; of entries in accounts, προσετέθη τὰ τέλη τῷ κυριακῷ λόγῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 77.15 (2nd c.AD),compare “BGU” 620.15 (4th c.AD), etc.}}; π. τινὶ [ἀργύριον] **pay,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri Michegan 28.24 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 647.56 (3rd c.BC), “PRyl.” 153.27 (2nd c.AD)}}; πρόσθες εἰς ὄνομα Ἐπωνύχου **credit** to account of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, “Ostr.” 1159 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; **pay in, deposit** gold in a bank or mint, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 23.32 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III.2** with _accusative_ _person_, τίνα τῇδε προστιθῶ στάσε; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 114}}; Ἀθηναίοις π. σφᾶς αὐτούς **join** their party, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.92}}; π. ἑαυτόν τινι ἐς πίστιν, ἐπὶ ἰδίοις κέρδεσι, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.46, 50.}}
**III.3** in Mathematics texts, **add**, πὸτ ἀριθμόν.. φᾶφον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 170.8 (probably); [χωρίον] ἕτερον αὐτῷ τουτὶ ἴσον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 84d}}; πρὸς πεπερασμένον ἀεὶ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 266b2}}:—_passive_, εἴ κα.. ποτὶ τὸ ἕτερον τῶν βαρέων ποτιτεθῇ,.. ῥέπειν ἐπὶ τὸ βάρος ἐκεῖνο ᾧ ποτετέθη {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1 “Def.” 2, compare 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1 “Ax.” 2, etc.; κοινοῦ -τεθέντος 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 742.15.}}
**III.4** in Logic, **add some determining word**, opposed to ἀφαιρεῖν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 91b27, compare “EN” 1147b33.}}
**III.5** in LXX and **NT, continue** or **repeat** an action, with _infinitive_, προσέθηκεν ἔτι λαλῆσαι {{LXX|LXX.Gen.18.29; οὐ προσθήσω ἔτι πατάξαι [prev. work] 8.21}}; οὐ μὴ προσθῶ πεῖν I will not drink **again,** {{NT|NT.Mark.14.25 (variant)}}; also προσθεὶς Ἰὼβ εἶπεν Job **continued** and said, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Job.27.1; προσθεὶς εἶπε παραβολήν NT.Luke.19.11}}; προσθεῖσα ἔτεκεν υἱόν she bore **another** son, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.38.5}}:—also in _middle_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.}}
**B** _middle_, **side with** one, οἷς ἂν σὺ προσθῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1332, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11, 8.48, 87, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.12, 11.6, 52.25}}; τῷ ἀστῷ π. **to be favourable, wellinclined** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.160, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.34}}; τῇ ἡδονῇ **side with** pleasure, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1201a2}}: _absolutely_, **come in, submit**, Epist.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.39.}}
**B.2** **assent, agree**, οὔ οἱ ἔγωγε π. τῇ γνώμῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109, compare 3.83, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.10; τῷ λόγῳ τῷ λεχθέντι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.120; τῷ Καρχηδονίων νόμῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 674a}}: later with _infinitive_, **consent, bring onself to**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.1.8.}}
**B.3** φῆφον δ᾽ Ὀρέστῃ τήνδ᾽ ἐγὼ προσθήσομαι **will deposit** this vote **in favour of** Orestes, i.e. will vote in his favour, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 735; ἡμῖν ἂν προσθέμενοι τὴν φῆφον εὐορκοίητε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.69}}; so μὴ μιᾷ φήφῳ π. (i.e. τὴν γνώμην), ἀλλὰ δυοῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.20}}; φῆφον π. ἐναντίαν τινί{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 40; φῆφον π. ὥστε ἀποκτεῖναι “OGI” 218.102 (Ilium, 3rd c.BC).}}
**B.4** in Mathematics texts, **add,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Sammelb. 6951 ii 30, +others (2nd c.AD).}}
**B.II** with _accusative_ _person_, **associate with oneself**, i.e. **take to one as a friend, ally**, or **helper, win over**, π. τὸν δῆμον πρὸς τὴν ἑωυτοῦ μοῖραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.69, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18; εἰ στρατὸν προσθέοιτο φίλον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.53, compare 69, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 404}}; ταύτην προσθοῦ δάμαρτα **take** her to wife, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1224}}: also in bad sense, πολέμιον π. τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.12.}}
**B.II.2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **apply to oneself**, βάλανον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.a', compare 4.30 (_absolutely_, [prev. work] 1.26.δ᾽); ὀξύβαφον προσθοῦ λαβών 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 361; πατρὸς στέρνα προσθέσθαι θέλω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1408}}: _metaphorically_, **put on**, τῇ ὄφει ἀχθηδόνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37}}; **add to oneself, gain**, τί ἂν προσθείμην πλέο; what should I **be profited?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 40}}; π. χάριν, ={ἐπιχαρίζεσθαι}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 767}}; especially of evils, **bring** or **take upon onself**, πρὸς κακοῖσι κακόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 531; μέριμναν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1460; κακά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 146; ἄχθος ἐπ᾽ ἄχθει π. διπλοῦν [prev. author] “Andr.” 396}}; οἰκεῖον πόνον, κινδύνους αὐθαιρέτους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.78, 144}}; ἔχθρας ἑκουσίους πρὸς ταῖς ἀναγκαίαις π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 346b.}}
**B.II.2.b** **bring upon** others, οἱ.. πόλεμον προσεθήκαντο **made** war **upon** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.65}}; οὐκ ἄν σφι Σπαρτιήτας μῆνιν οὐδεμίαν προσθέσθαι **vented** any wrath upon.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.229.}}
**B.III** in LXX and **NT, continue** or **repeat** an action (compare above {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.5), οὐ προσθήσεσθε ἔτι ἰδεῖν αὐτούς LXX.Exo.14.13}}; προσέθετο πέμφαι ἕτερον {{NT|NT.Luke.20.11}}; προσέθετο συλλαβεῖν καὶ Πέτρον he **caused** Peter **also** to be arrested, {{LXX+NT|NT.Act.12.3; also Φαραὼ προσέθετο τοῦ ἁμαρτάνειν LXX.Exo.9.34}}; οὐ προσέθετο τοῦ ἐπιστρέψαι{{LXX|LXX.Gen.8.12.}}

4370:
** προστρέχω**,
_future_ **-δρᾰμοῦμαι**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.224}}:—**run to** or **towards, come to** one, πρὸς τοὺς νεκρούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 440a}}; τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1084, “Av.” 759, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.10, etc.}}: _absolutely_, **run up**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HG” 3.1.18, “Cyr.” 7.1.15, D.[same place], etc.}}
**2** in hostile sense, **make a sally**, πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.47.}}
**3** of things, **happen to** one, τινι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.37 codices}}; προστρέχει πολλαχοῦ τὸ γίγνεται” **occurs** frequently, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 401.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **join** or **side with**, [τῇ συγκλήτῳ] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.10.4, etc.; πρὸς τὴν τῶν πολλῶν γνώμην [prev. author] 28.7.8; πρὸς τὴν ἀλήθειαν [prev. author] 18.15.2.}}
**II.2** **approach**, -τρέχων τῇ ἐννόμῳ ἡλικίᾳ, i.e. not quite of age, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 247.12 (1st c.AD).}}
**II.3** **resemble**, with _dative_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 32.}}

4371:
** προσφάγιον** [ᾰ], τό,
(προσφάζω)
**B** **victim sacrificed beforehand,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(5).593.12 (Iulis, 5th c.BC).}}

4372:
** πρόσφᾰτος**, ον,
**fresh, not decomposed**, of a corpse miraculously preserved, νῦν δέ μοι ἑρσήεις καὶ π. ἐν μεγάροισιν κεῖσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.757}}; νεκρὸς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.89, 121}}.; τροφὴ ἔτι π. (i.e. before digestion begins) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 675b32}}; [ζῷα] τὰ πεπωκότα πόμα π. which have taken a **recent** drink, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “HA” 520b31; πορφύρας.. πρόσφατον τὸ ἄνθος ἔτι φυλαττούσης 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 36}}; of fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 218.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 462.4, “PMich.Zen.” 72.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐχῖνοι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 14}}; of poultry, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ λεπτυνούσης διαίτης - CMG” 8}}; [κρέα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”) , compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.15, +others; δέλεαρ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 534a12}}; ζῷα π., opposed to salted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.31, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.728; ἄλφιτα καὶ ἄλητα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.44}}, gloss on{ποταίνια} in {{Refs|“Acut.” 37}}; καρποί, ἔλαιον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 926a30, 927a29}}; ῥίζαι [σιλφίου] {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.3.5; σταφυλή LXX.Num.6.3, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.51; φῦκος 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 35; νάρδος 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.7; χιών 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.55.1; παγάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.299 (unless πρόσφατον ξενωθείς}} = **recently** entertained); ὕδωρ **newly-drawn** well-water, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.690c; ποτόν 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4}}; αἷμα **uncoagulated**, opposed to πεπηγότες θρόμβοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.10; [καταμηνίων ῥύσις] -ωτέρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 764a6}}; σπέρμα, οὖρον, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 924b28, 907b25.}}
**2** of events and actions, **recent**, δίκαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 804 (Lyric poetry); ἐπιστολαί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 128; ὀργή 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.19; ὀχεία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 509b31; φόβος 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 3.1; φθόνος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 24; θεωρίαι καὶ μαθήσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1237a24; φαντασία [prev. author] “MM” 1203b4}}; λύπη defined as δόξα πρόσφατος κακοῦ παρουσίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.52; ἀτύχημα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.21.9; εὐεργεσίαι [prev. author] 2.46.1}}; [πράγματα] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.146b}}; ὄγκοι (={οἰδήματα}) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).145}}; βήξ, i.e. not yet chronic, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.123, compare 2.46}}; γάλα, i.e. lately begun to be secreted, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.89}}; of persons, **recent in date**, of Homer, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 351b35}}; μάρτυρες.. οἱ μὲν παλαιοὶ οἱ δὲ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1375b27: used predicatively, χρόνοι [τοῖς πλουσίοις] τοῦ δίκην ὑποσχεῖν.. δίδονται, καὶ τἀδικήμαθ᾽ ἕωλα.. ὡς ὑμᾶς καὶ ψύχρ᾽ ἀφικνεῖται, τῶν δ᾽ ἄλλων ἡμῶν ἕκαστος π. κρίνεται}} the cases of us poor men are served up **fresh**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.112}}; νεαλὴς καὶ π. **fresh** (because recently imprisoned), {{Refs|[prev. author] 25.61.}}
**3** **new**, οὐκ ἔστι πᾶν π. ὑπὸ τὸν ἥλιον {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.1.9}}; οὐκ ἔσται ἐν σοὶ θεὸς π.{{LXX+NT+4th c.AD+|LXX.Psa.80(81).10; ὁδὸν π. καὶ ζῶσαν NT.Heb.10.20; ἀεὶ ἡδίων ἡ π. ἀφροδίτη 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.39.}}
**II** _adverb_ -τως **newly, lately**, π. ἠγγελμένων [{{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 16.2, cf. LXX.Deu.24.5, “OGI” 315.23 (Pessinus, 2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 28.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.115, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.6.2, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 30.3, Anon.Hist. (“FGrH” 153) p.826 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus (Orig. ={νεωστὶ ἀνῃρημένος} (compare πέφαται, etc.) _accusative_ to 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}]; perhaps **slaughtered for** (the occasion).)

4373:
Included with: ** πρόσφᾰτος**, ον,
**fresh, not decomposed**, of a corpse miraculously preserved, νῦν δέ μοι ἑρσήεις καὶ π. ἐν μεγάροισιν κεῖσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.757}}; νεκρὸς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.89, 121}}.; τροφὴ ἔτι π. (i.e. before digestion begins) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 675b32}}; [ζῷα] τὰ πεπωκότα πόμα π. which have taken a **recent** drink, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “HA” 520b31; πορφύρας.. πρόσφατον τὸ ἄνθος ἔτι φυλαττούσης 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 36}}; of fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 218.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 462.4, “PMich.Zen.” 72.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐχῖνοι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 14}}; of poultry, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ λεπτυνούσης διαίτης - CMG” 8}}; [κρέα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”) , compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.15, +others; δέλεαρ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 534a12}}; ζῷα π., opposed to salted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.31, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.728; ἄλφιτα καὶ ἄλητα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.44}}, gloss on{ποταίνια} in {{Refs|“Acut.” 37}}; καρποί, ἔλαιον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 926a30, 927a29}}; ῥίζαι [σιλφίου] {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.3.5; σταφυλή LXX.Num.6.3, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.51; φῦκος 2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 35; νάρδος 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.7; χιών 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.55.1; παγάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.299 (unless πρόσφατον ξενωθείς}} = **recently** entertained); ὕδωρ **newly-drawn** well-water, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.690c; ποτόν 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4}}; αἷμα **uncoagulated**, opposed to πεπηγότες θρόμβοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.10; [καταμηνίων ῥύσις] -ωτέρα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 764a6}}; σπέρμα, οὖρον, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 924b28, 907b25.}}
**2** of events and actions, **recent**, δίκαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 804 (Lyric poetry); ἐπιστολαί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 128; ὀργή 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 18.19; ὀχεία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 509b31; φόβος 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 3.1; φθόνος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 24; θεωρίαι καὶ μαθήσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Eudemia” 1237a24; φαντασία [prev. author] “MM” 1203b4}}; λύπη defined as δόξα πρόσφατος κακοῦ παρουσίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.52; ἀτύχημα 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.21.9; εὐεργεσίαι [prev. author] 2.46.1}}; [πράγματα] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.146b}}; ὄγκοι (={οἰδήματα}) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).145}}; βήξ, i.e. not yet chronic, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.123, compare 2.46}}; γάλα, i.e. lately begun to be secreted, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.89}}; of persons, **recent in date**, of Homer, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 351b35}}; μάρτυρες.. οἱ μὲν παλαιοὶ οἱ δὲ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1375b27: used predicatively, χρόνοι [τοῖς πλουσίοις] τοῦ δίκην ὑποσχεῖν.. δίδονται, καὶ τἀδικήμαθ᾽ ἕωλα.. ὡς ὑμᾶς καὶ ψύχρ᾽ ἀφικνεῖται, τῶν δ᾽ ἄλλων ἡμῶν ἕκαστος π. κρίνεται}} the cases of us poor men are served up **fresh**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.112}}; νεαλὴς καὶ π. **fresh** (because recently imprisoned), {{Refs|[prev. author] 25.61.}}
**3** **new**, οὐκ ἔστι πᾶν π. ὑπὸ τὸν ἥλιον {{LXX|LXX.Eccl.1.9}}; οὐκ ἔσται ἐν σοὶ θεὸς π.{{LXX+NT+4th c.AD+|LXX.Psa.80(81).10; ὁδὸν π. καὶ ζῶσαν NT.Heb.10.20; ἀεὶ ἡδίων ἡ π. ἀφροδίτη 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.39.}}
**II** _adverb_ -τως **newly, lately**, π. ἠγγελμένων [{{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 16.2, cf. LXX.Deu.24.5, “OGI” 315.23 (Pessinus, 2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 28.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.115, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.6.2, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 30.3, Anon.Hist. (“FGrH” 153) p.826 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus (Orig. ={νεωστὶ ἀνῃρημένος} (compare πέφαται, etc.) _accusative_ to 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}]; perhaps **slaughtered for** (the occasion).)

4374:
** προσφέρω**
(once ποσφέρω, which see), _Doric dialect_ **ποτιφέρω** Prov. cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.239a}}: _future_
**A** προσοίσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 257}}: _Ionic dialect_ _aorist_ προσένεικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.87}}: _Ionic dialect_ _aorist_ _passive_ προσηνείχθην {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.71}}:—**bring to** or **upon, apply to**, π. πύργοισι κλιμάκων προσαμβάσεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 488; πῦρ σοι [prev. author] “Andr.” 257}}; μηχανὰς [τοῖσι τείχεσι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.18, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.58 (and so _metaphorically_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125 (unless in signification 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2)}}; π. νόμον, ψήφισμα πρὸς τὴν συγγραφήν **bring to bear** against.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.39); τὴν χεῖρα πρὸς τοὺς μυκτῆρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.87}}; but χέρα τινὶ προσενεγκεῖν **lay** hands **upon.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.36}}; π. τὰς χεῖρας αὐτοῖς, in hostile sense, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.79.4, compare “PCair.Zen.” 18.8 (3rd c.BC), “PPetr.” 2p.10 (3rd c.BC)}} (but also in a friendly relation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.31}} {προσφεύγω}, and in supplication to the gods, **hold out** one's hands **to**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 106.12, 107.14 (2nd c.BC))}}; ἀνάγκην or ἀναγκαίην τισὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.136, 172, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 76 (Lyric poetry); βίην τισί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.19; τινὶ βάσανον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 23a}}; so of surgical or medical treatment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑλκῶν” 24}}; πταρμὸν [τῇ λυγγί] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 189a}} (_passive_){{Refs 2nd c.AD+|. compare 187e; τὰς τομὰς καὶ τὰς καύσεις τινί 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 55.17}}; κλύδωνα σαυτῷ αὐθαίρετον **bring upon** thyself, {{Refs|“Tragica Adespota” 568}}: without _dative_, **apply, employ, use**, καινὰ σοφά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 298, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1130 (compare below 3); ἴαμα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51; τεχνήματα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 322; πάσας μηχανάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 112; πάντας ἐλέγχους 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 484}}; π. τόλμαν **bring** it **to bear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.30; also π. πόλεμον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.γ (variant)}}; ἔρωτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 187e.}}
**A.2** **add**, μηδὲ π. μέθυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 481 (or in signification 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.3a); εἰ κακὸν προσοίσομεν νέον παλαιῷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 78}} (or perhaps, **bear in addition**); π. τι πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125 (or in signification 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.1).}}
**A.3** **present, offer**, ἄεθλον, of a triumphal ode, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.108; λουτρὰ πατρί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 434}}; [τόξα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 775; τὴν δᾷδά τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1052; τὴν χεῖρά τινι ἄκραν [prev. author] “Lys.” 436; δῶρα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97 (_passive_)}}; οὐθὲν κολοβὸν προσφέρομεν πρὸς τοὺς θεούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 101}}; οἶνον μὴ π. {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 696 (from Chios)}}; σφάγια καὶ θυσίας {{LXX+NT|LXX.Amos.5.25, +others, cf. NT.Heb.11.4; τὸ δῶρόν σου NT.Matt.5.24, etc.}}
**A.3.b** especially of food, drink, or medicine, θαλλὸν χιμαίραις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 502; π. τὰ ῥυφήματα καὶ τὰ πόματα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 26, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 270b, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 55, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 189, etc.; π. τὸ φάρμακον τῇ κεφαλῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 157c}}; ἑαυτῷ π φάρμακον **administer** poison to oneself, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 472.6 (2nd c.AD)}}; **set** food **before** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.13 and 14, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 792a}}: with _infinitive_, π. τινὶ ἐμπιεῖν καὶ φαγεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.1; also διψῶντι γάρ τοι πάντα προσφέρων σοφὰ οὐκ ἂν πλέον τέρψειας ἢ᾽ μπιεῖν διδούς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 763; χυμὸς ἐπιτήδειος προσφέρειν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 24; ὁ προσφέρων [prev. author] “Epid.” 1.23:}} —_passive_, τὰ προσφερόμενα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. passage], 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.2; ἡ προσφερομένη τροφή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 230c.}}
**A.4** **address** proposals, an offer, etc., π. λόγον τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 5.30, compare 40; περὶ σπονδῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.109; ὅτι.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.6; λόγους π. τισί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.4; λόγους π. περὶ ξυμβάσεως τοῖς στρατηγοῖς [prev. author] 2.70, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.52; λόγους τισὶ ξυναποστῆναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.57.}}
**A.5** **convey** property **by deed of gift** or **by bequest,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Arch.Papyrus 4.130 (2nd c.AD)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.71.6 (2nd c.AD).}}
**A.II** **contribute, pay**, ἑκατὸν τάλαντα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.91, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138}}; π. μετοίκιον **pay** an alientax, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 2.1, compare “OGI” 13.20 (Samos, 4th c.BC, _middle_), “PGiss.” 50.12 (3rd c.AD)}}; **bring in, yield**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.15, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.9.}}
**A.III** _intransitive_, **resemble**, with _accusative_ of respect in which, π. νόον ἀθανάτοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.4; θηρὸς χρωτὶ νόον προσφέρων [prev. author] “fragment” 43; π. τρόπους παιδί “Tragica Adespota” 453; compare below 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.5.}}
**A.IV** **bear in addition**, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2.}}
**B** _passive_, with _future_ προσοίσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.44, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.22:}}_aorist_ προσηνεγκάμην, ={προσηνέχθην}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.8}}:—**to be borne towards**, and of ships, **put in**, εἰς λιμένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.6: hence}}
**B.2** **attack. assault**, πρός τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34, 111, 112, 7.209, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.20, etc.}}; τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.109, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126, etc.}}; κατὰ τὸ ἰσχυρότατον προσηνείχθησαν **attacked** where the enemy was strongest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.71, compare 5.101, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 422b}}; προσφέρεσθαι ἄποροι difficult **to engage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.49, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 223b.}}
**B.3** without any sense of hostility, **go to** or **towards, approach**, ἐκ τοῦ Ἰκαρίου πελάγεος προσφερόμενοι **sailing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.96; π. τοῖσι Κορινθίοισι [prev. author] 8.94}}; τῷ σκοπέλῳ, τῇ Τρῳάδι, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 15, “DMort.” 19.2; πόλεμος ἀπὸ Πελοποννήσου -φερόμενος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 8}}; τὰ -όμενα πρήγματα matters **that were brought** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.173.}}
**B.4** **deal with, behave oneself** in a certain way **towards** a person, ἀπὸ τοῦ ἴσου ὑμῖν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140; τοῖς κρείσσοσι καλῶς [prev. author] 5.111, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.16; τισὶν οὐ μετρίως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.24, compare “PTeb.” 750.2 (2nd c.BC), “Sammelb.” 5675.6 (2nd c.BC)}}; φιλανθρώπως [τῇ Ποτειδαία] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.8, compare “SIG” 807.13 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae.)}}; ὀρθότατα ἵπποις π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.1; also τίνα τρόπον προσφέρῃ πρὸς τὰ παιδικά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 205b, compare “Phdr.” 252d; ἄριστα π. πρὸς τοὺς ἀμφισβητοῦντας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.22}}; also of circumstances, ταῖς ξυμφοραῖς εὐξυνετώτερον **meet** them with intelligence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.18}}; πρὸς τὰ πράγματα ἄριστα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.44; πρὸς τὰς τύχας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604d}}; πρὸς λόγον **answer** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.44: _absolutely_, χρησμῳδέων π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; ὀλιγώρως π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.17.}}
**B.5** προσφέρεσθαί τινι **come near, be like**, ὁ χαρακτὴρ τοῦ προσώπου προσφέρεσθαι ἐδόκεε ἐς ἑωυτόν [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116; compare above 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111, and see at {προσφερής}.}}]
**B.II** **to be added**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor “Prolegomena ad Hephaestionis Euchiridion” p.89C.}}
**C** _middle_, with _future_ **-οίσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 8}}: _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _aorist 1_ _subjunctive_ **-ενέγκηται**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.64U.:}} —προσφέρεσθαί τι **take**, of food or drink, **assimilate**, π. σῖτον, ποτόν. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.41, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.145, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.4.5, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus [same place], 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 30, “Cic.” 3, etc.}}
**C.2** **exhibit**, ὑμῖν φιλοτιμίαν Epist.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.167, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.14U., “Inscription Prien.” 42.14, 108.221 (2nd c.BC), etc.; also π. ἑαυτόν [prev. work]111.294 (1st c.BC).}}
**C.3** like the _active_, **apply**, κἂν ὁτιοῦν δουλείας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 563d; πᾶσαν σπουδὴν καὶ μηχανήν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.18.11, compare “Supp.epigram” 2.663.5 (Prusa, 2nd c.BC), “PTeb.” 27.14, +others (2nd c.BC).}}
**C.4** **contribute**, πλεῖστα πρός τι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Athenio Comicus 1.2 (assuming variant)}}; **bring with one** as dowry, εἱματισμὸν καὶ κόσμον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“PEleph.” 1.4 (4th c.BC),compare “PGiss.” 2.12 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; comparesupr.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.}}
**C.5** **declare**, μὴ εἰδέναι γράμματα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PHamb.” 39.63 (2nd c.AD),compare “POxy.” 237 vii 26 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**C.6** ={προσορίζω}, **add** land **by deed of conveyance**, κυρία ἔσται -ομένη πρὸς πόλιν ἣν ἂν βούληται {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 225.10 (Didyma, 3rd c.BC), compare 221.44 (Ilium, 3rd c.BC), +others}}
**C.7** **convey** property, π. ἐν προσφορᾷ [μέρος οἰκίας] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 155.7 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}

4375:
** προσφιλ-ής**, ές,
(φιλέω) **dear, beloved**, τῶν ἡλίκων.. προσφιλεστάτῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.40}}: with _dative_, **dear** or **friendly to.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.163, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 78, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 507e, etc.}}: of things, **pleasing, agreeable**, ἔργον θεοῖσι π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 580}}; στολή, χάρις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 224 (_superlative_), 558}}; πάσαις ἡλικίαις.. ἡ χρῆσις αὐτῆς (i.e. τῆς μουσικῆς) ἐστὶ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1340a5; π. ἑκάστῳ.. τὸ κατὰ φύσιν [prev. author] “HA” 590a10; τῇ αἰσθήσει 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 45}}; also of actions, **lovely**, ὅσα π. {{NT|NT.Phil.4.8.}} _adverb_ -λῶς **agreeably**, with _dative_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 331.9 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC).}}
**II** _active_, of persons, **kindly affectioned, well-disposed**, ὥς μ᾽ ἔθεσθε προσφιλῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 532, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.92, 7.86.}} _adverb_ -λῶς **kindly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 442, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 822b}}; π. ἡμῖν ἔχειν to be **kindly affectioned** to us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.44; π. χρῆσθαί [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.3.16}}: _comparative_ **-έστερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 248d}}: _superlative_ **-έστατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 1.1}}, **-εστάτως** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator (assuming variant) cited in 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.63: poetry προσφιλέως “IG” 9(1).235 (from Larymna).}}

4376:
** προσφορ-ά**, ἡ,
(προσφέρω) **bringing to, applying**, τῶν κλιμάκων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.16.7}}; **application, use**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 638c.}}
**2** **presenting, offering**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 792a}}; οἱ ἄρτοι τῆς π. **shew**bread, {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.7.48.}}
**II** (from _passive_) **that which is added, increase**, τῶν ἡμαρτημένων ἄκη μέν ἐστι π. δ᾽ οὐκ ἔστ᾽ ἔτι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1270}}; **bounty, benefit**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 581}}; wedding **present**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 30.19}}; **offering**, {{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Psa.39(40).7, +others, NT.Act.21.26: _plural_, [prev. work] 24.17, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.4.1, “Stud.Pal.” 1.7.27 (5th c.AD), etc.}}; **offerings** for the dead, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMonac.” 8.5, 23 (6th c.AD), “PLond.” 5.1708.62 (6th c.AD)}}; **deed of gift**, especially of **donatio propter nuptias**, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 288.8 (2nd c.AD), “PTeb.” 351.1 (2nd c.AD), “PRyl.” 155.20 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** **income, revenue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator “fragment” 31, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 19.8.2.}}
**III** (from _middle_) **taking** of food, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Somno et Vigilia” 458a22, “Metaphorical” 1000a14, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.9.4, 8.4.4, “Od.” 5}}; ἡ τοῦ ὑγροῦ π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 671a13; πόσεις καὶ -φοραί 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.129e.}}
**III.2** **food, victuals**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.33 (_plural_), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.9.6, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 49.87.}}
**III.3** **flavour**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.8.11, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.33f}}; **bouquet**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.19.1.}}

4377:
** προσφων-έω**,
**call** or **speak to, address**, _present_ first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 236: used by 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _imperfect_, the _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ being προσέειπον, τινα {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.69: _absolutely_, 5.159, 10.109, +others: later with _dative_ _person_, LXX.1Esd.2.21, Antig.Rex cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.7, NT.Matt.11.16, NT.Act.22.2}}: with _double_ _accusative_, **address** words **to** a person, οὐδέ τί μιν προσεφώνεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.332, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 159, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 664.}}
**2** **call by name**, ποδαπὸν ὅμιλον τόνδε.. προσφωνοῦμε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supp.” [same place]; ὀνόματι π. τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 942}}; π. τινὰ βασιλέα **salute** him king, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.38.3, etc.}}
**3** **issue directions** or **orders**, τινι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.109 (2nd c.BC): _absolutely_, followed by ὅπως, [prev. work] 124.21 (2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 106.20 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** with _accusative_ _of things_, **pronounce, utter**, τήνδε π. φάτιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1213; τὰ ὑπογεγραμμένα “IG” 7.2713.7 (Acraeph., 1st c.AD)}}; **address** or **dedicate** a book, τινι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 16.11.4, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 1, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.313f, etc.}}
**II.2** **make a report,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 475,476 (2nd c.AD)}}; περί τινος Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 27.15 (2nd c.AD): _absolutely_, “OGI” 572.43 (Myra, 2nd-3rd c.AD).}}

4383:
** πρόσωπον**, τό:
_plural_ πρόσωπα, _Epic dialect_ (_Aeolic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.212)}} προσώπατα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.192, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.230 (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus), 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.419, etc.; _dative_ προσώπασι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.212: a _masculine_ _nominative_ πρόσωπος is cited from 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 250}}:— **face, countenance** (compare μέτωπον), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always in _plural_, even of a single person, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.212, 18.414, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.361, +others (except 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.24), and so in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 594 (variant{-πον}), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 871.6 (see.below), “El.” 1277 (Lyric poetry), “OC” 314, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.11 (uncertain), “Anthologia Graeca” 9.322 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus): _sing._ in “Hymni Homerici” 10.2, 31.12}}], and usually in later writers; π. κλιθὲν προσώπῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.12; εἰς π. βλέπειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 280}}; ἐς π. τινὸς ἀφικέσθαι come before him,{{LXX+2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 720; π. πρός τινα στρέφειν [prev. author] “Ph.” 457; οὐκ ὄψεσθε τὸ π. μου LXX.Gen.43.3, compare “UPZ” 70.5 (2nd c.BC)}}; κατὰ πρόσωπον **in front, facing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.106, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.43, etc.; τὴν κατὰ π. τῆς ἀντίας φάλαγγος τάξιν [prev. work] 6.3.35}}; κατὰ π. Αἰγύπτου **facing, fronting** Egypt, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.25.18}}; opposed to κατὰ νώτου, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.28.9}}; κατὰ π. ἄγειν, opposed to κατὰ κέρας ὑπεραίρειν, {{Refs|[prev. author] 11.14.6, etc.}}; κατὰ π. **in person**, ἡ κατὰ π. ἔντευξις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 17}}; κατὰ π. παραμυθήσασθαι, opposed to διὰ τοῦ ψαφίσματος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).86.22 (Epid.); so κατὰ πρόσωπα 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus “Ars astronomica” 11.21}}; also πρὸς τὸ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.9}}; ἐπὶ προσώπου Ἰεριχώ in **front** of Jericho, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Deu.34.1; ἔρρ᾽ ἐκ προσώπου 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 8.59; ἀπὸ π. τῆς γῆς LXX.Amos.9.8}}; βλέπειν εἰς π. τινός regard his **countenance,** {{LXX+NT|NT.Matt.22.16: usually of the face of man or God, as λειτουργῶν τῷ π. Κυρίου LXX.1Ki.2.11; οἱ ἄρτοι τοῦ π., of shewbread, [prev. work] 21.6}}: of the ibis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.76}}; of dogs, ἀπὸ τῶν π. φαιδραί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.2}}; of horses, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 631a5}}; of deer,{{Refs|[prev. work] 579a2}}; of fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 30, 33.16}}; **face** of the moon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 871.6 (_plural_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.920b: _metaphorically_, ἀργυρωθεῖσαι πρόσωπα.. ἀοιδαί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.8.}}
**2** **front, façade**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “P.” 6.14, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 189 (Lyric poetry, _plural_)}}; κατὰ π. τοῦ ἱεροῦ, τῆς νεώς, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.2 (3rd c.BC), 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 3.1, 2; τιθέναι τὰς φιάλας ἐπὶ πρόσωπον 2nd-1st c.BC: Asclepiades Myrleanus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.501d.}}
**II** **one's look, countenance**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 639, 794 (_anapaest meter_, _plural_), “Eu.” 990 (_anapaest meter_, _plural_), etc.}}; οὐ τὸ σὸν δείσας π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448: _metaphorically_, φαίνοισα π. Ἀλάθεια 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.17.}}
**II.2** Astrology texts, decan considered as the **domain** of a planet, ἐν ἰδίοις π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 62.21, Paul. Al. “C.” 2.}}
**III** ={προσωπεῖον}, **mask**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.287 (-εῖον is variant), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1449a36, b4, “Pr.” 958a17, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.144 (variant), 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.47; π. ὑπάργυρον κατάχρυσον “IG” 12.276.6, compare 42(1).102.58,68 (Epid., 4th c.BC), “Clara Rhodos” 6/7.428}}; ὀθόνινον π. probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 142; π. περίθετον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristomenes Comicus 5}}; of the Roman **imagines**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.53.5}}; **bust** or **portrait,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Sammelb. 5221, “OGI” 432.1 (Naksh-i-Rustam, 3rd c.AD).}}
**III.2** **dramatic part, character**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.199S., 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.29.45 and 57}}; κωφὸν π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.19.3}}; **character** in a book, τὸ τῆς Ἑλλάδος ὄνομα καὶ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.11.5}}; τὸ τοῦ Ὀδυσσέως π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 12.27.10, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.32}}; also ἀστοχεῖν τοῦ π., of an author,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Callisth. 44J.; ἐπὶ προσχήματι καὶ π. δικαστῶν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 168.}}
**IV** **person**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.52S. (_plural_); ἀδίκως μὴ κρῖνε πρόσωπον 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 10}}; προσώπῳ, οὐ καρδίᾳ **in person, in bodily presence**, {{NT|NT.1Thes.2.17, cf. NT.2Cor.5.12}}; ποιεῖν or πληροῦν τὸ π. τινός to represent a **person,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|PRein. 56.30 (4th c.AD), “Sammelb.” 6000ii 12 (6th c.AD)}}; λαμβάνειν π. τινός admit a **person** to one's presence, εἰ προσδέξεταί σε, εἰ λήψεται πρόσωπόν σου {{LXX|LXX.Mal.1.8}}; hence, ={προσωποληπτεῖν}, {{NT|NT.Luke.20.21, NT.Gal.2.6}}; μὴ ἀποστρέψῃς τὸ π. μου, i.e. do not reject my prayer, {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.2.20}}; θαυμάσαι π. ἀσεβοῦς{{LXX|LXX.Pro.18.5}}; ὁ θεὸς ὁ μέγας.., ὅστις οὐ θαυμάζει π. οὐδὲ οὐ μὴ λάβῃ δῶρον{{LXX|LXX.Deu.10.17.}}
**IV.2** **legal personality**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus Borysth. cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 4.46.}}
**IV.3** Grammars, **person**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 638.4, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 3.12, etc.}}; γυναικεῖα π. {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 2.}}
**IV.4** π. πόλεως a **feature** of the city, of a person, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Familiares” 15.17.2.}}
**IV.5** _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.23 (cf. Nicolaus Rhetor “Progymnasmata” p.4 F.).}}

4384:
** προτάσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **προτάττω**, 2 _aorist_ _passive_ προετάγην [ᾰ] {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 306.16}}:— **place** or **post in front**, σφᾶς αὐτοὺς π. πρὸ τῶν Ἑλλήνων **put** themselves **in front of** them, so as to defend them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.107}}; π. σφῶν αὐτῶν Ἀστύμαχον **put** him **at their head**, as speaker, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.52:}} —_middle_, προετάξαντο τῆς φάλαγγος τοὺς ἱππέας **posted** the horse **in front** of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.10}}:—_passive_, **stand before** one, so as to protect, ἄναξ, προτάσσου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 835 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸ προταχθέν **the front rank, van**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.3.37; οἱ προτεταγμένοι [prev. author] “HG” 2.4.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1340; προταχθέντες ὑπὲρ ἁπάντων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.99}}; also in documents, ὁ -τεταγμένος the **aforesaid,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 1112.18 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** **class as prior**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.1.25}}; **prefer**, τί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 546.}}
**3** Grammars, **prefix**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 631.7 (_passive_), etc.}}; προτάσσεται τῷ ῥήματι καὶ ὑποτάσσεται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 116.6.}}
**II** **appoint** or **determine beforehand**, χρόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 164; ἆθλον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 956b17, 20 (_active_ and _passive_)}}:—_passive_, προτεταγμένοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 1.5.}}
**III** _middle_, **set before oneself, take as an example**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 218e}}; **propose to oneself**, ἐκ τούτου τὸ ζῆν {{Refs|[prev. work] 224d.}}

4385:
** προτείνω**,
**stretch out before, hold before**, τὸν χαλινόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 6.11 (_passive_)}}; [ὁ ναυτίλος] π. τὰς πλεκτάνας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 525a28.}}
**2** **expose to danger**, ψυχὴν.. προτείνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1270.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **hold out** as a pretext or excuse, π. πρόφασιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.156; σκῆψιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1067; θεούς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 992; παιδὸς θάνατον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 428}}:—_middle_, π. τὴν ἡλικίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 317c.}}
**II** **stretch forth, hold out**, χεῖρα, as a suppliant, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 130; τὰς χεῖρας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.45, 7.233 (for punishment, 3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 2.2); φύλλον οἱ ἱκέται προτείνουσι 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.275a (προτιμῶσι 275)}}; also προτείνει χεὶρ ἐκ χερὸς ὀρέγματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus for ὀρεγόμενα) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1110 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. ἑαυτόν **leaning forward**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 449b}}: hence _intransitive_, **stretch forward**, προτείνουσα εἰς τὸ πέλαγος [ἄκρα] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Criti.” 111a, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.29.2, etc.}}
**II.2** π. χεῖρα δεξιάν **offer, tender** it as a pledge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1292, compare “Tr.” 1184, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1118, etc.; π. πίστιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.117.}}
**II.3** **hold out, offer**, μεγάλα π. ἐπ᾽ οἷσι ὁμολογέειν ἐθέλουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140. ; κέρδος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 777; τελετάς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 238; κάλλος [prev. author] “Hel.” 28; φάντασμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 382a; ἐλπίδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 131; δραχμὰς εἴκοσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1019; ἐλευθερίαν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.50; δέλεαρ π. τὴν ἡδονήν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2 13a; ἐμοὶ λόγους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 230d}}: with _infinitive_, π. τινὶ λαβεῖν ὅ τι χρῄζει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.8}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24, 7.161; ἔρωτα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 266b; φιλίαν προτενεῖται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.5; τὴν ἀειλογίαν [prev. author] 19.2}}:—_passive_, δυοῖν προτεινομένοιν ἀγαθοῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.37, compare 12.117.}}
**II.4** **put forward, propose**, π. ζητήματα, ἐρωτήματα, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.737d, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.8.1; αἴνιγμά τινι 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.70, etc.}}:—_middle_, **offer** or **put forward** as instances, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 518b}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.1, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.3.}}
**II.5** Med.. μισθὸν προτείνασθαι **stipulate for** as a reward, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.34.}}
**III** **put forward as a proposition** (πρότασις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1.1), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 47a15, “Top.” 104a5, +others:}} —_middle_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 164b4}}:—_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.}}

4386:
** πρότερος**
and **πρῶτος**, _comparative_ and _superlative_ formed from πρό, opposed to ὕστερος, ὕστατος. _comparative_ **πρότερος**, α, ον,
**I** of Place, **before, in front**, π. πόδες the **fore**-feet, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.228}}; π. ἵπποι horses **in front**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.43:}} — but mostly,
**II** of Time, **former, earlier**, ἄνδρες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.405; ἄνθρωποι 5.637, 23.332}}; οἱ π. **men of former times**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.308 (rarely without _Article_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1338 (_anapaest meter_), etc.); οὗτος δὲ προτέρης γενεῆς π. τ᾽ ἀνθρώπων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.790}}: also, **older**, opposed to ὁπλότερος, {{Refs|2.707, etc.; γενεῇ π. 15.182}}; but παῖδες π. children **by the first** or **a former marriage**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.22; παῖδες ἐκ τῆς π. γυναικός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.2}}; τῇ προτέρῃ (i.e. ἡμέρᾳ) **on the day before**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.50}}; ἠοῖ τῇ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.794 (in Prose more frequently τῇ προτεραίᾳ, compare προτεραῖος)}}; τοῦ π. ἐνιαυτοῦ the year **before,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.352.11}}; τοῖς π. Παναθηναίοις the **preceding** P., {{Refs|[prev. work]57.8}}; τὰ π. what has **preceded**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.2.8}}:—frequently used predicatively, sometimes where we should expect the _adverb_ (which is never used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), ὅ με π. κάκ᾽ ἔοργε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.351, compare 16.569, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 708, etc.; σπονδὰς οὐ λύσετε πρότεροι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.123; οἱ π. ἐπιόντες [prev. passage]; τοῖς π. μετὰ Κύρου ἀναβᾶσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.12, compare “IG” 22.1.7; εἰ μὴ π. ἑωράκη αὐτὸν ἢ ἐκεῖνος ἐμέ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336d, compare 432c, etc.; ὅτι εἴη π. ὑπὸ ἐκείνων ἠδικημένος “PCair.Zēn.” 288.9 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** as regular _comparative_, with _genitive_, ἐμέο πρότερος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.124; π. τούτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.168, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86b, “Hp.Ma.” 282d; τὰς γυναῖκας μὴ ἀπιέναι προτέρας τῶν ἀνδρῶν “IG” 12(5).593.19 (Iulis, 5th c.BC)}}; τῇ π. ἡμέρᾳ τῆς τροπῆς the day **before.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1316a16; προτέρᾳ εἰδυῶν Ὀκτωμβρίων “IG” 7.2225.14 (Thisbe, Senatus Consultum, 2nd c.BC); τῷ π. ἔτει Παναθηναίων τῶν μεγάλων [prev. work]22.212.27; τῷ π. ἔτει τῆς ἥττης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.43.6: followed by ἤ, τῷ προτέρῳ ἔτεϊ ἢ τὸν κρητῆρα [ἐληΐσαντο] 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.47.}}
**III** of Rank, Worth, and generally of Precedence, **superior**, τῷ γένει, τῇ δυνάμει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.17, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.15}}; π. τινὸς πρός τι **superior to** him in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 183b}}; π. τι ἄγειν, π. ποιήσασθαι τὰ σὰ πράγματα, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 58.36, 52.1.}}
**IV** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, _neuter_ πρότερον}} frequently as _adverb_, **before, earlier**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.31, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.45, “IG” 12.374.265, etc.}}; ὀλίγον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317e: with _genitive_, π. φήμης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 866 (_anapaest meter_); ὀλίγῳ τι π. τούτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.95}}; πολλοῖσι ἔτεσι π. τούτων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 96; ἐνιαυτῷ π. τῆς ἁλώσεως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.60}}; also πρὸ τῶν Περσικῶν δέκα ἔτεσι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 642d, compare “Criti.” 112a}}; τούτου π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.1.2: most frequently followed by ἤ, π. ἢ κατὰ τὴν προσδοκίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 264b; also μὴ π. ἀπαναστῆναι ἢ ἐξέλωσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.87, compare 7.54, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.2, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.63, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, π. ἢ βασιλεῦσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.2, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69, etc.: followed by πρίν, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82; by πρὶν ἄν, [prev. work] 140}}; by πρὶν ἤ with _verb_ in Indic., [{{Refs|[prev. author] 6.45, 8.8, or Subj., 7.8.β (variant{πρὶν ἂν ἢ}), 9.93}}]; also οὐ π. εἰ μὴ.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 10, etc.}}; οὐ π. ἕως.., or ἕως ἂν.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.71, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.640c; μὴ π., ἀλλ᾽ ὅταν.. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.13.3: also used with the _Article_, τὸ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 522a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.14, etc. (τὸ π}}., also, **for the first time, Ep. Gal.**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.13): with _genitive_, τὸ π. τῶν ἀνδρῶν τούτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.144}}: the _adverb_ is frequently put between _Article_ and _substantive_, ὁ π. βασιλεύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.84; τὰ π. ἀδικήματα [prev. author] 6.87; αἱ π. ἁμαρτίαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1355, etc.}}
**B** _superlative_ **πρῶτος**, η, ον, _Doric dialect_ **πρᾶτος** (which see):
**B.I** as _adjective_,
**B.I.1** of Place, **foremost**, πρώτοισιν ἐνὶ προμάχοισι μιγέντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.379}}; ἐν πρώτοις, μετὰ πρώτοισι alone, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.424, 11.64; πρώτῃ ἐν ὑσμίνῃ, ἐνὶ πρώτῳ ὁμάδῳ, 15.340, 17.380}}; τῆς πρώτης τάττειν (i.e. τάξεως) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.180, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.15, etc.}}; ἐν π. ῥυμῷ at **the front** or **end** of the pole, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.40, 16.371}}; πρώτῃσι θύρῃσιν at the **outermost** doors, {{Refs|22.66}}; π. ξύλον the **front** bench, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 25, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.121, etc.}}; οἱ π. πόδες, like{πρόσθιοι}, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.193.}}
**B.I.2** of Time, στάντα πρὸς π. ἕω looking towards **first** dawn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 477; περὶ π. νύκτα 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.70.}}
**B.I.3** of Order, serving as ordinal to εἷς, ἄεθλα θῆκε.. τῷ πρώτῳ· ἀτὰρ αὖ τῷ δευτέρῳ.., αὐτὰρ τῷ τριτάτῳ.., κτλ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.265, compare 6.179; opposed to ὕστατος, 2.281, 5.703, etc.}}; opposed to τελευταῖος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 314}}; opposed to τανύστατος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.449; πρῶτοι πάντων ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2; τὰ π. τῶν ὀνομάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 421d; τῇ π. τῶν ἡμερέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.168, etc.; π. ἄξων “IG” 12.115.10}}; ἐπὶ τοῦ π. [ἱερείου] **first-offered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.9}}; ἐν τοῖς π. λόγοις in the **earlier** books, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 263a11, +others}}; ἐν πρώτοις among **the first**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.40}}; hence, **above all, especially**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 522c}}; in _Attic dialect_, ἐν τοῖς πρῶτοι (see. ὁ, ἡ, τό {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus VIII. 6):—frequently used predicatively of being the first to do something, Νέστωρ πρῶτος κτύπον ἄϊε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.532; πρῶτος ἀνατέλλει 3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus “Cal.” 42; εἴθε π. σοι ἐνέτυχον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tyr.” 21.}}
**B.I.3.b** Philos., **first in order of existence, primary**, αἱ π. οὐσίαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 2b26, compare “Metaphorical” 1032b2; π. ὕλη, π. φιλοσοφία, [prev. work] 1015a7, 1061b19}}; **primitive, simple**, οἰκία π., ἡ π. πόλις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1252b10, 1291a17; ἡ π. κοινωνία [prev. work] 1257a19; ἡ π. ὀλιγαρχία [prev. work] 1293a14}}; ὁ π. συλλογισμός **normal, typical**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1357a17}}; τὰ π. σώματα, μόρια,= τὰ ὁμοιομερῆ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.673,674}}; πρῶτα κατὰ φύσιν, e.g. health, perception, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.34; τὰ π. πάθη [prev. work]92; αἱ π. ἀρεταί [prev. work]64.}}
**B.I.3.c** in Mathematics texts, πρῶτοι ἀριθμοί **prime** numbers, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 11,12}}; but also, **first** numbers (= 1 to 100,000,000) in the notation of {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra, “Aren.” 3.2.}}
**B.I.3.d** πρῶτος is sometimes used where we should expect πρότερος, Αἰνείας δὲ πρῶτος ἀκόντισεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.502, compare 18.92}}: in late Greek followed by _genitive_, πρῶτός μου ἦν {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.John.1.15, 30, compare 15.18; οἱ πρῶτοί μου ταῦτα ἀνιχνεύσαντες 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 8.12; πρώτη εὕρηται ἡ περὶ τοὺς πόδας κίνησις τῆς διὰ τῶν χειρῶν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.630c; γεννήτορα πρῶτον μητέρος εἰς ἀΐδην πέμψει 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 1.329, 4.404}}; ἀλόχου πρῶτος **before** his wife, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 12(5).590.5 (vi (?) 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus).}}
**B.I.4** of Rank or Dignity, μετὰ πρώτοισιν among **the first men** of the state, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.60, etc.; νομίσαντες πρῶτοι ἂν εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.28}}; διαβάλλειν τοὺς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.26, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1266a18; αἱ π. πόλεις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8; ὁ π. ἄρχων “IG” 12(3).481.10 (from Thera), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2837 (from Aphrodisias)}}; ὁ π. τῆς πόλεως, as a title, {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).292.2 (from Paros)}}; ὁ π. τῆς νήσου {{NT|NT.Act.28.7}}; τῶν π. φίλων, title at the Ptolemaic court, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 31.15 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; τῶν π., as military title, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.110.72 (3rd c.BC), “PPetr.” 3p.23 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 815 “fragment” 4.23, +others(3rd c.BC)}}: with _genitive_, ἐν πρώτοισι Μυκηναίων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.643; οἱ π. στρατοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1305, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 304, etc.: with _dative_ modi, ἀρετῇ π., οἱ π. καὶ χρήμασι καὶ γένει, πλούτῳ π. τῶν Ἑλλήνων, etc., 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1425, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.65, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.31, etc.; π. ἐν συμφοραῖς βίου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 33.}}
**B.I.5** of Degree, **first, highest**, μοῖρα {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 145 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}
**B.II** as _substantive_ in _neuter_ _plural_ πρῶτα, τά,
**B.II.1** (i.e. ἆθλα), **first prize**, τὰ π. λαβών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.275; τὰ π. δόρει κρατύνων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1313; ἔχειν πρῶτα κυναγεσίας “Anthologia Graeca” 6.118 (Antip.); τὰ π. φέρεσθαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.57, etc.}}
**B.II.2** **first part, beginning**, τῆς Ἰλιάδος τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 392e}}; ἐν τοῖς π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 221d; τὸ π. τοῦ ᾄσματος [prev. author] “Prt.” 343c.}}
**B.II.3** **first, highest**, in degree, τὰ π. τᾶς λιμῶ (_Doric dialect_) the **extremities** of famine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 743 (nisi to be read ἄπρατα); ἐχέτωσαν τὰ π. τῆς εὐδαιμονίας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 10; ἐς τὰ π. τιμᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39, compare 56}}; φρενῶν ἐς τὰ ἐμεωυτοῦ π. οὔκω ἀνήκω I have not yet come to the **highest development** of my judgment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.13, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.22}}; of persons, ἐὼν τῶν Ἐρετριέων τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.100}}; Λάμπων.. Αἰγινητέων [ἐὼν] τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.78, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 917}}; ἐστὶν τὰ π. τῆς ἐκεῖ μοχθηρίας (of a person) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 421.}}
**B.II.4** Philos., **primary things, elements**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 38.1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 335a29; τὰ π. αἴτια [prev. author] “Mete.” 338a20; also τὸ π. ἐνυπάρχον ἑκάστῳ [prev. author] “Ph.” 193a10.}}
**B.II.5** in Logic, **the first undemonstrable propositions**, on which all future conclusions rest, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Top.” 100b18; τὰ π. ἀναπόδεικτα [prev. author] “APo.” 71b26.}}
**B.III** in Adverbial phrases,
**B.III.1** τὴν πρώτην (i.e. ὥραν, ὁδόν) **first, for the present, just now**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 662, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1038a35, etc.; τὴν πρώτην εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153.}}
**B.III.2** with Preps., ἀπὸ πρώτης (i.e. ἀρχῆς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77; ἀπὸ τῆς π. εὐθύς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 1}}; ἐκ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 45.14; κατὰ πρώτας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 292b, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.19; κατὰ τὴν π. εὐθύς [prev. author] 62.3}}; παρὰ τὴν π. **the first time**, opposed to ἐπὶ τῆς δευτέρας, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.22.}}
**B.III.3** frequently as _adverb_ in _neuter_ _singular_ and _plural_, πρῶτον, πρῶτα,
**B.III.3.a** **first, in the first place**, πρῶτόν τε καὶ ὕστατον (_vulgar form_ ὕστερον) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 34; π. μὲν.., δεύτερον αὖ.., τὸ τρίτον αὖ.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.179}}; τί π. τοι ἔπειτα, τί δ᾽ ὑστάτιον καταλέξ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.14; Κύπριδα μὲν πρῶτα.., αὐτὰρ ἔπειτ᾽.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.458; οὐρῆας μὲν π. ἐπῴχετο.., αὐτὰρ ἔπειτα.. 1.50; π. μὲν.., ἔπειτα δὲ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 632, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.2, 23, “An.” 5.6.7 - 8, “Hier.” 11.8, etc.; π. μὲν.., ἔπειτα.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89a, etc.; π. μὲν.., ἔπειτα δεύτερον.., τρίτον δὲ.. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.7; π. μὲν.., εἶτα.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15b; π. μὲν.., εἶτα δὲ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.16; π. μὲν.., εἶτα.., ἔτι δὲ.. [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.1; π. μὲν..,.. δὲ αὖ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 935a; π. μὲν.., ἔτι δὲ.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.10, etc.; π. μὲν.., ἔτι τοίνυν.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.57}}; frequently answered only by δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.48, etc.}}; sometimes the answering clause must be supplied, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 810, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.7, etc.: also πρῶτον μὲν.. δεύτερον μήν.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66a: also πρῶτα μὲν.., ἔπειτα.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 616, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 728; πρῶτα μὲν.., ἔπειτα.., εἶτα.. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 548; πρῶτα μὲν..,.. δὲ.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 447}}; πρῶτα μὲν.., ἔπειτα δὲ.. X {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“HG” 7.1.7, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 919}}; ἐπεί σε πρῶτα κιχάνω since my **first** meeting is with you, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.228, compare 7.53, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.274: also τὸ πρῶτον}}, **first, in the first place, at the beginning**, ὡς τὸ π. ὑπέστην καὶ κατένευσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.267; οὕνεκά σ᾽ οὐ τὸ π., ἐπεὶ ἴδον, ὧδ᾽ ἀγάπησα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.214. compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.443, 6.345, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.41, “N.” 3.49}}; τὸ μὲν οὖν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 333d, etc.}}; τὸ π..., μετὰ ταῦτα.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Dem. 1.12: also τὰ π., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.6, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.257, etc.; πόντῳ μὲν τὰ π..., αὐτὰρ ἔπειτα.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.424; τὰ π. μὲν.., ὡς δὲ.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 412; τὰ π..., τέλος δὲ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 149.5, compare 966.}}
**B.b** **too early, before the time**, ἦ τ᾽ ἄρα καὶ σοὶ πρῶτα (variant for{πρωΐ}) παραστήσεσθαι ἔμελλε Μοῖρ᾽ ὀλοή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.28.}}
**B.c** ={πρότερον}, **before**, ἢν.. πρῶτον ἀπόλωμαι κακῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1079; π. οὐδ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἑνὸς.. κρατηθέντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.1}}; θάλασσα π. ἦν ἢ γενέσθαι γῆν variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 31; λόγῳ π. ἢ τοῖς ἔργοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1420b28; οὐ π. αὐτὴν ἀπέκτειναν πρὶν ἢ ἀπεκύησεν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 5.18; π. συμμελετᾶν ἢ μελετᾶν μαθέτω “Anthologia Graeca” 12.206 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**B.d** **first, for the first time**, οὐ.. νῦν πρῶτα ποδώκεος ἄντ᾽ Ἀχιλῆος στήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.89; οὐ νῦν πρῶτον, ἀλλὰ καὶ πάλαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 966; ἐνταῦθα πρῶτον ἔφαγον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.16.}}
**B.e** πρῶτον, πρῶτα are used after the _relative_ _pronoun_ and after _relative_ _adverbs_, like Engl.**once** (= **at all**), οὐδ᾽ ἐνοσίχθων λήθετ᾽ ἀπειλάων, τὰς.. Ὀδυσῆϊ π. ἐπηπείλησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.127, compare 3.320, 10.328, 13.133, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.319, 19.136}}; μοῖραν δ᾽ οὔ τινά φημι πεφυγμένον ἔμμεναι ἀνδρῶν.. ἐπὴν τὰ π. γένηται when **once** he is born,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.489, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.183, 4.13, 414; οὔτε.. Λυκίους ἐδύναντο τείχεος ἂψ ὤσασθαι, ἐπεὶ τὰ π. πέλασθεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.420, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.106, 221}}; also ἐπεὶ τὸ (or τὰ) π. **now that.. **, ἀλλ᾽ ἐπεὶ οὖν τὸ π. ἀνέκραγον, οὐκ ἐπικεύσω **now that** I have spoken up,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.467; τὸ μὲν οὔ ποτε φύλλα καὶ ὄζους φύσει, ἐπεὶ δὴ πρῶτα τομὴν ἐν ὄρεσσι λέλοιπε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.235, compare 276, 19.9: with}}_participle_, τῷ ῥ᾽ Αἴας τὸ π. ἐφεζόμενος μέγ᾽ ἀάσθη (the rock) on which **once** seated Aeschylus blasphemed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.509}}: the sense **as soon as** is never necessary in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, but is possible in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.414, 19.355}}; δινέμεν εὖτ᾽ ἂν πρῶτα φανῇ σθένος Ὠαρίωνος when **once** (or perhaps, **as soon as**), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 598}}; ὅπως τις πρῶτα γένοιτο πάντας ἀποκρύπτεσκε **as soon as** each was born, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 156}}; ὡς τὸ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.14; τότ᾽ εὐθὺς.., ὅτε πρῶτον εἶδον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.141}}; αὖθίς με ἀνερέσθαι ὅταν ἐντύχῃς πρῶτον the **first time** you meet me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 211b; ἐὰν μάθω γε πρῶτον.. τί λέγεις [prev. author] “R.” 338c.}}
**B.IV** _adverb_ πρώτως **primarily**, first in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, π. καὶ κυρίως “EN” 1157a30; opposed to δευτέρως, [prev. work] 1158b31}}; π. καθ᾽ αὑτό, opposed to κατὰ συμβεβηκός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 192b22, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.692, +others, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.168b.}}
**B.IV.2** ὅτε π. ἐπεδήμησεν.. when he **first** visited.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BSA” 27.228 (Sparta, 2nd c.AD).}} —(From πρῶτος was formed a new _superlative_ πρώτιστος, which see)

4387:
** πρότερος**
and **πρῶτος**, _comparative_ and _superlative_ formed from πρό, opposed to ὕστερος, ὕστατος. _comparative_ **πρότερος**, α, ον,
**I** of Place, **before, in front**, π. πόδες the **fore**-feet, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.228}}; π. ἵπποι horses **in front**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.43:}} — but mostly,
**II** of Time, **former, earlier**, ἄνδρες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.405; ἄνθρωποι 5.637, 23.332}}; οἱ π. **men of former times**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.308 (rarely without _Article_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1338 (_anapaest meter_), etc.); οὗτος δὲ προτέρης γενεῆς π. τ᾽ ἀνθρώπων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.790}}: also, **older**, opposed to ὁπλότερος, {{Refs|2.707, etc.; γενεῇ π. 15.182}}; but παῖδες π. children **by the first** or **a former marriage**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.22; παῖδες ἐκ τῆς π. γυναικός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.2}}; τῇ προτέρῃ (i.e. ἡμέρᾳ) **on the day before**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.50}}; ἠοῖ τῇ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.794 (in Prose more frequently τῇ προτεραίᾳ, compare προτεραῖος)}}; τοῦ π. ἐνιαυτοῦ the year **before,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.352.11}}; τοῖς π. Παναθηναίοις the **preceding** P., {{Refs|[prev. work]57.8}}; τὰ π. what has **preceded**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.2.8}}:—frequently used predicatively, sometimes where we should expect the _adverb_ (which is never used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus), ὅ με π. κάκ᾽ ἔοργε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.351, compare 16.569, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 708, etc.; σπονδὰς οὐ λύσετε πρότεροι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.123; οἱ π. ἐπιόντες [prev. passage]; τοῖς π. μετὰ Κύρου ἀναβᾶσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.12, compare “IG” 22.1.7; εἰ μὴ π. ἑωράκη αὐτὸν ἢ ἐκεῖνος ἐμέ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 336d, compare 432c, etc.; ὅτι εἴη π. ὑπὸ ἐκείνων ἠδικημένος “PCair.Zēn.” 288.9 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** as regular _comparative_, with _genitive_, ἐμέο πρότερος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.124; π. τούτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.168, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86b, “Hp.Ma.” 282d; τὰς γυναῖκας μὴ ἀπιέναι προτέρας τῶν ἀνδρῶν “IG” 12(5).593.19 (Iulis, 5th c.BC)}}; τῇ π. ἡμέρᾳ τῆς τροπῆς the day **before.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1316a16; προτέρᾳ εἰδυῶν Ὀκτωμβρίων “IG” 7.2225.14 (Thisbe, Senatus Consultum, 2nd c.BC); τῷ π. ἔτει Παναθηναίων τῶν μεγάλων [prev. work]22.212.27; τῷ π. ἔτει τῆς ἥττης 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.43.6: followed by ἤ, τῷ προτέρῳ ἔτεϊ ἢ τὸν κρητῆρα [ἐληΐσαντο] 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.47.}}
**III** of Rank, Worth, and generally of Precedence, **superior**, τῷ γένει, τῇ δυνάμει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.17, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.15}}; π. τινὸς πρός τι **superior to** him in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 183b}}; π. τι ἄγειν, π. ποιήσασθαι τὰ σὰ πράγματα, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 58.36, 52.1.}}
**IV** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, _neuter_ πρότερον}} frequently as _adverb_, **before, earlier**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.31, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.45, “IG” 12.374.265, etc.}}; ὀλίγον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317e: with _genitive_, π. φήμης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 866 (_anapaest meter_); ὀλίγῳ τι π. τούτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.95}}; πολλοῖσι ἔτεσι π. τούτων{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 96; ἐνιαυτῷ π. τῆς ἁλώσεως 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.60}}; also πρὸ τῶν Περσικῶν δέκα ἔτεσι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 642d, compare “Criti.” 112a}}; τούτου π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.1.2: most frequently followed by ἤ, π. ἢ κατὰ τὴν προσδοκίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 264b; also μὴ π. ἀπαναστῆναι ἢ ἐξέλωσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.87, compare 7.54, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.2, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.63, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, π. ἢ βασιλεῦσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.2, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69, etc.: followed by πρίν, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82; by πρὶν ἄν, [prev. work] 140}}; by πρὶν ἤ with _verb_ in Indic., [{{Refs|[prev. author] 6.45, 8.8, or Subj., 7.8.β (variant{πρὶν ἂν ἢ}), 9.93}}]; also οὐ π. εἰ μὴ.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 10, etc.}}; οὐ π. ἕως.., or ἕως ἂν.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.71, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.640c; μὴ π., ἀλλ᾽ ὅταν.. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.13.3: also used with the _Article_, τὸ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 522a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.14, etc. (τὸ π}}., also, **for the first time, Ep. Gal.**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.13): with _genitive_, τὸ π. τῶν ἀνδρῶν τούτων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.144}}: the _adverb_ is frequently put between _Article_ and _substantive_, ὁ π. βασιλεύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.84; τὰ π. ἀδικήματα [prev. author] 6.87; αἱ π. ἁμαρτίαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1355, etc.}}
**B** _superlative_ **πρῶτος**, η, ον, _Doric dialect_ **πρᾶτος** (which see):
**B.I** as _adjective_,
**B.I.1** of Place, **foremost**, πρώτοισιν ἐνὶ προμάχοισι μιγέντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.379}}; ἐν πρώτοις, μετὰ πρώτοισι alone, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.424, 11.64; πρώτῃ ἐν ὑσμίνῃ, ἐνὶ πρώτῳ ὁμάδῳ, 15.340, 17.380}}; τῆς πρώτης τάττειν (i.e. τάξεως) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.180, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.15, etc.}}; ἐν π. ῥυμῷ at **the front** or **end** of the pole, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.40, 16.371}}; πρώτῃσι θύρῃσιν at the **outermost** doors, {{Refs|22.66}}; π. ξύλον the **front** bench, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 25, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.121, etc.}}; οἱ π. πόδες, like{πρόσθιοι}, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.193.}}
**B.I.2** of Time, στάντα πρὸς π. ἕω looking towards **first** dawn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 477; περὶ π. νύκτα 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.70.}}
**B.I.3** of Order, serving as ordinal to εἷς, ἄεθλα θῆκε.. τῷ πρώτῳ· ἀτὰρ αὖ τῷ δευτέρῳ.., αὐτὰρ τῷ τριτάτῳ.., κτλ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.265, compare 6.179; opposed to ὕστατος, 2.281, 5.703, etc.}}; opposed to τελευταῖος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 314}}; opposed to τανύστατος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.449; πρῶτοι πάντων ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2; τὰ π. τῶν ὀνομάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 421d; τῇ π. τῶν ἡμερέων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.168, etc.; π. ἄξων “IG” 12.115.10}}; ἐπὶ τοῦ π. [ἱερείου] **first-offered**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.9}}; ἐν τοῖς π. λόγοις in the **earlier** books, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 263a11, +others}}; ἐν πρώτοις among **the first**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.40}}; hence, **above all, especially**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 522c}}; in _Attic dialect_, ἐν τοῖς πρῶτοι (see. ὁ, ἡ, τό {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus VIII. 6):—frequently used predicatively of being the first to do something, Νέστωρ πρῶτος κτύπον ἄϊε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.532; πρῶτος ἀνατέλλει 3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus “Cal.” 42; εἴθε π. σοι ἐνέτυχον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tyr.” 21.}}
**B.I.3.b** Philos., **first in order of existence, primary**, αἱ π. οὐσίαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 2b26, compare “Metaphorical” 1032b2; π. ὕλη, π. φιλοσοφία, [prev. work] 1015a7, 1061b19}}; **primitive, simple**, οἰκία π., ἡ π. πόλις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1252b10, 1291a17; ἡ π. κοινωνία [prev. work] 1257a19; ἡ π. ὀλιγαρχία [prev. work] 1293a14}}; ὁ π. συλλογισμός **normal, typical**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1357a17}}; τὰ π. σώματα, μόρια,= τὰ ὁμοιομερῆ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.673,674}}; πρῶτα κατὰ φύσιν, e.g. health, perception, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.34; τὰ π. πάθη [prev. work]92; αἱ π. ἀρεταί [prev. work]64.}}
**B.I.3.c** in Mathematics texts, πρῶτοι ἀριθμοί **prime** numbers, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7 “Def.” 11,12}}; but also, **first** numbers (= 1 to 100,000,000) in the notation of {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra, “Aren.” 3.2.}}
**B.I.3.d** πρῶτος is sometimes used where we should expect πρότερος, Αἰνείας δὲ πρῶτος ἀκόντισεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.502, compare 18.92}}: in late Greek followed by _genitive_, πρῶτός μου ἦν {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.John.1.15, 30, compare 15.18; οἱ πρῶτοί μου ταῦτα ἀνιχνεύσαντες 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 8.12; πρώτη εὕρηται ἡ περὶ τοὺς πόδας κίνησις τῆς διὰ τῶν χειρῶν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 14.630c; γεννήτορα πρῶτον μητέρος εἰς ἀΐδην πέμψει 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 1.329, 4.404}}; ἀλόχου πρῶτος **before** his wife, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 12(5).590.5 (vi (?) 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus).}}
**B.I.4** of Rank or Dignity, μετὰ πρώτοισιν among **the first men** of the state, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.60, etc.; νομίσαντες πρῶτοι ἂν εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.28}}; διαβάλλειν τοὺς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.26, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1266a18; αἱ π. πόλεις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8; ὁ π. ἄρχων “IG” 12(3).481.10 (from Thera), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2837 (from Aphrodisias)}}; ὁ π. τῆς πόλεως, as a title, {{Refs|“IG” 12(5).292.2 (from Paros)}}; ὁ π. τῆς νήσου {{NT|NT.Act.28.7}}; τῶν π. φίλων, title at the Ptolemaic court, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 31.15 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; τῶν π., as military title, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.110.72 (3rd c.BC), “PPetr.” 3p.23 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 815 “fragment” 4.23, +others(3rd c.BC)}}: with _genitive_, ἐν πρώτοισι Μυκηναίων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.643; οἱ π. στρατοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1305, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 304, etc.: with _dative_ modi, ἀρετῇ π., οἱ π. καὶ χρήμασι καὶ γένει, πλούτῳ π. τῶν Ἑλλήνων, etc., 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1425, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.65, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 16.31, etc.; π. ἐν συμφοραῖς βίου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 33.}}
**B.I.5** of Degree, **first, highest**, μοῖρα {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 145 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}
**B.II** as _substantive_ in _neuter_ _plural_ πρῶτα, τά,
**B.II.1** (i.e. ἆθλα), **first prize**, τὰ π. λαβών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.275; τὰ π. δόρει κρατύνων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1313; ἔχειν πρῶτα κυναγεσίας “Anthologia Graeca” 6.118 (Antip.); τὰ π. φέρεσθαι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.57, etc.}}
**B.II.2** **first part, beginning**, τῆς Ἰλιάδος τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 392e}}; ἐν τοῖς π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 221d; τὸ π. τοῦ ᾄσματος [prev. author] “Prt.” 343c.}}
**B.II.3** **first, highest**, in degree, τὰ π. τᾶς λιμῶ (_Doric dialect_) the **extremities** of famine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 743 (nisi to be read ἄπρατα); ἐχέτωσαν τὰ π. τῆς εὐδαιμονίας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 10; ἐς τὰ π. τιμᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.39, compare 56}}; φρενῶν ἐς τὰ ἐμεωυτοῦ π. οὔκω ἀνήκω I have not yet come to the **highest development** of my judgment, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.13, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.22}}; of persons, ἐὼν τῶν Ἐρετριέων τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.100}}; Λάμπων.. Αἰγινητέων [ἐὼν] τὰ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.78, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 917}}; ἐστὶν τὰ π. τῆς ἐκεῖ μοχθηρίας (of a person) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 421.}}
**B.II.4** Philos., **primary things, elements**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 38.1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 335a29; τὰ π. αἴτια [prev. author] “Mete.” 338a20; also τὸ π. ἐνυπάρχον ἑκάστῳ [prev. author] “Ph.” 193a10.}}
**B.II.5** in Logic, **the first undemonstrable propositions**, on which all future conclusions rest, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Top.” 100b18; τὰ π. ἀναπόδεικτα [prev. author] “APo.” 71b26.}}
**B.III** in Adverbial phrases,
**B.III.1** τὴν πρώτην (i.e. ὥραν, ὁδόν) **first, for the present, just now**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 662, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1038a35, etc.; τὴν πρώτην εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153.}}
**B.III.2** with Preps., ἀπὸ πρώτης (i.e. ἀρχῆς) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77; ἀπὸ τῆς π. εὐθύς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 1}}; ἐκ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 45.14; κατὰ πρώτας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 292b, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.19; κατὰ τὴν π. εὐθύς [prev. author] 62.3}}; παρὰ τὴν π. **the first time**, opposed to ἐπὶ τῆς δευτέρας, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.22.}}
**B.III.3** frequently as _adverb_ in _neuter_ _singular_ and _plural_, πρῶτον, πρῶτα,
**B.III.3.a** **first, in the first place**, πρῶτόν τε καὶ ὕστατον (_vulgar form_ ὕστερον) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 34; π. μὲν.., δεύτερον αὖ.., τὸ τρίτον αὖ.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.179}}; τί π. τοι ἔπειτα, τί δ᾽ ὑστάτιον καταλέξ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.14; Κύπριδα μὲν πρῶτα.., αὐτὰρ ἔπειτ᾽.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.458; οὐρῆας μὲν π. ἐπῴχετο.., αὐτὰρ ἔπειτα.. 1.50; π. μὲν.., ἔπειτα δὲ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 632, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.2, 23, “An.” 5.6.7 - 8, “Hier.” 11.8, etc.; π. μὲν.., ἔπειτα.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89a, etc.; π. μὲν.., ἔπειτα δεύτερον.., τρίτον δὲ.. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.7; π. μὲν.., εἶτα.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15b; π. μὲν.., εἶτα δὲ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.16; π. μὲν.., εἶτα.., ἔτι δὲ.. [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.1; π. μὲν..,.. δὲ αὖ.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 935a; π. μὲν.., ἔτι δὲ.. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 4.10, etc.; π. μὲν.., ἔτι τοίνυν.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.57}}; frequently answered only by δέ, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.48, etc.}}; sometimes the answering clause must be supplied, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 810, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.7, etc.: also πρῶτον μὲν.. δεύτερον μήν.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66a: also πρῶτα μὲν.., ἔπειτα.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 616, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 728; πρῶτα μὲν.., ἔπειτα.., εἶτα.. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 548; πρῶτα μὲν..,.. δὲ.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 447}}; πρῶτα μὲν.., ἔπειτα δὲ.. X {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“HG” 7.1.7, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 919}}; ἐπεί σε πρῶτα κιχάνω since my **first** meeting is with you, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.228, compare 7.53, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.274: also τὸ πρῶτον}}, **first, in the first place, at the beginning**, ὡς τὸ π. ὑπέστην καὶ κατένευσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.267; οὕνεκά σ᾽ οὐ τὸ π., ἐπεὶ ἴδον, ὧδ᾽ ἀγάπησα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.214. compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.443, 6.345, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.41, “N.” 3.49}}; τὸ μὲν οὖν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 333d, etc.}}; τὸ π..., μετὰ ταῦτα.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Dem. 1.12: also τὰ π., 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.6, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.257, etc.; πόντῳ μὲν τὰ π..., αὐτὰρ ἔπειτα.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.424; τὰ π. μὲν.., ὡς δὲ.. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 412; τὰ π..., τέλος δὲ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 149.5, compare 966.}}
**B.b** **too early, before the time**, ἦ τ᾽ ἄρα καὶ σοὶ πρῶτα (variant for{πρωΐ}) παραστήσεσθαι ἔμελλε Μοῖρ᾽ ὀλοή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.28.}}
**B.c** ={πρότερον}, **before**, ἢν.. πρῶτον ἀπόλωμαι κακῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1079; π. οὐδ᾽ ὑφ᾽ ἑνὸς.. κρατηθέντες 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.1}}; θάλασσα π. ἦν ἢ γενέσθαι γῆν variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 31; λόγῳ π. ἢ τοῖς ἔργοις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1420b28; οὐ π. αὐτὴν ἀπέκτειναν πρὶν ἢ ἀπεκύησεν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 5.18; π. συμμελετᾶν ἢ μελετᾶν μαθέτω “Anthologia Graeca” 12.206 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**B.d** **first, for the first time**, οὐ.. νῦν πρῶτα ποδώκεος ἄντ᾽ Ἀχιλῆος στήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.89; οὐ νῦν πρῶτον, ἀλλὰ καὶ πάλαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 966; ἐνταῦθα πρῶτον ἔφαγον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.16.}}
**B.e** πρῶτον, πρῶτα are used after the _relative_ _pronoun_ and after _relative_ _adverbs_, like Engl.**once** (= **at all**), οὐδ᾽ ἐνοσίχθων λήθετ᾽ ἀπειλάων, τὰς.. Ὀδυσῆϊ π. ἐπηπείλησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.127, compare 3.320, 10.328, 13.133, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.319, 19.136}}; μοῖραν δ᾽ οὔ τινά φημι πεφυγμένον ἔμμεναι ἀνδρῶν.. ἐπὴν τὰ π. γένηται when **once** he is born,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.489, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.183, 4.13, 414; οὔτε.. Λυκίους ἐδύναντο τείχεος ἂψ ὤσασθαι, ἐπεὶ τὰ π. πέλασθεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.420, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.106, 221}}; also ἐπεὶ τὸ (or τὰ) π. **now that.. **, ἀλλ᾽ ἐπεὶ οὖν τὸ π. ἀνέκραγον, οὐκ ἐπικεύσω **now that** I have spoken up,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.467; τὸ μὲν οὔ ποτε φύλλα καὶ ὄζους φύσει, ἐπεὶ δὴ πρῶτα τομὴν ἐν ὄρεσσι λέλοιπε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.235, compare 276, 19.9: with}}_participle_, τῷ ῥ᾽ Αἴας τὸ π. ἐφεζόμενος μέγ᾽ ἀάσθη (the rock) on which **once** seated Aeschylus blasphemed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.509}}: the sense **as soon as** is never necessary in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, but is possible in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.414, 19.355}}; δινέμεν εὖτ᾽ ἂν πρῶτα φανῇ σθένος Ὠαρίωνος when **once** (or perhaps, **as soon as**), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 598}}; ὅπως τις πρῶτα γένοιτο πάντας ἀποκρύπτεσκε **as soon as** each was born, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 156}}; ὡς τὸ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.8.14; τότ᾽ εὐθὺς.., ὅτε πρῶτον εἶδον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.141}}; αὖθίς με ἀνερέσθαι ὅταν ἐντύχῃς πρῶτον the **first time** you meet me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 211b; ἐὰν μάθω γε πρῶτον.. τί λέγεις [prev. author] “R.” 338c.}}
**B.IV** _adverb_ πρώτως **primarily**, first in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, π. καὶ κυρίως “EN” 1157a30; opposed to δευτέρως, [prev. work] 1158b31}}; π. καθ᾽ αὑτό, opposed to κατὰ συμβεβηκός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 192b22, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.692, +others, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 5.168b.}}
**B.IV.2** ὅτε π. ἐπεδήμησεν.. when he **first** visited.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BSA” 27.228 (Sparta, 2nd c.AD).}} —(From πρῶτος was formed a new _superlative_ πρώτιστος, which see)

4388:
** προτίθημι**
(also thematic forms, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ προτιθεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.133}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ προτιθεῖσι {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.197)}}, _future_ **-θήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67}}: _aorist_ προὔθηκα [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.409, etc. (for προθέουσι see at {προθέω} (B)):}}] —_middle_ (see. below):—_passive_, _aorist 1_ προὐτέθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 803, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 90b, etc.}}; but _present_ and _imperfect_ _passive_ are usually supplied by πρόκειμαι:—**set before, set out**, especially of meals, τραπέζας νίζον καὶ πρότιθεν (_Epic dialect_ for προὐτίθεσαν) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.112; βοῦν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 537; τούτοισι προθεῖναι δαῖτα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1294, “Ant.” 775, “Ph.” 274, etc.; ξείνια στρατῷ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.29}}:—_middle_, **set before oneself, have set before one**, δαῖτα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] 1.133, 4.26; κλίνας καὶ τραπέζας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.99e}}; also προτίθεσθαί τισι ἄριστον **cause** it **to be set before** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Chionides Comicus 7.}}
**b** π. τινὰ κυσίν **throw** him to the dogs, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.409}}; τινὰ θηρσὶν ἁρπαγὴν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 896.}}
**c** **hand over for burial**, τινά τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1198, compare 1487.}}
**2** **expose** a child, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.112; π. τινὰ ἔρημον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 268}}:—_passive_, ὁ θανάτῳ προτεθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 803 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **set up, institute, propose**, especially of contests, ἅμιλλαν λόγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 546; λόγων τοὺς ἀγῶνας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67; εἰ πονηρίας ἀγὼν προτεθείη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 90b}}; ἄπορον αἵρεσιν **offer** a choice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 196c (but also προτίθεσθαί τινι αἵρεσιν [prev. author] “Lg.” 858a); π. νόμον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1046.}}
**3.b** **set up as a mark** or **prize**, στέφανόν τισι τῶν ἀγώνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.46; σκοπὸν κάλλιστον ἐν τῷ ζῆν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.8.9}}:—_passive_, προὐτέθην ἐγὼ ἆθλον.. δορός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 42.}}
**3.c** **set as a penalty**, θάνατον ζημίαν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.44}}; τιμωρίας ἔτι μείζους οὐ μέχρι τοῦ δικαίου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 82; τῶν νόμων τὰ ἔσχατα ἐπιτίμια προτεθηκότων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.37; τοῖσι ἐκείνου ἀπογόνοισι ἀέθλους τοιούσδε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.197.}}
**4** **fix, set**, ἐς ἑβδομήκοντα ἔτεα οὖρον τῆς ζόης ἀνθρώπῳ π. {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.32:}} —_middle_; οὖρον π. ἐνιαυτόν{{Refs|[prev. work] 74.}}
**5** **appoint as a task** or **duty**, τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1049; νεωτέρῳ τοῦτο βαστάζειν πρόθες [prev. author] “Ant.” 216}}; δμῳαῖς π. πένθος οἰκεῖον στένειν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1249, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.94}}:—_middle_, **propose to oneself** as a task or object, ὅπερ προὐθέμεθα σκέψασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 259e, “R.” 352d, compare “Sph.” 221a, “Tht.” 169c; π. ψέγειν αὐτὸ ἢ ἐπαινεῖν [prev. author] “Lg.” 638c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1142b19 (uncertain), 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.1.6, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 19.}}
**5.b** _middle_, with _future_ _infinitive_, **propose to oneself, intend**, προτεθειμένου κατοικήσειν ἐνταῦθα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Syr.D.” 13.256 (Seleucia in Pieria, 2nd c.BC).}}
**6** _middle_, also, **put forth on one's own part, display, show**, εὐλάβειαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1334; ἀνδραγαθίαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.64; ἔχθραν [prev. author] 8.85.}}
**7** _middle_, θνητοὺς ἐν οἴκτῳ προθέμενος **setting before oneself** in pity, feeling compassion for, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 241.}}
**8** **advance** money, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IPE” 12.32 B 30,63 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)}}; τὸ διδόμενον ἆθλον ὑπὸ τοῦ δήμου αὐτὸς προέθηκεν ἐκ τοῦ ἰδίου {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 12(9).234.18 (Eretria, 1st c.BC)}}; τισὶν τῶν πολιτῶν εἰς λύτρα προτιθείς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 708.15 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II** π. νεκρόν **lay out** a dead body, **let** it **lie in state**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.8}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 664, “Supp.” 53 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 611, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.18, etc.; ποτήρια χρύσεα προθεῖτο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.148.}}
**II.2** **set out** wares **for show** or **sale**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 25, +others}}
**II.3** **display** a public notice, τὸ λεύκωμα πρὸ τοῦ ναοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).1109.35 (Coropa, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἐπ᾽ αὐτῆς τῆς οἰκίας π. ἐν λευκώματι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 106.20 (2nd c.BC)}}; τὸ ὑπογεγραμμένον ἔκθεμα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 664.4 (Egypt, 1st c.AD),compare “POxy.” 2108.8 (_passive_, 3rd c.AD)}}; **notify publicly**, τοὺς [προέδρους] προτιθέναι περὶ ὧν δεῖ βουλεύεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).68.80 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; περὶ ὧν.. οἱ ἄρχοντες προτίθεισι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Ἀρχ.Δελτ.9 παρ.53 (from Eresus),compare “IG” 12(2).526a21 ([prev. passage], 4th c.BC), 645b35 (Nesus, 4th c.BC)}}; τὰς πράξεις (exactions).. τῶν προτιθεμένων (_passive_) κατὰ τὰς ἐγγραφάς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1321b42}}; **call** a case **for trial by means of a public notice**, τῶν κατ᾽ αὐτοὺς προτεθέντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 118.18 (2nd c.BC)}}; οἱ προτεθέντες ἐπ᾽ ἐμὲ καὶ μὴ ὑπακούσαντες ἴστωσαν ὅτι.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PHamb.” 29.6 (1st c.AD)}}; **advertise** for sale or other purpose, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“BGU” 992 i 7 (_passive_, 2nd c.BC), “PLips.” 64.44 (4th c.AD), etc.}}; τοῖς συλλημψομένοις ὑμῖν γέρα προτιθέντα **advertising** rewards to those of you who apprehend (robbers), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1408.16 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.4** **propose, bring forward** a thing to be examined and debated, also **give an opportunity for** debate, voting, ἐς μέσον σφι π. πρῆγμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.206; π. τὸν λόγον [prev. author] 8.59; γνώμας σφίσιν αὐτοῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.139}}; π. λόγον (i.e. εἰς ἐκκλησίαν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.65; λόγον περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.3}}; γνώμας π. αὖθις Ἀθηναίοις, of the Prytanes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.14, compare 3.36}}; π. τὴν διαγνώμην αὖθις περὶ Μυτιληναίων{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 42, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.15}}; π. βουλὴν εἴτε.. εἴτε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.15; τὸν ἐπιμήνιον, ἢν μὴ προθῇ, ἑκατὸν στατῆρας ὀφείλειν “SIG” 58.10 (Milet., 5th c.BC), compare 141.12 (Corc. Nigra, 4th c.BC), 167.13 (Mylasa, 4th c.BC), +others}}; προθεῖναι αὖθις περὶ Μυτιληναίων λέγειν **propose** a discussion about.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38; τὸ συμφέρον ἡ πόλις προὐτίθει σκοπεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.273, etc.}}; π. αὐτοῖς κρίσιν **appoint** a trial for them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.8; π. αὐτοῖς ἀγορὰν δικῶν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 4; προέθηκε.. λέγειν τὰ ἑκατέροισι.. κατέργασται}} **has proposed** (or **initiated**) a recital of what each has done, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.27}}: with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.38}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, π. γνώμην ἀποφαίνεσθαι τὸν βουλόμενον {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.49}}:—_middle_, ἤν τις προθῆται ψῆφον, ὥστε μὴ εἶναι τὸν νόμον τοῦτον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 45.33 (Halic., 5th c.BC):}} —_passive_, οὐ προὐτέθη σφίσι λόγος speech **was** not **allowed** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.5; ψῆφος περὶ ἡμῶν ὑπὲρ ἀνδραποδισμοῦ προτεθεῖσα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.65.}}
**II.5** **convene** a meeting, προὔθεσαν οἱ πρυτάνεις ἐκκλησίαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 19}}:—_middle_, σύγκλητον τήνδε γερόντων προὔθετο λέσχην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 161 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**III** **put forward**, as one foot before the other, βραδύπουν ἤλυσιν ἄρθρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 67 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**III.2** **hold out** as a pretext, αἰτίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1051}}:—_middle_, τὴν συγγένειαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.19.1, etc.}}
**IV** **put before** or **first**, προτιθέντι ἀνάγκη.. λέγειν, opposed to ἐπιλέγοντι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1394a15, compare b28; π. τοῦ λόγου προοίμιον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 723c}}; **set down first** in writing, προθεὶς ἄρχοντα Νικόμαχόν φησιν οὕτως· ἐπὶ τούτου κτλ.” {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Didymus Grammaticus}} **in D.**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|1.19, compare 8.17, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.183}}:— _middle_, **put in front**, τοὺς γροσφομάχους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.33.9}}; **premise**, λόγον {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.118.11; τὰς προειρημένας αἰτίας [prev. author] 4.25.6}}:—_passive_, τὸ μὴ καὶ τὸ οὒ προτιθέμενα τῶν ἐπιόντων ὀνομάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 257c: _metaphorically_, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42.}}
**IV.2** **put before** or **over**, πρόσθεν ὀμμάτων πέπλον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1550}}:— _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1218, etc.}}
**IV.3** **prefer** one to another, τί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 963; ἡδονὴν ἀντὶ τοῦ καλοῦ [prev. author] “Hipp.” 382}}:—_middle_, πάρος τοὐμοῦ πόθου προὔθεντο τὴν τυραννίδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 419.}}

4389:
** προτρέπω**,
**urge forwards**; used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only _intransitive_ in _middle_ or _passive_, **turn to flight**, προτρέποντο μελαινάων ἐπὶ νηῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.700}}; of the sun, ὅτ᾽ ἂν ἂψ ἐπὶ γαῖαν ἀπ᾽ οὐρανόθεν προτράπηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.18, compare 12.381: _metaphorically_, ἄχεϊ προτραπέσθαι}} **give myself up** to grief, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.336.}}
**II** later, in _active_, **urge on, impel**, τίς σ᾽ ἀνάγκῃ τῇδε προτρέπε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1193; π. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.123}}; opposed to κωλύω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1113b26}}: with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **urge on, impel, persuade** one to do a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.90, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 270; π. τὰ δέοντα ποιεῖν ὑμᾶς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.3, compare “BGU” 164.17 (2nd-3rd c.AD), etc.}}; π. τοὺς δικαστὰς ὀργίζεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.3; ὁ καλῶς προτρέπων ἐρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 181a}}; προτρέψαι (or -τρέψασθαι) τινὰ ὥστε πειρᾶσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.63: followed by a _preposition_, π. τινὰ εἰς or ἐπὶ φιλοσοφίαν, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275a, 307a; ἐπ᾽ ἐλευθερίαν τὰ πλήθη [prev. author] “Lg.” 699e; ἐπ᾽ ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.8, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 10, etc.; ἐπὶ τὰς ἀδικίας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.46; πρὸς τὸ παρέπεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89a, etc.; συμβουλεύει ἢ προτρέπων ἢ ἀποτρέπων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1358b15}}:— _middle_, with _accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 990, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 358, etc.}}; προτρέπεσθαι ἀνθρώπους ἐπ᾽ ἀρετήν, τοὺς συνόντας πρὸς ἐγκράτειαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.1, 4.5.1; νόμοι πολίτας ἐς δικαιοσύνην π. [prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.2.14; προὐτράπετο εἰς τὸ διαλέγεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 348c; πρὸς ἀρετῆς ἐπιτηδεύματα π. τοὺς πολίτας [prev. author] “Lg.” 711b}}; ὡς.. προετρέψατο ὁ Σόλων τὸν Κροῖσον εἴπας.., ἐπειρώτα.., as Solon's story **led** Croesus **on**, he asked, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31}}; προτρέψομαι **I will lay an injunction on thee**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1446 (variant)}}; **prescribe**, ἐν τῇ φαρμακείῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος ἀνθρώπου” 9, but compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.122, 19.133}}:—_passive_, **to be persuaded** or **influenced**, ὑπὸ τῶν ὀρνίθων ἀποτρέπεσθαι καὶ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.4, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 29}}: with _infinitive_, ὑπ᾽ ἐκείνων -τραπήσεσθαι ὅ τι ἂν κελεύωνται ποιεῖν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.87K.; ὑπὸ τῆς ἐλπίδος π. “SIG” 1073.37 (Olympia, 2nd c.AD)}}; προετράπη γράψαι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus “Vit.Arat.” p.77 Maass.}}
**III** **promote**, οὖρα, χυμόν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.32; γάλα, σπέρμα, [prev. work] 771:}} —_passive_, ἢν προτρέπηται ὁποῖα δεῖ if **stimulated** in the right way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 51.}}
**IV** in _middle_, **outstrip, outdo**, πάντας ἐν τῷ πίνειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.624c.}}
**V** **search out, discover**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 715 (_accusative_ to Scholia; uncertain reading).}}

4390:
** προτρέχω**,
_aorist_ προὔδρᾰμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.2}}:—**run forward** or **forth**, [prev. cited]; ἀπὸ τοῦ δένδρου δύο βήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.10; [ἕλμινθες] τῷ στομάχῳ προτρέχουσαι 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.37.}}
**II** **run in advance of, outrun**, στάδια πέντε τῶν ὁπλιτῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.4; πολλοῖς ἡ γλῶττα προτρέχει τῆς διανοίας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.41; π. τὰ κοπριζόμενα τῶν ἀκόπρων 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.7.7.}}
**III** ἡ προτρέχουσα εἰκασία the **{προτρέφω}** simile, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus “Allegoriae Quaestiones Homericae” 5.}}

4391:
** προϋπ-άρχω**,
**take the initiative in** a thing, with _genitive_, ἀδικίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40}}; τῶν εὐεργεσιῶν, τῆς ἔχθρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.36,125: with _dative_, π. τῷ ποιεῖν εὖ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.46}}: with _neuter_ _adjective_, π. τι ἔς τινα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.34:}} —_passive_, τὰ προϋπηργμένα εἰς αὑτόν **benefits formerly received**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.25, compare “OGI” 244.8 (Daphne, 2nd c.BC), 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 7p.429M.; but also προϋπηργμένα ἀδικήματα 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 4.5.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **exist before, be pre-existent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.85, 4.126, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317d, “PLille” 1v4 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; οἱ νόμοι οἱ προϋπάρχοντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292b20, compare “SIG” 526.31 (Itanos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἡ τῶν σωμάτων αὔξησις ἐκ προϋπαρχόντων ἐστίν from **pre-existent materials**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1419b22; πᾶσα μάθησις ἐκ προϋπαρχούσης γίνεται γνώσεως [prev. author] “APo.” 0.71a1}}; προϋπάρξαντα **things that happened before, past events**, variant for{πρὶν ὑπ}. in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.11}}; τὰ προϋπάρχοντα **former possessions**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 31, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 38.38}}; οἱ π. ὕπατοι the **previous** consuls, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.106.2:}}_perfect_ _passive_, τὰ προϋπηργμένα **antecedents**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.262, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1367b13}}; οἰκειότης, χάρις προϋπηργμένη, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.29, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “Synt.” 132.21.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_, προϋπάρχειν δεῖ τὸ κινοῦν τοῦ κινουμένου must **exist before.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Motu Animalium” 700b1; π. ἑαυτοῦ 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.208}}; **to be logically prior**, μέθοδος πασῶν προϋπάρχουσα καὶ κυριωτέρα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Nicomachus Gerasenus Mathematicus “Arithmetica Introductio” 1.4.}}

4392:
** πρόφᾰσ-ις**, εως, ἡ,
(προφαίνω) **motive** or **cause alleged**, whether truly or falsely: then, **actual motive** or **cause**, whether alleged or not:
**I** **alleged motive, plea**, without implication of truth or falsity, ἐπὶ σμικρῇ π. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 323; νόστου π. γλυκεροῦ κώλυεν μεῖναι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.32; κατὰ θεωρίης πρόφασιν ἐκπλώσας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29; π. ἔχων, ὡς.. [prev. author] 6.133}}; καὶ ἐπὶ μεγάλῃ καὶ ἐπὶ βραχείᾳ π. whether the **plea put forward** be a trifle or a weighty matter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141}}; τῆς αἰτίας τὴν π. the **plea** in the case, the **basis** of the charge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.7}}; τοιαύτας ἔχοντες π. καὶ αἰτίας **pleas** and motives, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.13}}; π. ἐπιεικής{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 9; ἀναγκαῖαι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.20, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.17}}; προφάσεις ἀληθεῖς λέγοντος **pleading** what was in fact true, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.17.}}
**I.2** **falsely alleged motive** (or **cause), pretext, pretence, excuse**, π. ἰδίης ἀβουλίης an **excuse** for.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 119; οὔτε τιν᾽ ἔχων π. οὔτε λόγον εὐτράπελον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 468 (Lyric poetry); καλλίστην εἶναι π., τιμωρεῖσθαι μὲν δοκεῖν, ἔργῳ δὲ χρηματίζεσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.6: _absolutely_ in _accusative_, πρόφασιν}} **in pretence, ostensibly**, στενάχοντο γυναῖκες Πάτροκλον π., σφῶν δ᾽ αὐτῶν κήδε᾽ ἑκάστη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.302, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 362 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 466, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ ἀληθές, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33: in _dative_, προφάσει [prev. author] 3.86}}; προφάσει τῶν δημοσίων on the **pretence** that public debts are owing, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 669.15 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; προφάσιος [εἵνεκεν], προφάσεως ἕνεκα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.135, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.14; προφάσεως χάριν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1297a14}}; ἐκ μικρᾶς π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.17.3; ἐπὶ προφάσιος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.150: followed by an}}_infinitive_, αὕτη γὰρ ἦν σοι π. ἐκβαλεῖν ἐμέ for casting me out, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1034; οὔτε.. ἔστιν οὐδεμία π. τοῦ μὴ δρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 20c}}; π. τοῖς δειλοῖς ἔχει μὴ ἰέναι gives them an **excuse** or **plea** for not going, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 469c; οὐδεμία σοι π. ἐστιν ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.15}}; εὑρὼν π. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“BGU” 1024 vi 21 (4th c.AD).}}
**I.2.b** phrases, πρόφασιν διδόναι, ἐνδοῦναι, allow, afford an **excuse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.53, 18.158; οὐκ ἐνδώσομεν π. οὐδενὶ κακῷ γενέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87}}; π. μηδεμίαν θέμενος making no **excuse**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 364}}; π. προτεῖναι put forward a **pretext**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.156; π. τὴν Παυσανίεω ὕβριν προϊσχόμενοι [prev. author] 8.3; προφάσεις παρέχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 581, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.35, 18.156}}; προφάσιας εἷλκον kept making **pretences**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86; πάσας π. ἕλκουσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 726; π. δέχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 421d (compare ἀγών 111.5); π. εὑρίσκειν τοῦ ἀδικήματος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.65; π. καλῶς εὑρημένη 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 36; ἔχθρας π. ζητήσουσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 234a, compare “PCair.Zen.” 270.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; π. τινὰ πρεσβείας πορισάμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 350a; π. κατασκευάσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.17}}; ἔχει προφάσεις it is excusable,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.1.27; πρόφασιν ταύτην τῆς διαφορᾶς ποιούμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 349d; προφάσεις εὐλόγους εἰλήφεσαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.152; ἐχόμενος προφάσιος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.94; ἐπιλαβέσθαι [prev. author] 3.36, 6.49; τὰς π. ἀφελεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.27; προφάσεως δεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1373a3: personified, τὰν Ἐπιμαθέος ὀψινόου θυγατέρα Π. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.28.}}
**I.2.c** elliptically, μή μοι πρόφασιν no **excuse**, no **shuffling**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 345; μὴ προφάσεις ἐνταῦθά μοι 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 127.1.}}
**II** **the actual motive, purpose**, or **cause**, whether alleged or not, οὔτ᾽ εὐνῆς πρόφασιν κεχρημένος οὔτε τευ ἄλλου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.262; ἐπ᾽ αὐτομολίας προφάσει ἀπέρχονται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.13}}; τὸ ἐκ προφάσεως τῶν.. στρατιωτῶν δηληγατευθὲν μέτρον ἐλαίου for the **purpose** of.., {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLips.” 64.2, compare 8 (4th c.AD)}}; τὴν ἀληθεστάτην π., ἀφανεστάτην δὲ λόγῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.23, compare 6.6, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.156, “SIG” 888.138 (Scaptopara, 3rd c.AD, _plural_)}}: especially as a medical technical term, **external exciting cause**, ἐκ πάσης π. ἐκτιτρώσκουσι they miscarry on any **provocation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.12, compare “Epid.” 3.3, 3.17.ιά, “Acut.(Sp.”) 6; τοὺς δ᾽ ἄλλους ἀπ᾽ οὐδεμιᾶς π... τῆς κεφαλῆς θέρμαι.. ἐλάμβανε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 16, “Fract.” 15, +others}}: generally, **cause**, σμικρὰ π. ἔξωθεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556e}}; βραχεῖα π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 477; ἀπὸ μηδεμιᾶς π. ἔξωθεν ἀξιολόγου Diocl.fragment 82}}; φανερὴ π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.41, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.4.33; ἐπεὶ δέ οἱ ἔδεε κακῶς γενέσθαι, ἐγένετο ἀπὸ προφάσιος τὴν ἐγὼ.. ἀπηγήσομαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.161, compare 4.145, 7.230; ἄνθρωπός εἰμι, τοῦτο δ᾽ αὐτὸ τῷ βίῳ π. μεγίστην εἰς τὸ λυπεῖσθαι φέρει 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 106, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 230, 811, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 194}}; βραχείας προφάσεως ἔδει μόνον ἐφ᾽ ᾗ.. δεξόμεθα.. it needed but a little **to move** us to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1180.}}
**II.2** **occasion**, θοἰμάτιον δεικνὺς τοδὶ πρόφασιν ἔφασκον, ὦ γύναι, λίαν σπαθᾷς I said **à propos**,.. I **took occasion to** say.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 55}}; ἐπὶ τῇ ἐμῇ π. **à propos of** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.19}}; ἐπὶ τῇ π. τῆς ἐμαυτοῦ ἀρχῆς on the **occasion** of my accession, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFay.” 20.11 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**III** **persuasion, suggestion**, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 662 (Lyric poetry).}}
**IV** **preface**, τὰς σημειώσεις ὑπὸ μίαν ἐκθησόμεθα π. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Ther.” 3.}}

4393:
** προφέρω**,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ προφέρεσκον, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 4.275, “IG” 14.1747.6 (from Rome):}}_future_ προοίσω: _aorist 1_ προήνεγκα: _aorist 2_ προήνεγκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.17}}: Hom.only _present_; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _present_ _subjunctive_ προφέρῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.323}}:—**bring before** or **to** one, **present**, ὡς ὄρνις.. νεοσσοῖσι προφέρῃσι μάστακα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place]; νέκυν Ἀχιλλῆϊ 17.121; μάντεις σφάγια προὔφερον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.69; προενέγκας τὴν ἐπιστολήν “BGU” 1141.11 (1st c.BC),compare “PTeb.” 291.43 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** of words, σφιν ὀνείδεα π. **cast** reproaches **in** their **teeth**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.251}}; π. τινί **throw in** one's **teeth, bring forward, allege**, especially in the way of reproach or objection, μή μοι δῶρ᾽ ἐρατὰ πρόφερε χρυσέης Ἀφροδίτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3.64, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.3, 8.61, 125, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.100; π. τοὔνομα τοῦθ᾽ ὡς ὄνειδος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.190; δικαιώσεις ἀλλήλοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.17}}: _absolutely_, **reproach** (followed by words quoted), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.120}}:—also in _middle_, τὴν ἐν Δωδώνῃ ἀσέβειαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.11.2; εἶναι βασιλικὴν γῆν “PTeb.” 81.17 (2nd c.BC),compare “PAmh.” 2.30.7 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**3** **utter**, μῦθον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 189 (_anapaest meter_)}}:—_middle_, ζῷα ἀνθρωπίνας π. φωνάς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.73, compare 15, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.218c.}}
**3.b** π. Αἴγιναν πάτραν **proclaim** it as their country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).43}}; π. εἰς μέσον or εἰς τὸ μ. **publish**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 812c, 936a:}} —_middle_, ὁπόσσω κα προφέρηται for whatever sum [the priest] **lays down,** {{Refs|Berl.Sitzb. 1927.169 (from Cyrene).}}
**4** **bring forward, cite**, μὴ π. τὴν τότε γενομένην ξυνωμοσίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.64, compare 5.26 (_passive_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 259d}}; προφέρων Ἄρτεμιν **putting forward** her **authority**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 201 (Lyric poetry)}}; π. τὰς ἐπονειδίστους τῶν ἡδονῶν **citing by way of example**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1173b21, compare “Pol.” 1288a20}}:—also in _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 57a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 14.6}}; ἀναμνήσεως χάριν π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.66.10}}; αὐτοῦ -ομένου τὴν περὶ τὸ σῶμα γεγενημένην ἀσθένειαν **pleading..**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 244.10 (Daphne, 2nd c.BC)}}; **cite**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; **recite**, ποιήματα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.109, compare 16.92; ἀριθμοὺς τῶν ἀρχαίων ποιητᾶν “SIG” 703.7, compare 660.3 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}
**5** of an oracle, **propose as a task**, τοῖσι Θηραίοισι προέφερε ἡ Πυθίη τὴν ἐς Λιβύην ἀποικίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.151; ἡ Πυθίη προφέρει σφι, τὰς Ἀθήνας ἐλευθεροῦν [prev. author] 5.63:}} —_passive_, δόμοισι προὐνεχθέντος ἐν χρηστηρίοις (_genitive_ _absolutely_) **it having been commanded** to do so, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 964.}}
**II** **bring forward, display**, π. κρατερὸν μένος {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.479; σπουδήν LXX “Si.Prol.” 22}}; ἔριδα π. **show**, i.e. engage in, rivalry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.92; ἀντιώσεσθαι πόλεμον προφέρων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.γ:}} —_middle_, ξεινοδόκῳ ἔριδα προφέρεσθαι **challenge** one's host **to** rivalry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.210, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.7.}}
**II.2** **bring out**, ἐντεῦθεν ὥσπερ ἐκ ταμιείου π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.44}}; ἤνοιξα τὸν τόπον τῶν οἰναρίων καὶ προενήνεχα (sic) οἴνου κεράμια νά {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1288.12 (4th c.AD)}}; ἠξίωσαν προενεχθῆναι αὐτὸν ἀπὸ τοῦ δεσμωτηρίου that he **should be produced..**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“BGU” 1024 vii 4 (4th c.AD).}}
**III** **carry off, sweep away**, of a storm, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.346, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.64}}; of death, π. σώματα τέκνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1111 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**IV** **put** or **move forward**, πόδα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1332 (Lyric poetry)}}; **carry forward, pass on**, σκυταλίδα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 22.27}}: hence, **promote, further**, ἠώς τοι προφέρει μὲν ὁδοῦ, π. δὲ καὶ ἔργου **furthers** one on the road and in the work, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 579: without _genitive_, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.344 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor)}}; μέγα π. εἴς τι **conduce, help** to wards gaining an object, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93}}; μεγάλη τύχη πρὸς πάντα π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 78.38}}:—_passive_, **move forward**, προενεχθέντος τοῦ σώματος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 711a29.}}
**IV.2** _intransitive_, **surpass, excel** another, δόξας ἔργα πολὺ προφέρει {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 161, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 12.5:}} with _dative_ _of things_, εἴρια καλλονῇ τε προφέροντα καὶ ἀρετῇ τῶν ἀπὸ τῶν ὀΐων (tree) wool **surpassing** sheep's wool in beauty and goodness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.106; πλούτῳ καὶ εἴδεϊ προφέρων Ἀθηναίων [prev. author] 6.127}}; ἡ Νάξος εὐδαιμονίῃ τῶν νήσων π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.28; π. εἰς εὐτυχίαν τινῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1092 (_anapaest meter_): _absolutely_, ἐν πάντα νόμον εὐθύγλωσσος ἀνὴρ π. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.86}}; πλούτῳ καὶ ἐξουσία, εὐψυχία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.123, 2.89, compare 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 4.275; ἔν τινι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 77.11.}}
**V** **bring forth** children, {{Refs|“IG” 14.1747.6 (from Rome).}}
**VI** **carry before**, λύχνον τινί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.31}}:—_passive_, τὸ ῥόπαλον προεφέρετο αὐτοῦ {{Refs|[prev. author] 72.17.}}

4394:
** προφητ-εία**, ἡ,
**gift of interpreting the will of the gods, gift of prophecy**, διδασκαλίαν ὡς π. ἐκχεῶ {{LXX|LXX.Sir.24.33}}; ἡ δὲ προφητείη δίης φρενός ἐστιν ἀπορρώξ Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 40.}}
**2** concrete, **prophecy** or **oracular response**, {{LXX+3rd c.AD+|LXX.2Ch.15.8, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.27.}}
**II** **office of** προφήτης {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2, εἴ τοι μέμηλεν ἔμπεδος π. “BMus.Inscription” 921b7 (from Branchidae), compare “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2869 (from Didyma), 2880 (from Branchidae), “OGI” 494.8 (Milet.); in Egypt, προφητειῶν καὶ γραμματειῶν καρπεῖαι “PTeb.” 6.34 (2nd c.BC).}}
**III** in **NT, gift of expounding scripture**, or **of speaking and preaching, under the influence of the Holy Spirit** (compare προφήτης), {{NT|NT.Rom.12.6, NT.1Cor.12.10, NT.1Tim.1.18, 4.14, +others}}

4395:
** προφητ-εύω**,
_Doric dialect_ **προφᾱτ-** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 150 and Inscrr.(see. below):}} —in _imperfect_ and _aorist 1_ the augment is sometimes placed after the _preposition_, προ-εφήτευον, -εφήτευσα{{LXX+NT|as LXX.1Ki.22.12 (variant), NT.Act.19.6 (variant), LXX.Sir.46.20 (but ἐπροφήτευσανLXX.Num.11.25, +others)}}:— **to be a** προφήτης or **interpreter** of the gods, μαντεύεο, Μοῖσα, προφατεύσω δ᾽ ἐγώ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same place]}}; τίς προφητεύει θεο; who **is** his **interpreter?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 413; οἱ προφητεύοντες τοῦ ἱροῦ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.111; ἡψυχὴ τὰ θεῖα καταλαβομένη τοῖς τε ἀνθρώποις προφητεύουσα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 391a16; οὗ [μαντείου] προειστήκει προφητεύων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 2.33, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.412b}}; οὐκ ἔστιν ὅστις σοι προφητεύσει τάδε **will be** thy **intermediary in asking** this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 369}}; ἡ μανία.. προφητεύσασα **with** **oracular power**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 244d:}} —_passive_, τὰ προφητευθέντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.9.}}
**II** **expound, interpret, preach, under the influence of the Holy Spirit,** {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Luke.1.67, NT.John.11.51, NT.Act.2.17, 19.6, NT.1Cor.11.4, 13.9, +others: also δημιουργῶν χεῖρες π. τὰ ποιήματα 4th c.AD: Callistratus Sophista “Statuarum descriptiones” 2.}}
**III** **hold office of** προφήτης, Θεοδώρου προφᾱτεύοντος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 7.4155 (Ptoön), compare 12(1).833.6 (from Lindus), “PGnom.” 211 (2nd c.AD).}}
**IV** **to be a quack doctor**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.172.}}

4396:
** προφήτ-ης**, ου,
_Doric dialect_ and _Boeotian dialect_ **προφάτας** [ᾱ], α, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. below), 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.68: ὁ·}} (πρό, φημί):— properly **one who speaks for a god and interprets his will** to man, Διὸς π. **interpreter, expounder of the will** of Zeus, of Tiresias, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.60}}; Βάκχου π., perhaps of Orpheus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 972}}; [Διονύσου] π., of the Bacchae, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 551 (Lyric poetry)}}; Νηρέως π., of Glaucus, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 364}}; especially of the Delphic Apollo, Διὸς π. ἐστὶ Λοξίας πατρός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 19}}; of the **minister** and **interpreter** at Delphi, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.36, 37}}; at the Ptoön,{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 135, “IG” 7.4135.13 (2nd c.BC)}}; compare προφῆτις.
**2** title of official **keepers of the oracle** at Branchidae, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2884, +others, “Supp.epigram” 1.426 (Milet., 1st c.AD)}}; elsewhere, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 14.961, 1032, 1084, 2433 (from Massilia), 9(2).1109.22 (Coropa, 2nd-1st c.BC), etc.}}
**2.b** in Egyptian temples, **member of the highest order of the clergy, priest**, π. θεῶν Εὐεργετῶν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 6.3 (2nd c.BC),compare “OGI” 56.59 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**3** **interpreter, expounder** of the utterances of the μάντις (which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 72a: hence, of Poets, Πιερίδων π. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Pae.” 6.6; Μουσᾶν π. 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 262d.}}
**4** **possessor of oracular powers**, of Amphiaraus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 611, compare “Ag.” 409 (Lyric poetry)}}; of Pseudo-Bacis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 972}}; of Epimenides, {{NT|NT.Tit.1.12.}}
**5** generally, **interpreter, declarer**, ἐγὼ π. σοι λόγων γενήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 211}}; π. ἀτόμων, of the Epicureans, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.187b}}; τῶν Πύρρωνος λόγων, of Timon, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.53}}; **spokesman**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.7.1.}}
**5.b** _metaphorically_, **proclaimer, harbinger**, κώμου προφάτας, of the wine-bowl, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.50; δείπνου π. λιμός 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 217.23; φθόης π. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 184.4; τέττιξ.. θέρεος γλυκὺς π. “Anacreontea” 32.11.}}
**II** **herald** at the games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9.28 (_plural_).}}
**III** in LXX, **revealer of God's will, prophet**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.9.9, +others:— hence}}
**III.2** in **NT, inspired preacher and teacher, organ of special revelations from God**, {{NT|NT.1Cor.12.28, 14.32}}; and (as comprised in this),
**III.2.b** **foreteller, prophet of future events,** {{NT|NT.Acts.2.30, 3.18, 21, NT.2Pet.3.2.}}
**III.3** **herbalist**, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.10, +others}}; **quack doctor**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.761.}}

4397:
** προφητ-ικός**, ή, όν,
**oracular, prophetic**, ἀνήρ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.515}}; λόγος{{NT+1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 95, NT.2Pet.1.19; νοῦς 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus “Fragmenta” 66H. (_superlative_),compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 60.}}
**2** εἰκὼν χαλκῆ π. **in the official robes of a** προφήτης, {{Refs|“Rev.Phil.” 44.38 (from Didyma)}}; so ἠμφιεσμένος π. σχήματι, στολίσας σεαυτὸν π. σχήματι, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.933, “PMag.Berol.” 1.278.}}

4398:
** προφῆτις**, ιδος,
_feminine_ of προφήτης, especially of the Pythia, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 42, 321, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 244a, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.26 M.: generally, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3796 (from Chalcedon), LXX.Exo.15.20, LXX.Judg.4.4, “Schwyzer” 633.20 (Eresus, 2nd-1st c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_, π. τῆς ἀληθείας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.2.}}
**2** **prophet's wife**, {{LXX|LXX.Isa.8.3.}}

4399:
** προφθάνω** [ᾰ],
**outrun, anticipate**, with _accusative_, προφθάσασα καρδία γλῶσσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1028 (Lyric poetry); ἐγὼ.. σε προφθάσας λέγω.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 500a, compare “PCair.Zen.” 520.2 (3rd c.BC)}}: with _participle_, προὔφθης με παρακύψασα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 884, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.73, LXX.1Ki.20.25, +others: with _genitive_, προέφθασα τοῦ φυγεῖνLXX.Jonah.4.2, cf. LXX.1Mac.10.4.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **to be beforehand**, στόματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1385, Theo Sm. p.160H.}}: _aorist_ _middle_, προφθάμενος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.913.}}
